 bg-primary px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-primary-foreground\",children:\"Return to Home\"})]})})},$1=[{to:\"/what-is-commander\",label:\"What is Commander?\"},{to:\"/commanders\",label:\"Commanders\"},{to:\"/archetypes\",label:\"Archetypes\"},{to:\"/strategies\",label:\"Strategies\"},{to:\"/about\",label:\"About\"}];function Rs({children:e,className:t=\"\"}){const r=Pt();return l.jsxs(\"div\",{className:`discovery-forge forge-content min-h-screen text-foreground ${t}`,children:[l.jsx(\"header\",{className:\"border-b border-border bg-card/80 backdrop-blur-sm sticky top-0 z-20\",children:l.jsxs(\"div\",{className:\"mx-auto flex max-w-4xl items-center justify-between px-6 py-4\",children:[l.jsxs(ie,{to:\"/\",className:\"flex items-center gap-2 font-display text-lg font-semibold text-primary\",children:[l.jsx(xt,{className:\"h-5 w-5\"}),\"Commander Forge\"]}),l.jsx(\"nav\",{className:\"hidden sm:flex items-center gap-6\",children:$1.map(n=>l.jsx(ie,{to:n.to,className:\"text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\",children:n.label},n.to))})]})}),l.jsxs(\"main\",{className:\"mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-12\",children:[l.jsxs(\"button\",{onClick:()=>r(-1),className:\"mb-8 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-primary transition-colors\",children:[l.jsx(Os,{className:\"h-3.5 w-3.5\"}),\"Back\"]}),e]}),l.jsx(\"footer\",{className:\"border-t border-border bg-card/50\",children:l.jsxs(\"div\",{className:\"mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-10\",children:[l.jsxs(\"div\",{className:\"flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center justify-between gap-6\",children:[l.jsxs(\"div\",{children:[l.jsx(ie,{to:\"/\",className:\"font-display text-sm font-semibold text-primary\",children:\"Commander Forge\"}),l.jsx(\"p\",{className:\"mt-1 text-xs text-muted-foreground\",children:\"AI-powered MTG Commander deck building\"})]}),l.jsx(\"nav\",{className:\"flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2\",children:$1.map(n=>l.jsx(ie,{to:n.to,className:\"text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\",children:n.label},n.to))})]}),l.jsx(\"div\",{className:\"discovery-rule mt-6\",\"aria-hidden\":\"true\",children:l.jsx(\"span\",{})}),l.jsx(\"p\",{className:\"mt-4 text-xs text-muted-foreground/60\",children:\"Powered by Scryfall · Not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast\"})]})})]})}function X7(){return Gs(\"Commander Rules Explained | What is EDH in MTG? — Commander Forge\",\"Commander (EDH) rules explained: 100-card singleton decks, 40 life, color identity, commander damage, the command zone, mulligans, and banned cards.\"),Ko([{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Article\",headline:\"Commander Rules Explained: What is EDH in Magic: The Gathering?\",description:\"A complete guide to the Commander (EDH) format: deck construction rules, color identity, commander damage, the command zone, and how to get started.\",author:{\"@type\":\"Organization\",name:\"Commander Forge\"},publisher:{\"@type\":\"Organization\",name:\"Commander Forge\"},mainEntityOfPage:\"https://commander-forge.com/what-is-commander\"},{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",mainEntity:[{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"What does EDH mean in MTG?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:'EDH stands for Elder Dragon Highlander, the original community name for the format now officially called Commander. \"Elder Dragon\" refers to the five original Legends elder dragons that early players used as commanders, and \"Highlander\" means singleton — only one copy of each card. EDH and Commander mean the same thing today.'}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"What are the rules of Commander?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:\"A Commander deck is exactly 100 cards including one legendary creature as your commander. Every card must fall within the commander's color identity, and no card may appear more than once except basic lands. Players start at 40 life, the commander begins in the command zone, and 21 combat damage from a single commander eliminates a player.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"What is color identity in Commander?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:\"Color identity is every mana symbol in your commander's mana cost plus every mana symbol in its rules text, including hybrid and phyrexian symbols. Reminder text does not count. A card can only go in your deck if its own color identity is a subset of your commander's.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"How does commander damage work?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:\"Commander damage is tracked per commander. If a single commander deals 21 or more combat damage to the same player over the course of the game, that player loses, regardless of their remaining life total. Non-combat damage from a commander does not count.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"What is the commander tax?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:\"When your commander dies, is exiled, or is shuffled away, you may return it to the command zone instead. Each time you cast it from the command zone after the first, it costs an extra {2} — this is the commander tax, and it is cumulative for the whole game.\"}},{\"@type\":\"Question\",name:\"How many players do you need for Commander?\",acceptedAnswer:{\"@type\":\"Answer\",text:\"Commander is designed for free-for-all multiplayer, most commonly four players. It also works well at three, and one-on-one Commander is popular though some cards are much stronger in that setting.\"}}]}]"
  cf,archetypes_narrative,js_data,7056,"{lands:{howItWins:\"Turns extra land drops into damage and card advantage — landfall payoffs like Avenger of Zendikar or Scute Swarm convert a ramp engine into a lethal board in a single turn.\",playIf:\"You enjoy grindy, resilient decks that rebuild after board wipes because the resource base itself is the engine.\",watchFor:\"Non-basic land hate and the fact that lands dodge most creature removal but fold to Armageddon effects you did not plan for.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},artifact:{howItWins:\"Accumulates cheap artifacts until a payoff scales out of control — cost reducers, Treasure loops, or an artifact-count finisher closing the game from nowhere.\",playIf:\"You like colourless flexibility and engines that work in any colour identity.\",watchFor:\"Mass artifact removal such as Vandalblast or Bane of Progress can undo half your board at once — hold protection or rebuild pieces.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},enchantress:{howItWins:\"Every enchantment replaces itself through Enchantress-style draw triggers, then a constellation or aura payoff converts the accumulated permanents into a win.\",playIf:\"You want to draw a lot of cards while building a board most decks struggle to interact with.\",watchFor:\"Enchantment removal is rarer than artifact removal, but a single Back to Nature can be catastrophic in the wrong pod.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},sacrifice:{howItWins:\"Loops expendable creatures through a sacrifice outlet while death triggers drain the table — Aristocrats decks often win without ever attacking.\",playIf:\"You like decks with an inevitable, incremental clock and lots of small decisions each turn.\",watchFor:\"You need three pieces at once — fodder, an outlet, and a payoff. Redundancy on the outlet is what makes the deck consistent.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},graveyard:{howItWins:\"Treats the graveyard as a second hand: mill or discard your best threats, then reanimate them far ahead of their mana cost.\",playIf:\"You want to cast enormous creatures on turn four and do not mind rebuilding when interaction hits.\",watchFor:\"Graveyard hate is common at Commander tables. Bojuka Bog or Rest in Peace can blank the plan, so keep a hand-based backup line.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},blink:{howItWins:\"Flickers value creatures repeatedly, re-triggering enter-the-battlefield abilities for tutors, removal and card draw until you simply out-resource the table.\",playIf:\"You enjoy toolbox decks where the right creature answers whatever problem appears.\",watchFor:\"Slow starts. Blink needs a critical mass of ETB creatures before the engine produces more than one card a turn.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},spellslinger:{howItWins:\"Chains instants and sorceries with magecraft or prowess payoffs attached, turning a handful of cheap spells into a lethal burst or an unstoppable draw chain.\",playIf:\"You like holding up interaction and playing at instant speed on other players' turns.\",watchFor:\"Creature-light boards are vulnerable. Make sure your payoff creatures are protected, or you spend the game rebuying them.\",difficulty:\"Demanding\"},creatures:{howItWins:\"Deploys efficient bodies, buffs them, and attacks. Extra combat steps and power-matters payoffs turn a normal board into lethal damage.\",playIf:\"You are new to the format or want the most straightforward, low-friction game plan.\",watchFor:\"Board wipes. Hold back a rebuild threat rather than committing everything to the table at once.\",difficulty:\"Beginner friendly\"},control:{howItWins:\"Answers every meaningful threat, accumulates card advantage, and eventually deploys an inevitability engine the table cannot remove.\",playIf:\"You enjoy reading the table, holding mana open, and deciding which threats are actually worth an answer.\",watchFor:\"Three opponents means you cannot counter everything. Prioritise ruthlessly and lean on sweepers over one-for-one answers.\",difficulty:\"Demanding\"},lifegain:{howItWins:\"Rarely wins by being at high life — it wins by converting life gained into triggers: tokens, card draw, or drain effects that shrink three opponents at once.\",playIf:\"You want a durable deck that survives aggression while building towards a drain finish.\",watchFor:\"Lifegain without payoffs does nothing. Every life-gain card should be attached to a reason for gaining it.\",difficulty:\"Beginner friendly\"},tokens:{howItWins:\"Goes wide with creature tokens, then converts board width into damage with anthems, an overrun effect, or a sacrifice payoff.\",playIf:\"You like explosive turns and boards that visibly get bigger every round.\",watchFor:\"Sweepers hit you hardest of all. Populate and recursion effects give you a second wind after a wipe.\",difficulty:\"Beginner friendly\"},burn:{howItWins:\"Points direct damage at faces rather than boards, using damage doublers and repeatable pingers to cover the 120 life a four-player table represents.\",playIf:\"You want a fast, aggressive deck with a very clear plan.\",watchFor:'Multiplayer maths. Single-target burn is inefficient here — prioritise \"each opponent\" effects and damage multipliers.',difficulty:\"Moderate\"},voltron:{howItWins:\"Loads equipment and auras onto one creature and kills through 21 commander damage — often in two swings, and often before anyone is set up.\",playIf:\"You like a focused, aggressive plan with clear threat assessment.\",watchFor:\"Card disadvantage from auras and being the archetype the whole table removes first. Protection and hexproof effects are mandatory.\",difficulty:\"Moderate\"},stax:{howItWins:\"Denies resources — taxing, tapping down and restricting permanents — until opponents cannot function, then wins slowly under the lock.\",playIf:\"You enjoy resource-denial puzzles and asymmetric effects that hurt you least.\",watchFor:\"Table politics. Stax draws attention immediately, so make sure the lock closes rather than just annoying everyone.\",difficulty:\"Demanding\"},counters:{howItWins:\"Grows creatures with +1/+1 counters and multiplies them with proliferate, doublers and counter payoffs until a single creature ends the game.\",playIf:\"You like incremental growth and decks that reward playing to the board.\",watchFor:\"Counters vanish with the creature. Spread counters or run recursion so one removal spell does not undo four turns of work.\",difficulty:\"Beginner friendly\"},mill:{howItWins:\"Empties opponents' libraries so they lose on their draw step — with three opponents, that means heavy mass-mill and repeatable effects, not one-shot spells.\",playIf:\"You want an alternative win condition that ignores life totals entirely.\",watchFor:\"Mill feeds graveyard decks. Pair it with exile effects, or pick your targets before you accelerate someone else's reanimator plan.\",difficulty:\"Demanding\"},chaos:{howItWins:\"Warps the rules of the game with randomness, cascade chains and theft effects, taking advantage of the disruption more effectively than the table can.\",playIf:\"You value memorable games over consistency and enjoy unpredictable board states.\",watchFor:\"Symmetrical chaos helps everyone. Choose effects your deck is deliberately built to exploit.\",difficulty:\"Demanding\"}}"
  em,_index,page_text,19124,"Commander Strategies | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nCommander Strategies\nDiscover powerful Commander strategies and find the perfect playstyle for your next EDH deck. Each strategy guide includes recommended commanders, key cards, and deckbuilding tips.\nSacrifice & Value\nLifegain\nLifegain decks are for players who want each point of life to become a resource, not just a larger cushion against attacks. This strategy wants to gain life in steady repeatable triggers, using lifegain commanders, Soul Warden effects, and payoff cards, to win through life drain, oversized creatures, or a charged Aetherflux Reservoir. Unlike Group Hug, it keeps the resources for itself, and unlike Aristocrats it is driven by life gained rather than death triggers. The best lifegain decks EDH players build feel patient early, then convert a safe life total into pressure.\nView Strategy →\nAristocrats\nAristocrats decks are for players who enjoy turning expendable creatures into cards, Treasure, damage, and life drain whenever they die. This strategy wants to make cheap bodies, use sacrifice commanders and free outlets, and convert each death trigger into value to win through drain effects or recursive sacrifice loops. Unlike Tokens, it cares less about attacking with many bodies, and unlike broad Sacrifice decks it is specifically built around death trigger payoffs. The best aristocrats commanders let you grind from small pieces while the table decides whether removing your board helps you or hurts them.\nView Strategy →\nSacrifice\nSacrifice decks are for players who want their own permanents to be flexible resources rather than pieces to protect at all costs. This strategy wants to feed creatures, artifacts, and tokens into a sacrifice outlet, using fodder makers, recursion, and sacrifice commanders, to win through mana loops, edicts, drain payoffs, or a steady resource lead. Unlike Aristocrats, it is broader than death trigger drain, and unlike Tokens it values bodies mainly as expendable fuel. The best sacrifice commanders keep the engine moving even when opponents point removal at your board.\nView Strategy →\nCard Draw\nCard Draw decks are for players who want a full hand to be the engine, the protection, and eventually the win condition. This strategy wants to draw extra cards every turn, using card draw commanders, repeatable engines, wheels, and draw payoffs, to win through card advantage EDH plans such as damage triggers, stocked interaction, or superior late-game resources. Unlike Control, it is not only answering threats, and unlike Spellslinger it cares about the draw event more than the spell cast. The best card draw commanders either refill you directly or convert each extra card into pressure.\nView Strategy →\nBlink\nBlink decks are for players who want every creature to matter more than once. This strategy wants to exile and return creatures with enter-the-battlefield effects, using blink commanders, flicker spells, and repeatable engines, to win through compounding value, protected threats, or trigger loops. Unlike Control, Blink handles the game through reusable creature triggers instead of mostly counterspells, and unlike Tokens it may make bodies incidentally rather than going wide first. The best blink commanders make modest creatures convert into cards, removal, ramp, and rebuilt boards over several turns.\nView Strategy →\nGraveyard\nReanimator\nReanimator decks are for players who want to turn the graveyard into a shortcut for creatures that should cost far more mana. This strategy wants to load its own graveyard with high-impact targets, using discard, self-mill, and Entomb-style tutors, to win by casting cheap reanimation spells that cheat creatures into play ahead of schedule. Unlike Graveyard value decks, Reanimator is less about recurring small resources every turn, and unlike Mill it is not trying to empty libraries as the primary weapon. The best reanimator commanders and graveyard commanders help discard threats, return them, or convert each reanimated creature into immediate pressure.\nView Strategy →\nGraveyard\nGraveyard decks are for players who want every discarded, milled, or destroyed card to remain part of the game plan. This strategy wants to stock the graveyard, using self-mill, discard outlets, sacrifice, and dredge-style cards, to win by recurring threats, lands, and value pieces until opponents run out of clean answers. Unlike Reanimator, graveyard value decks EDH players build are not centered on one huge creature arriving early, and unlike Mill they use self-mill as fuel rather than treating libraries as the main target. The best graveyard commanders turn the graveyard into a steady resource engine that can rebuild after removal.\nView Strategy →\nMill\nMill decks are for players who want to pressure a different resource: the cards left in each library. This strategy wants to put cards from libraries into graveyards, using repeatable mill commanders, doublers, and self-mill engines, to win by emptying opponents' decks or by converting stocked graveyards into value. Unlike Reanimator, Mill is not mainly trying to cheat one creature into play, and unlike Graveyard value it treats milling as the weapon or fuel source rather than the recursion engine itself. The best mill decks EDH players build respect the multiplayer math by multiplying output, protecting engines, or turning every milled card into another resource.\nView Strategy →\nSpell-Based\nSpellslinger\nSpellslinger decks are for players who want each instant and sorcery to advance the board, refill the hand, or pressure life totals. This strategy wants to cast cheap spells across every turn cycle, using spellslinger commanders, magecraft payoffs, and copy effects, to win through accumulated damage, spell-made tokens, or card advantage. Unlike Storm, it does not need one huge chain turn, and unlike Combo it is not waiting for specific pieces to end the game at once. The best instants and sorceries decks reward steady sequencing and careful use of interaction.\nView Strategy →\nCombo\nCombo decks are for players who enjoy finding a narrow line, protecting it, and ending the game with specific cards working together. This strategy wants to assemble combo commanders, tutors, draw, and compact two-card or three-card packages, using infinite combos or deterministic loops, to win in one decisive moment. Unlike Spellslinger it is not paid for every spell cast, unlike Storm it does not need a long chain turn, and unlike Big Mana it does not rely on casting one enormous payoff. The best combo decks EDH players build balance speed with enough interaction to survive the table's disruption.\nView Strategy →\nStorm\nStorm decks are for players who want to sculpt a hand, pick one decisive turn, and chain cheap spells until the count becomes lethal. This strategy wants to cast many spells in one turn, using cantrips, rituals, cost reducers, and storm commanders, to win through Grapeshot, Tendrils of Agony, Aetherflux Reservoir, or another storm-count payoff. In the Spellslinger, Storm, Big Mana, and Combo cluster, Spellslinger grinds value from each instant or sorcery, Big Mana spends on fewer huge spells, Combo assembles fixed pieces, and Storm turns spell volume into the win. The best storm combo EDH decks reward careful sequencing because every card has to convert into mana, cards, count, or a protected finish.\nView Strategy →\nBig Mana\nBig Mana decks are for players who want the game to build toward casting one spell that changes every resource count at once. This strategy wants to accelerate beyond normal mana development, using land ramp, mana rocks, doublers, and big mana commanders, to win through X spells, huge spells EDH tables have to answer, or haymakers like Torment of Hailfire and Expropriate. In the Ramp, Landfall, and Big Mana overlap, Ramp is acceleration, Landfall turns land drops into triggers, and Big Mana is the payoff plan for spending all that mana. Unlike Storm, it usually casts fewer spells in a turn and asks each one to matter on resolution.\nView Strategy →\nCreatures & Combat\nTokens\nToken decks are for players who want one card to become many bodies and then decide whether those bodies attack, block, draw cards, or fuel another engine. This strategy wants to flood the battlefield with creatures, using token commanders, repeatable generators, and anthem effects, to win through a go wide attack or by converting the swarm into damage, drain, or mana. Tokens is different from Aristocrats because making bodies comes first, from Counters because it grows wide instead of tall, and from Voltron because it spreads pressure across many creatures. The best token decks EDH players build reward patience against sweepers and careful timing on the finishing turn.\nView Strategy →\nVoltron\nVoltron decks are for players who want the game to revolve around one protected attacker and the timing of each combat step. This strategy wants to build one creature, usually a commander, using equipment and auras for power, evasion, and protection, to win through 21 commander damage on each opponent. Unlike Tokens or Aggro, Voltron does not spread pressure across many bodies, and unlike broad Equipment decks the gear is focused on one threat rather than a wider artifact engine. The best Voltron commanders make commander damage decks feel like a resource puzzle: suit up, connect, protect the threat, then rebuild if the table answers it.\nView Strategy →\n+1/+1 Counters\n+1/+1 counter decks are for players who want small creature upgrades to become permanent pressure across the board. This strategy wants to grow creatures over several turns, using +1/+1 counter commanders, counter doublers, and counter payoffs, to win through combat damage from creatures that keep getting larger. Unlike Tokens, Counters grows creatures over time instead of making many bodies, and unlike Voltron it spreads power beyond one attacker; unlike Proliferate, it focuses on +1/+1 growth rather than every counter type, while Infect is a separate poison plan. Counters matter decks reward pilots who sequence early creatures, multipliers, and protection so each trigger converts into lasting board presence.\nView Strategy →\nProliferate\nProliferate decks are for players who want one trigger to move several counter-based plans forward at the same time. This strategy wants to place useful counters first, using +1/+1 counters, poison, loyalty, charge counters, and repeatable proliferate commanders, to win through a grown board, planeswalker ultimates, or the final poison counter. Unlike +1/+1 Counters, Proliferate multiplies any counter type rather than only creature size, and unlike Infect it can use poison as one route without making ten poison the whole plan. Counter synergy EDH decks built around proliferate reward pilots who seed the right counters, then chain small increases until every permanent matters.\nView Strategy →\nEquipment\nEquipment decks are for players who want the gear itself to be the engine, turning ordinary creatures into credible attackers. This strategy wants to assemble weapons, tutors, and equip-cost shortcuts, using equipment commanders that find or attach gear, to win through upgraded combat, commander damage, or a team carrying shared tools. Unlike Voltron, Equipment does not have to put every card on one creature, and unlike Artifacts it focuses on weapons rather than every artifact type. The best equipment decks EDH players build recover well after creature sweepers because the gear usually stays on the battlefield.\nView Strategy →\nInfect\nInfect decks are for players who want life totals to matter less than reaching ten poison on each opponent. This strategy wants to connect with infect and toxic creatures, using pump spells, evasion, protection, and infect commanders, to win through poison counter decks EDH opponents have to answer early. In the Counters, Proliferate, and Infect overlap, Counters grows creatures, Proliferate multiplies many counter types, and Infect is the poison win condition that proliferate can accelerate. The social cost is real: once poison appears, the table often treats you as the first threat, and some pods restrict the mechanic before deck selection.\nView Strategy →\nAggro\nAggro decks are for players who want to commit threats early, attack often, and make combat math matter every turn. This strategy wants to pressure all opponents with efficient attackers, using anthems, haste, attack triggers, extra combats, and aggro commanders, to win through repeated combat damage before slower engines stabilize. In Commander that still means pushing through 120 total damage, so go-wide aggro EDH decks need refueling and scaling damage rather than only fast starts. Aggro differs from Tokens because it focuses on attacking with the bodies, from Voltron because it spreads pressure across many creatures, and from Infect because it deals life-total damage instead of poison.\nView Strategy →\nControl & Resources\nRamp\nRamp decks are for players who want to spend the early game accelerating so later turns happen ahead of schedule. This strategy wants to build more mana than the table, using mana ramp EDH staples, extra land drops, dorks, and rocks, to win through earlier threats, large spells, or a resource lead opponents must answer. Ramp is different from Landfall because acceleration is the point, and different from Big Mana because it is the engine rather than the payoff. The best ramp commanders reward extra mana sources while still giving you something meaningful to cast.\nView Strategy →\nControl\nControl decks are for players who enjoy deciding which threats matter, spending answers carefully, and winning after the table has slowed down. This strategy wants to stabilize the game, using control commanders, counterspells, removal, sweepers, and card-draw engines, to win through a protected finisher, planeswalker, combo finish, or long-term card advantage. Unlike Stax, Control answers threats on the stack or battlefield instead of locking everyone out of resources. The best control decks EDH players build accept that multiplayer control cannot counter everything, so they lean on sweepers and engines that trade across several opponents.\nView Strategy →\nStax\nStax decks are for players who want to win by deciding which resources matter before opponents can use them. This strategy wants to tax mana, cards, combat, and casting windows, using stax commanders and resource denial EDH pieces like Winter Orb and Smokestack, to win through a board state where your deck still functions and opponents cannot rebuild. Unlike Control, which answers threats after they appear, Stax locks the costs and rules of the game so many threats never get cast. The best stax commanders break parity, then convert the slowed table into combat damage, attrition, or a compact combo.\nView Strategy →\nGroup Hug\nGroup Hug decks are for players who want table politics to matter as much as board position. This strategy wants to give everyone extra cards, lands, or mana, using group hug commanders and symmetrical resource effects, to win through political leverage, hidden payoffs, or a carefully timed finish. Unlike Lifegain, it does not make your own life total the engine; it gives resources away and tries to profit from who uses them. The best political decks EDH players remember are frank about the risk: your gifts can help the wrong player first.\nView Strategy →\nEnchantress\nEnchantress decks are for players who want each enchantment cast to replace itself and add another layer to the board. This strategy wants to chain cheap enchantments, using enchantress commanders, draw engines, and protective pieces, to win through constellation triggers, token makers, or an aura-based Voltron line. Unlike general Card Draw decks, Enchantress ties its cards to enchantment casts, and unlike Lifegain it treats life from Sythis-style triggers as support rather than the main engine. The best enchantment decks EDH players build feel incremental: ramp a little, draw a card, protect the engine, then convert the chain into threats.\nView Strategy →\nArtifacts\nArtifact decks are for players who want cheap permanents, mana rocks, and utility pieces to become one connected engine. This strategy wants to build artifact count and mana density, using artifact commanders, cost reducers, Treasure, recursion, and untap effects, to win through construct armies, loops, or artifact payoffs. Unlike Equipment, it cares about every artifact rather than just weapons, and unlike Ramp it turns the mana rocks themselves into the engine instead of only casting bigger spells. The best artifact decks EDH players build reward careful sequencing because each small piece can convert the next piece into more mana or cards.\nView Strategy →\nLandfall\nLandfall decks are for players who want every land drop to become a trigger, not just another mana source. This strategy wants to put lands onto the battlefield repeatedly, using fetch lands, extra land drops, land recursion, and landfall commanders, to win through tokens, counters, damage, cards, or other lands matter decks EDH payoffs. In the Ramp, Landfall, and Big Mana cluster, Ramp accelerates mana, Big Mana spends that mana on huge spells, and Landfall treats the land entering as the engine itself. The deck feels like turning the most reliable game action into a chain of visible rewards.\nView Strategy →\nFind Your Perfect Strategy\nNot sure which strategy fits your playstyle? Take our interactive quiz to get personalized commander recommendations based on your preferences.\nTake the Quiz\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,aggro,page_text,11144,"Best Aggro Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nAggro\nBack to SearchBest Aggro Commanders for EDH\nAggro decks are for players who want to commit threats early, attack often, and make combat math matter every turn. This strategy wants to pressure all opponents with efficient attackers, using anthems, haste, attack triggers, extra combats, and aggro commanders, to win through repeated combat damage before slower engines stabilize. In Commander that still means pushing through 120 total damage, so go-wide aggro EDH decks need refueling and scaling damage rather than only fast starts. Aggro differs from Tokens because it focuses on attacking with the bodies, from Voltron because it spreads pressure across many creatures, and from Infect because it deals life-total damage instead of poison.\nBest Colors for Aggro Decks\nBoros stacks anthems, haste, extra combats, protection, and attack-trigger payoffs like battle cry.\nGruul brings haste, larger bodies, trample, and ramp that deploys multiple attackers per turn.\nMardu adds removal, drain, token pressure, and doubled attack triggers for resilient combat turns.\nMono-red offers cheap creatures, haste, impulse draw, extra combats, and burn for weakened opponents.\nHow Do Aggro Decks Win in Commander?\nAggro decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy cheap efficient creatures early and attack before engines stabilize.\n✓ You spread pressure across opponents while tracking who can absorb the least damage.\n✓ You use anthems haste and attack triggers to make small bodies scale.\n✓ You chain extra combat steps when one board can convert into lethal damage.\n✓ You refuel with combat draw or commander triggers because 120 damage requires more than a fast start.\nBest Cards for Aggro EDH Decks\nShared Animosity\nConverts a wide board of shared creature types into scaling combat damage.\nAggravated Assault\nAdds repeatable combat steps so one board can attack multiple times.\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One\nDoubles attack triggers, improving raid, battle cry, and myriad payoffs.\nReconnaissance Mission\nRefills your hand when multiple attackers connect with opponents.\nFervor\nGives your creatures haste so new attackers pressure immediately.\nHow to Build a Aggro Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 25-35 creatures, weighted toward one, two, and three mana, so attacks start early\n✓Use 2-4 anthem effects like Shared Animosity or Cathars' Crusade to make small bodies scale\n✓Add haste enablers such as Fervor or Rising of the Day so new creatures attack immediately\n✓Include 2-3 extra combat cards like Aggravated Assault or Moraug, Fury of Akoum\n✓Use combat-triggered draw like Reconnaissance Mission or Toski, Bearer of Secrets to refuel\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep the mana curve low and land count around 33-35 because your hand should empty quickly\n✓Choose a commander that refuels or multiplies attacks, like Isshin or Winota, Joiner of Forces\n✓Include a small removal package to clear blockers rather than answer everything\n✓Favor creatures with evasion, menace, or trample so stalled boards do not stop attacks\nWin Conditions\n✓Use wide attacks boosted by anthems to pressure multiple opponents over consecutive turns\n✓Chain extra combats to convert one strong board into 40-plus damage in a turn\n✓Use burn and drain effects like Impact Tremors to finish opponents who stabilize at low life\nWeaknesses of Aggro Decks\n⚠Board wipes can erase several turns of development with one card, so overextending is a constant risk.\n⚠Dealing 120 total damage across three opponents means the deck can run out of gas without dedicated refueling.\n⚠Pillow-fort and lifegain decks can outlast early pressure and take over once the game goes long.\n⚠Late-game topdecks can be weak because cheap attackers lose impact as the table develops.\nIs Aggro Right for You?\nAggro is beginner-friendly because the core plan is clear: play creatures, attack, and keep pressure on the right players. It can be built on a tight budget with cheap creatures, anthems, and combat draw. It suits players who enjoy proactive turns, table reading, and honest combat math.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nTokens\nTokens makes bodies; Aggro turns bodies into repeated combat damage.\nEquipment\nEquipment concentrates power on geared threats; Aggro spreads pressure across attackers.\nInfect\nInfect races to ten poison; Aggro must deal life-total damage.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Aggro CommandersExtra CombatsBurn\nTop Aggro Commanders\nKrenko, Mob Boss\nMV: 4\nGoblinsTokensAggro+7 more\n$2.80TCGPlayer\n€1.72CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One\nMV: 3\nAggroTokensAttack Triggers+7 more\n$3.76TCGPlayer\n€2.99CardMarket\nKaalia of the Vast\nMV: 4\nFlyingAngelsAggro+7 more\n$3.79TCGPlayer\n€2.16CardMarket\nZurgo Stormrender\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsAggro+7 more\n$4.58TCGPlayer\n€3.43CardMarket\nBello, Bard of the Brambles\nMV: 3\nEnchantressArtifactsUnnatural+7 more\n$2.87TCGPlayer\n€3.35CardMarket\nWinota, Joiner of Forces\nMV: 4\nHatebearsAggroStax+7 more\n$4.34TCGPlayer\n€4.46CardMarket\nCaesar, Legion's Emperor\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nTokensAristocratsHumans+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nArabella, Abandoned Doll\nMV: 2\nTokensAggroBurn+7 more\n$0.85TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nTovolar, Dire Overlord // Tovolar, the Midnight Scourge\nMV: 3\nWerewolvesAggroWolves+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.46CardMarket\nVoja, Jaws of the Conclave\nMV: 5\nElves+1/+1 CountersAggro+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nZada, Hedron Grinder\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerGoblinsTokens+7 more\n$0.17TCGPlayer\n€0.12CardMarket\nPantlaza, Sun-Favored\nMV: 5\nDinosaursDiscoverRamp+7 more\n$5.75TCGPlayer\n€5.93CardMarket\nYuriko, the Tiger's Shadow\nMV: 3\nNinjutsuNinjasTempo+7 more\n$2.95TCGPlayer\n€2.80CardMarket\nÉowyn, Shieldmaiden\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nHumansTokensAggro+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.41CardMarket\nHenzie \"Toolbox\" Torre\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAggroAristocrats+7 more\n$1.00TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nThe Ur-Dragon\nMV: 9\nDragonsFlyingShapeshifters+7 more\n$37.54TCGPlayer\n€30.89CardMarket\nFire Lord Azula\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerComboSpell Copy+7 more\n$1.16TCGPlayer\n€1.05CardMarket\nLathril, Blade of the Elves\nMV: 4\nElvesTokensAggro+7 more\n$1.78TCGPlayer\n€1.45CardMarket\nLightning, Army of One\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronExtra Combats+7 more\n$9.59TCGPlayer\n€8.55CardMarket\nCloud, Ex-SOLDIER\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$1.44TCGPlayer\n€0.73CardMarket\nNajeela, the Blade-Blossom\nMV: 3\nWarriorsExtra CombatsAggro+7 more\n$7.23TCGPlayer\n€5.63CardMarket\nFynn, the Fangbearer\nMV: 2\nDeathtouchInfectAggro+7 more\n$0.20TCGPlayer\n€0.24CardMarket\nXenagos, God of Revels\nMV: 5\nExtra CombatsAggroRamp+7 more\n$11.21TCGPlayer\n€7.55CardMarket\nTannuk, Steadfast Second\nMV: 4\nAggroArtifactsWarp+7 more\n$1.81TCGPlayer\n€1.82CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Aggro commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Aggro commander?\nThe best aggro commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy cheap efficient creatures early and attack before engines stabilize.\nHow do Aggro decks win?\nAggro decks win through a combination of Shared Animosity, Aggravated Assault, Isshin, Two Heavens as One, and other synergistic pieces that aggro decks are for players who want to commit threats early, attack often, and make combat math matter every turn.\nWhat are the best colors for Aggro?\nBoros stacks anthems, haste, extra combats, protection, and attack-trigger payoffs like battle cry. However, Gruul brings haste, larger bodies, trample, and ramp that deploys multiple attackers per turn.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Aggro decks?\nBoard wipes can erase several turns of development with one card, so overextending is a constant risk. Dealing 120 total damage across three opponents means the deck can run out of gas without dedicated refueling. Pillow-fort and lifegain decks can outlast early pressure and take over once the game goes long. Late-game topdecks can be weak because cheap attackers lose impact as the table develops.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,aristocrats,page_text,11277,"Best Aristocrats Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nAristocrats\nBack to SearchBest Aristocrats Commanders for EDH\nAristocrats decks are for players who enjoy turning expendable creatures into cards, Treasure, damage, and life drain whenever they die. This strategy wants to make cheap bodies, use sacrifice commanders and free outlets, and convert each death trigger into value to win through drain effects or recursive sacrifice loops. Unlike Tokens, it cares less about attacking with many bodies, and unlike broad Sacrifice decks it is specifically built around death trigger payoffs. The best aristocrats commanders let you grind from small pieces while the table decides whether removing your board helps you or hurts them.\nBest Colors for Aristocrats Decks\nOrzhov supplies tokens, recursion, black drain payoffs, and sacrifice outlets for classic aristocrats decks.\nRakdos pairs sacrifice payoffs with damage triggers and Treasure to pressure life totals quickly.\nMardu combines white tokens, black drain, and red damage for dense outlet and payoff packages.\nGolgari adds recursion and creature value, letting the same bodies die more than once.\nHow Do Aristocrats Decks Win in Commander?\nAristocrats decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy cheap creatures and token makers that are useful when they die.\n✓ You set up free sacrifice outlets so bodies become value at instant speed.\n✓ You stack death trigger payoffs that drain life draw cards or make Treasure.\n✓ You rebuild after sweepers with low cost pieces and recursion.\n✓ You close by chaining sacrifices until repeated drain finishes the table.\nBest Cards for Aristocrats EDH Decks\nBlood Artist\nSignature death trigger payoff that drains an opponent whenever creatures die.\nZulaport Cutthroat\nDrains each opponent when your creatures die, scaling well in multiplayer.\nViscera Seer\nFree sacrifice outlet that scries and lets you respond to removal.\nPitiless Plunderer\nConverts creature deaths into Treasure to fund longer sacrifice chains.\nMayhem Devil\nTurns sacrificed permanents into damage, adding reach and spot removal.\nHow to Build a Aristocrats Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-10 death trigger payoffs like Blood Artist, Zulaport Cutthroat, and Bastion of Remembrance\n✓Include 5-8 free sacrifice outlets such as Viscera Seer, Carrion Feeder, and Ashnod's Altar\n✓Pack 10-12 fodder sources, using cheap recursive creatures and token makers to keep outlets active\n✓Add recursion so key payoffs and outlets return after removal or sweepers\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep the mana curve low so most engine pieces cost one to three mana\n✓Favor creatures that replace themselves or create extra bodies when they enter or die\n✓Include sacrifice-based removal that doubles as interaction and trigger fuel\n✓Use Treasure producers like Pitiless Plunderer to turn deaths into mana\nWin Conditions\n✓Drain the table with stacked death triggers during a mass sacrifice turn\n✓Assemble loops that repeatedly sacrifice and return a creature for infinite triggers\n✓Grind opponents out of resources, then finish with repeated drain\nWeaknesses of Aristocrats Decks\n⚠Aristocrats is vulnerable to graveyard hate like Rest in Peace, which shuts off recursion and some death loops.\n⚠The deck can struggle against exile removal or effects that stop death triggers from resolving.\n⚠Tax effects on activated abilities can slow free outlets enough for opponents to stabilize.\n⚠Drain damage often accumulates over several turns, so fast combo can win before the engine matters.\nIs Aristocrats Right for You?\nAristocrats fits players who enjoy small decisions, sacrifice timing, and turning removal into another trigger. The skill floor is moderate because you must track death triggers, but the core loop of fodder, outlet, and payoff is clear. Budget builds are realistic because many staple outlets and drain payoffs are inexpensive.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nSacrifice\nSacrifice is the broader engine; Aristocrats stacks death trigger payoffs as the win route.\nTokens\nTokens makes many bodies to use; Aristocrats sacrifices those bodies for death triggers.\nLifegain\nLife drain overlaps, but Lifegain starts from life triggers instead of creature deaths.\nGraveyard\nAristocrats fills the graveyard incidentally; Graveyard decks make recursion the central engine.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Aristocrats CommandersTreasureDiscard\nTop Aristocrats Commanders\nTeysa Karlov\nMV: 4\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$2.65TCGPlayer\n€1.68CardMarket\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth\nMV: 4\nAristocratsReanimatorSacrifice+7 more\n$2.38TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nSephiroth, Fabled SOLDIER // Sephiroth, One-Winged Angel\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nAristocratsSacrificeCombo+7 more\n$28.89TCGPlayer\n€27.97CardMarket\nChatterfang, Squirrel General\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$11.09TCGPlayer\n€7.02CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nYgra, Eater of All\nMV: 5\nSacrificeAristocratsTokens+7 more\n$11.96TCGPlayer\n€8.01CardMarket\nShirei, Shizo's Caretaker\nMV: 5\nAristocratsReanimatorSacrifice+7 more\n$0.27TCGPlayer\n€0.08CardMarket\nYawgmoth, Thran Physician\nMV: 4\nAristocrats-1/-1 CountersCombo+7 more\n$28.92TCGPlayer\n€27.94CardMarket\nCaesar, Legion's Emperor\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nTokensAristocratsHumans+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nZurgo Stormrender\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsAggro+7 more\n$4.58TCGPlayer\n€3.43CardMarket\nAyara, First of Locthwain\nMV: 3\nAristocratsLifegainTokens+7 more\n$8.06TCGPlayer\n€7.04CardMarket\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver\nMV: 4\nZombiesTokensAristocrats+7 more\n$9.30TCGPlayer\n€10.24CardMarket\nElenda, the Dusk Rose\nMV: 4\nAristocratsVampiresLifegain+7 more\n$6.27TCGPlayer\n€5.93CardMarket\nBraids, Arisen Nightmare\nMV: 3\nAristocratsSacrificeReanimator+7 more\n$4.61TCGPlayer\n€4.68CardMarket\nSavra, Queen of the Golgari\nMV: 4\nAristocratsSacrificeLifegain+7 more\n$7.84TCGPlayer\n€6.65CardMarket\nMarchesa, the Black Rose\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersAristocratsReanimator+7 more\n$3.19TCGPlayer\n€1.23CardMarket\nKorvold, Fae-Cursed King\nMV: 5\nSacrificeTreasureAristocrats+7 more\n$0.41TCGPlayer\n€1.13CardMarket\nHazel of the Rootbloom\nMV: 4\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$3.22TCGPlayer\n€4.20CardMarket\nElas il-Kor, Sadistic Pilgrim\nMV: 2\nLifegainAristocratsTokens+7 more\n$2.85TCGPlayer\n€1.22CardMarket\nProssh, Skyraider of Kher\nMV: 6\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.87CardMarket\nTeysa, Orzhov Scion\nMV: 3\nAristocratsTokensCombo+7 more\n$4.32TCGPlayer\n€1.63CardMarket\nSlimefoot and Squee\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAristocratsSaprolings+7 more\n$3.90TCGPlayer\n€2.69CardMarket\nAthreos, God of Passage\nMV: 3\nShadowborn ApostlesAristocratsLifegain+7 more\n$15.10TCGPlayer\n€10.69CardMarket\nClavileño, First of the Blessed\nMV: 3\nVampiresLifegainAristocrats+7 more\n$0.71TCGPlayer\n€0.86CardMarket\nHenzie \"Toolbox\" Torre\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAggroAristocrats+7 more\n$1.00TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Aristocrats commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Aristocrats commander?\nThe best aristocrats commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy cheap creatures and token makers that are useful when they die.\nHow do Aristocrats decks win?\nAristocrats decks win through a combination of Blood Artist, Zulaport Cutthroat, Viscera Seer, and other synergistic pieces that aristocrats decks are for players who enjoy turning expendable creatures into cards, treasure, damage, and life drain whenever they die.\nWhat are the best colors for Aristocrats?\nOrzhov supplies tokens, recursion, black drain payoffs, and sacrifice outlets for classic aristocrats decks. However, Rakdos pairs sacrifice payoffs with damage triggers and Treasure to pressure life totals quickly.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Aristocrats decks?\nAristocrats is vulnerable to graveyard hate like Rest in Peace, which shuts off recursion and some death loops. The deck can struggle against exile removal or effects that stop death triggers from resolving. Tax effects on activated abilities can slow free outlets enough for opponents to stabilize. Drain damage often accumulates over several turns, so fast combo can win before the engine matters.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,artifacts,page_text,11178,"Best Artifact Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nArtifacts\nBack to SearchBest Artifacts Commanders for EDH\nArtifact decks are for players who want cheap permanents, mana rocks, and utility pieces to become one connected engine. This strategy wants to build artifact count and mana density, using artifact commanders, cost reducers, Treasure, recursion, and untap effects, to win through construct armies, loops, or artifact payoffs. Unlike Equipment, it cares about every artifact rather than just weapons, and unlike Ramp it turns the mana rocks themselves into the engine instead of only casting bigger spells. The best artifact decks EDH players build reward careful sequencing because each small piece can convert the next piece into more mana or cards.\nBest Colors for Artifacts Decks\nMono-blue supplies cost reducers, untappers, artifact recursion, and artificer payoffs.\nIzzet adds Treasure, artifact sacrifice, and spell turns that convert artifacts into bursts of mana.\nAzorius adds protection, recursion, and taxing artifacts that keep artifact engines online.\nEsper layers black recursion and white protection onto blue artifact density for resilient engines.\nHow Do Artifacts Decks Win in Commander?\nArtifacts decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy cheap artifacts and mana rocks to reach critical mass early.\n✓ You use cost reducers affinity and Treasure to cast multiple pieces in one turn.\n✓ You untap sacrifice or recur artifacts to convert small permanents into cards and mana.\n✓ You rebuild from the graveyard after removal with artifact recursion.\n✓ You win through constructs loops drain payoffs or one large artifact threat.\nBest Cards for Artifacts EDH Decks\nEmry, Lurker of the Loch\nMills and recasts artifacts, turning the graveyard into a reusable engine.\nEtherium Sculptor\nReduces artifact costs so cheap pieces chain through a single turn.\nAcademy Manufactor\nTurns each Treasure, Clue, or Food into all three resources.\nClock of Omens\nUses spare artifacts to untap key pieces and enable activation loops.\nMystic Forge\nLets you cast artifacts from the top, keeping chains from running out.\nHow to Build a Artifacts Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 25-35 artifacts so affinity, metalcraft, and count-based payoffs turn on reliably\n✓Include 3-5 cost reducers like Etherium Sculptor, Foundry Inspector, and Cloud Key\n✓Use cheap cantrip artifacts such as Prophetic Prism, Mishra's Bauble, and Ichor Wellspring\n✓Add Treasure producers and Academy Manufactor to convert triggers into mana and cards\nEngine and Resilience\n✓Use untappers like Clock of Omens and Unwinding Clock to double key activations\n✓Include recursion such as Emry, Scrap Trawler, or Teshar to rebuild after wipes\n✓Refill with Mystic Forge, Thoughtcast, Reckoner's Bargain, or commander-based draw\n✓Protect key pieces with Welding Jar, Darksteel Forge, or counterspell backup\nWin Conditions\n✓Make large constructs with Urza, Karnstruct tokens, or Cyberdrive Awakener\n✓Assemble mana loops that convert untaps and Treasures into a lethal payoff\n✓Use Aetherflux Reservoir or Marionette Master to close games without combat\n✓Cast one large artifact threat when your engine produces more mana than opponents can answer\nWeaknesses of Artifacts Decks\n⚠Mass artifact removal like Vandalblast or Farewell can dismantle the engine in one spell.\n⚠Collector Ouphe, Stony Silence, and similar stax pieces can shut off key activations.\n⚠Early turns spent deploying small artifacts can fall behind fast creature pressure.\n⚠Engine-heavy hands can stall when cost reducers or payoff cards are removed immediately.\nIs Artifacts Right for You?\nArtifacts reward players who enjoy sequencing and turning small permanents into larger engines. Budget builds can start with inexpensive trinkets, cost reducers, and recursion, while premium rocks mainly raise the ceiling. The deck asks you to track many moving pieces, but each piece usually explains what it is converting into mana, cards, or damage.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nEquipment\nEquipment focuses on weapons and carriers; Artifacts cares about every artifact type.\nStax\nStax uses artifacts to deny resources; Artifacts uses them to build engines.\nRamp\nRamp uses mana rocks to accelerate; Artifacts makes the rocks part of the payoff.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Artifacts CommandersTreasureEquipment\nTop Artifacts Commanders\nBreya, Etherium Shaper\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboTokens+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nUrza, Chief Artificer\nMV: 6\nArtifactsTokensAffinity+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€2.01CardMarket\nUrtet, Remnant of Memnarch\nMV: 3\nArtifactsMyr+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$6.36TCGPlayer\n€2.66CardMarket\nUrza, Lord High Artificer\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$16.28TCGPlayer\n€14.42CardMarket\nIron Man, Titan of Innovation\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nArtifactsBirthing PodCombo+7 more\n$20.10TCGPlayer\n€15.83CardMarket\nMishra, Eminent One\nMV: 5\nArtifactsTokensClones+7 more\n$0.47TCGPlayer\n€0.36CardMarket\nShorikai, Genesis Engine\nMV: 4\nVehiclesArtifactsControl+7 more\n$2.86TCGPlayer\n€2.66CardMarket\nMendicant Core, Guidelight\nMV: 2\nArtifactsTokensClones+7 more\n$0.78TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nToph, the First Metalbender\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nLands MatterArtifacts+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.28TCGPlayer\n€0.75CardMarket\nImotekh the Stormlord\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nArtifactsReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKarn, Legacy Reforged\nMV: 5\nArtifactsComboRamp+7 more\n$19.46TCGPlayer\n€13.08CardMarket\nBello, Bard of the Brambles\nMV: 3\nEnchantressArtifactsUnnatural+7 more\n$2.87TCGPlayer\n€3.35CardMarket\nRagost, Deft Gastronaut\nMV: 2\nArtifactsBurnLifegain+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.22CardMarket\nLeonardo da Vinci\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nArtifactsThoptersTokens+7 more\n$0.72TCGPlayer\n€1.23CardMarket\nEmry, Lurker of the Loch\nMV: 3\nArtifactsComboMill+7 more\n$0.63TCGPlayer\n€0.80CardMarket\nOsgir, the Reconstructor\nMV: 4\nArtifactsReanimatorTokens+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nImskir Iron-Eater\nMV: 8\nArtifactsAffinitySacrifice+7 more\n$0.30TCGPlayer\n€0.13CardMarket\nJhoira, Weatherlight Captain\nMV: 4\nArtifactsStormCheerios+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.89CardMarket\nKilo, Apogee Mind\nMV: 3\nArtifacts+1/+1 CountersProliferate+7 more\n$2.07TCGPlayer\n€0.66CardMarket\nTetzin, Gnome Champion // The Golden-Gear Colossus\nMV: 3\nArtifactsTokensGnomes+7 more\n$0.29TCGPlayer\n€0.21CardMarket\nMegatron, Tyrant // Megatron, Destructive Force\nMV: 6Universes Beyond\nArtifactsReanimatorBurn+7 more\n$1.81TCGPlayer\n€0.88CardMarket\nInspirit, Flagship Vessel\nMV: 3\nArtifacts+1/+1 CountersCharge Counters+7 more\n$1.20TCGPlayer\n€0.76CardMarket\nAlela, Artful Provocateur\nMV: 4\nEnchantressFaeriesArtifacts+7 more\n$1.36TCGPlayer\n€0.51CardMarket\nGolbez, Crystal Collector\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nArtifactsReanimatorCombo+7 more\n$0.45TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nUrza, Lord Protector\nMV: 3\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$9.63TCGPlayer\n€7.46CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Artifacts commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Artifacts commander?\nThe best artifacts commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy cheap artifacts and mana rocks to reach critical mass early.\nHow do Artifacts decks win?\nArtifacts decks win through a combination of Emry, Lurker of the Loch, Etherium Sculptor, Academy Manufactor, and other synergistic pieces that artifact decks are for players who want cheap permanents, mana rocks, and utility pieces to become one connected engine.\nWhat are the best colors for Artifacts?\nMono-blue supplies cost reducers, untappers, artifact recursion, and artificer payoffs. However, Izzet adds Treasure, artifact sacrifice, and spell turns that convert artifacts into bursts of mana.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Artifacts decks?\nMass artifact removal like Vandalblast or Farewell can dismantle the engine in one spell. Collector Ouphe, Stony Silence, and similar stax pieces can shut off key activations. Early turns spent deploying small artifacts can fall behind fast creature pressure. Engine-heavy hands can stall when cost reducers or payoff cards are removed immediately.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
