nd\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nBig Mana\nBack to SearchBest Big Mana Commanders for EDH\nBig Mana decks are for players who want the game to build toward casting one spell that changes every resource count at once. This strategy wants to accelerate beyond normal mana development, using land ramp, mana rocks, doublers, and big mana commanders, to win through X spells, huge spells EDH tables have to answer, or haymakers like Torment of Hailfire and Expropriate. In the Ramp, Landfall, and Big Mana overlap, Ramp is acceleration, Landfall turns land drops into triggers, and Big Mana is the payoff plan for spending all that mana. Unlike Storm, it usually casts fewer spells in a turn and asks each one to matter on resolution.\nBest Colors for Big Mana Decks\nMono-green stacks land ramp, mana doublers, creature draw, and Genesis Wave-style payoffs.\nGruul pairs green acceleration with red Treasure bursts, damage-based X spells, and large combat threats.\nTemur adds blue card draw, copy effects, and Expropriate-class spells to a green ramp shell.\nMono-black uses Cabal Coffers-style lands, rituals, tutors, and Torment of Hailfire finishes.\nHow Do Big Mana Decks Win in Commander?\nBig Mana decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You spend the early turns accelerating with land ramp mana rocks dorks and doublers.\n✓ You protect your mana engine long enough to untap with more mana than the table.\n✓ You cast fewer spells than Storm and make each huge spell matter on resolution.\n✓ You aim X spells and haymakers where they convert mana into cards damage turns or permanents.\n✓ You rebuild through land-based acceleration after sweepers and keep looking for the next payoff.\nBest Cards for Big Mana EDH Decks\nTorment of Hailfire\nConverts a large X value into pressure on each opponent's life, hand, and board.\nGenesis Wave\nTurns green mana into permanents from the top of your library.\nExpropriate\nA ten-mana spell that converts votes into extra turns and stolen permanents.\nNyxbloom Ancient\nTriples mana from your sources, pushing X spells and haymakers far ahead.\nRishkar's Expertise\nRefills from your largest creature and casts another spell without paying mana.\nHow to Build a Big Mana Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 12-15 ramp pieces, mixing land ramp, mana rocks, and dorks according to your colors\n✓Add 2-4 mana multipliers such as Nyxbloom Ancient, Mana Reflection, or Zendikar Resurgent\n✓Include big mana lands like Nykthos, Shrine to Nyx or Cabal Coffers where your colors allow\n✓Pack 8-10 draw effects so you keep finding payoffs after each haymaker resolves\nWin Conditions\n✓Choose 4-6 haymakers such as X spells, Expropriate-class turns, or Genesis Wave-style deck dumps\n✓Favor payoffs that change the game on resolution instead of needing another full turn\n✓Include one instant-speed or hard-to-counter finisher when your pod has many blue decks\n✓Use X spells because they scale with early, medium, and late mana totals\nDeck Structure\n✓Play 38-40 mana sources counting lands and rocks so your opening hands develop reliably\n✓Keep interaction light but real, with 5-7 answers for faster combo decks and hate pieces\n✓Pick a commander that ramps, doubles mana, or gives huge spells immediate impact\n✓Cut mid-size value cards when they do not ramp, draw, protect, or finish\nWeaknesses of Big Mana Decks\n⚠A single counterspell on the haymaker can waste a full turn of mana and tempo.\n⚠The deck can struggle against aggro and combo decks that end games before the big turn arrives.\n⚠Hands heavy on ramp but light on payoffs can leave you with mana and nothing meaningful to cast.\n⚠Visible acceleration can make the table pressure you before you untap with the payoff.\nIs Big Mana Right for You?\nBig Mana is approachable because the plan is direct: ramp, protect your life total, then spend a large mana pool. It works at many budgets because Cultivate effects, mana rocks, and X spells can carry the shell while premium doublers stay optional. It suits players who enjoy visible buildup and high-impact turns more than hidden setup.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nRamp\nRamp accelerates mana; Big Mana spends that mana on enormous payoffs.\nStorm\nStorm chains many cheap spells; Big Mana resolves fewer huge ones.\nLandfall\nLandfall wins from land triggers; Big Mana wants the mana those lands produce.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Big Mana CommandersLands MatterTreasure\nTop Big Mana Commanders\nZhulodok, Void Gorger\nMV: 6\nEldraziBig ManaCascade+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nJodah, Archmage Eternal\nMV: 4\nBig ManaGood StuffCascade+7 more\n$1.02TCGPlayer\n€0.36CardMarket\nThe Infamous Cruelclaw\nMV: 3\nBig ManaObosh CompanionLands Matter+7 more\n$1.54TCGPlayer\n€1.68CardMarket\nImoti, Celebrant of Bounty\nMV: 5\nBig ManaCascadeRamp+7 more\n$0.35TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nKona, Rescue Beastie\nMV: 4\nBig ManaRampAggro+7 more\n$1.11TCGPlayer\n€0.62CardMarket\nUlalek, Fused Atrocity\nMV: 5\nEldraziBig ManaSacrifice+7 more\n$0.82TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nMarvo, Deep Operative\nMV: 5\nTopdeckBig ManaSea Creatures+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nJhoira of the Ghitu\nMV: 3\nBig ManaSuspendExtra Turns+7 more\n$0.49TCGPlayer\n€0.46CardMarket\nHelga, Skittish Seer\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersRampBig Mana+7 more\n$4.42TCGPlayer\n€4.09CardMarket\nKozilek, the Great Distortion\nMV: 10\nEldraziArtifactsBig Mana+7 more\n$6.40TCGPlayer\n€4.13CardMarket\nAshling, Rekindled // Ashling, Rimebound\nMV: 2\nSpellslingerRampBig Mana+7 more\n$0.56TCGPlayer\n€1.07CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of Mana\nMV: 3\nRampBig ManaVoltron+7 more\n$4.18TCGPlayer\n€1.80CardMarket\nThe Ur-Dragon\nMV: 9\nDragonsFlyingShapeshifters+7 more\n$37.54TCGPlayer\n€30.89CardMarket\nMayael the Anima\nMV: 3\nBig ManaRampStompy+7 more\n$2.94TCGPlayer\n€0.70CardMarket\nMaelstrom Wanderer\nMV: 8\nCascadeRampChaos+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nRaggadragga, Goreguts Boss\nMV: 4\nRampAggroBig Mana+7 more\n$0.25TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nBraids, Conjurer Adept\nMV: 4\nGroup HugBig ManaClones+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.32CardMarket\nZaxara, the Exemplary\nMV: 4\nX Spells+1/+1 CountersHydras+7 more\n$1.98TCGPlayer\n€1.32CardMarket\nYurlok of Scorch Thrash\nMV: 4\nGroup SlugBurnGroup Hug+7 more\n$0.30TCGPlayer\n€0.24CardMarket\nBelbe, Corrupted Observer\nMV: 2\nBig ManaRampEldrazi+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of All\nMV: 5\nLegendsLands MatterPhyrexians+7 more\n$0.56TCGPlayer\n€0.22CardMarket\nRuby, Daring Tracker\nMV: 2\nRampCascadeBig Mana+7 more\n$0.26TCGPlayer\n€0.13CardMarket\nKinnan, Bonder Prodigy\nMV: 2\nComboRampMidrange+7 more\n$16.80TCGPlayer\n€12.90CardMarket\nMagus Lucea Kane\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nX Spells+1/+1 CountersTyranids+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKruphix, God of Horizons\nMV: 5\nEldraziRampX Spells+7 more\n$4.64TCGPlayer\n€2.09CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Big Mana commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Big Mana commander?\nThe best big mana commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you spend the early turns accelerating with land ramp mana rocks dorks and doublers.\nHow do Big Mana decks win?\nBig Mana decks win through a combination of Torment of Hailfire, Genesis Wave, Expropriate, and other synergistic pieces that big mana decks are for players who want the game to build toward casting one spell that changes every resource count at once.\nWhat are the best colors for Big Mana?\nMono-green stacks land ramp, mana doublers, creature draw, and Genesis Wave-style payoffs. However, Gruul pairs green acceleration with red Treasure bursts, damage-based X spells, and large combat threats.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Big Mana decks?\nA single counterspell on the haymaker can waste a full turn of mana and tempo. The deck can struggle against aggro and combo decks that end games before the big turn arrives. Hands heavy on ramp but light on payoffs can leave you with mana and nothing meaningful to cast. Visible acceleration can make the table pressure you before you untap with the payoff.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,blink,page_text,10490,"Best Blink Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nBlink\nBack to SearchBest Blink Commanders for EDH\nBlink decks are for players who want every creature to matter more than once. This strategy wants to exile and return creatures with enter-the-battlefield effects, using blink commanders, flicker spells, and repeatable engines, to win through compounding value, protected threats, or trigger loops. Unlike Control, Blink handles the game through reusable creature triggers instead of mostly counterspells, and unlike Tokens it may make bodies incidentally rather than going wide first. The best blink commanders make modest creatures convert into cards, removal, ramp, and rebuilt boards over several turns.\nBest Colors for Blink Decks\nAzorius pairs white flicker spells with blue draw, bounce, and counterspells for the cleanest blink shell.\nBant adds green ramp and creature quality so blink engines curve out faster and rebuild larger boards.\nEsper adds black removal, drain, and recursion to blink creatures that disrupt or finish games.\nHow Do Blink Decks Win in Commander?\nBlink decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You cast creatures whose enter the battlefield triggers draw remove ramp or make bodies.\n✓ You reuse those triggers with one-shot flicker spells and repeatable blink engines.\n✓ You hold instant-speed blink to protect key creatures from removal or sweepers.\n✓ You grind cards and board presence by making the same creature work every turn.\n✓ You finish with a rebuilt board token engine or trigger loop.\nBest Cards for Blink EDH Decks\nEphemerate\nBlinks a creature for one mana, then rebounds to reuse or protect it again.\nThassa, Deep-Dwelling\nTurns one creature into a repeatable end-step trigger every turn.\nSoulherder\nBlinks each end step and grows as creatures leave exile.\nMulldrifter\nDraws two on entry, making every blink convert into more cards.\nGhostly Flicker\nBlinks two permanents and pairs with recursion creatures for value loops.\nHow to Build a Blink Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 flicker effects such as Ephemerate, Momentary Blink, Ghostly Flicker, and Conjurer's Closet\n✓Play 20-25 creatures with enter-the-battlefield effects that draw, remove, ramp, drain, or make tokens\n✓Include repeatable blink engines like Thassa, Deep-Dwelling and Soulherder\n✓Add recursion creatures such as Archaeomancer or Mnemonic Wall to rebuy flicker spells\nDeck Structure\n✓Prefer interaction on creatures so Reflector Mage, Knight of Autumn, or Ravenous Chupacabra can be reused\n✓Keep mana open when instant-speed blink can protect a key engine\n✓Balance value creatures with actual closers so the deck does not only draw cards\nWin Conditions\n✓Out-card opponents until your rebuilt board is larger than what removal can contain\n✓Blink token producers repeatedly to assemble a wide board without playing a token deck first\n✓Loop drain triggers such as Gray Merchant of Asphodel for a finishing turn\nWeaknesses of Blink Decks\n⚠Effects like Torpor Orb and Hushbringer shut off the enter-the-battlefield triggers the deck relies on.\n⚠Blink can pressure life totals slowly if its value creatures do not convert into a win.\n⚠Exile removal and repeated commander removal can leave the engine without a reusable target.\nIs Blink Right for You?\nBlink is a strong first engine deck because many creatures replace themselves and flicker spells double as protection. It suits players who enjoy sequencing triggers, saving creatures at the right moment, and grinding small advantages into a board. Budget builds work well with cards like Ephemerate, Mulldrifter, and Ghostly Flicker.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nControl\nControl answers with spells; Blink stabilizes through reusable creature triggers.\nCard Draw\nCard Draw sees more cards directly; Blink draws by reusing creatures like Mulldrifter.\nTokens\nTokens goes wide first; Blink may create tokens by looping enter-the-battlefield creatures.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Blink CommandersClonesCantrips\nTop Blink Commanders\nBrago, King Eternal\nMV: 4\nBlinkControlCombo+7 more\n$3.13TCGPlayer\n€2.23CardMarket\nNiko, Light of Hope\nMV: 4\nBlinkTokensClones+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nAtraxa, Grand Unifier\nMV: 7\nBlinkComboInfect+7 more\n$18.36TCGPlayer\n€20.82CardMarket\nAminatou, the Fateshifter\nMV: 3\nBlinkControlCombo+7 more\n$3.69TCGPlayer\n€4.67CardMarket\nPantlaza, Sun-Favored\nMV: 5\nDinosaursDiscoverRamp+7 more\n$5.75TCGPlayer\n€5.93CardMarket\nElesh Norn, Mother of Machines\nMV: 5\nBlinkTokensETB+7 more\n$25.34TCGPlayer\n€21.01CardMarket\nPreston, the Vanisher\nMV: 4\nBlinkTokensCombo+7 more\n$36.14TCGPlayer\n€27.39CardMarket\nKetramose, the New Dawn\nMV: 3\nExileBlinkControl+7 more\n$3.37TCGPlayer\n€5.73CardMarket\nPhelia, Exuberant Shepherd\nMV: 2\nBlink+1/+1 CountersExile+7 more\n$5.82TCGPlayer\n€6.19CardMarket\nRoon of the Hidden Realm\nMV: 5\nBlinkControlETB+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.26CardMarket\nKefka, Court Mage // Kefka, Ruler of Ruin\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nDiscardComboControl+7 more\n$6.86TCGPlayer\n€6.13CardMarket\nYorion, Sky Nomad\nMV: 5\nBlinkControlCombo+7 more\n$0.51TCGPlayer\n€0.74CardMarket\nRanar the Ever-Watchful\nMV: 4\nForetellBlinkTokens+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.30CardMarket\nPlagon, Lord of the Beach\nMV: 3\nToughness MattersBlinkCombo+7 more\n$0.97TCGPlayer\n€1.92CardMarket\nArthur, Marigold Knight\nMV: 5\nBlinkAggroETB+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAang, at the Crossroads // Aang, Destined Savior\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nBlinkClonesCombo+7 more\n$0.59TCGPlayer\n€0.54CardMarket\nKykar, Zephyr Awakener\nMV: 4\nBlinkControlTokens+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nGaladriel, Light of Valinor\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nElves+1/+1 CountersTokens+7 more\n$27.13TCGPlayer\n€23.87CardMarket\nTivit, Seller of Secrets\nMV: 6\nPoliticsArtifactsCombo+7 more\n$8.86TCGPlayer\n€5.89CardMarket\nYarok, the Desecrated\nMV: 5\nLands MatterLandfallETB+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nLagrella, the Magpie\nMV: 3\nBlinkComboControl+7 more\n$0.25TCGPlayer\n€0.13CardMarket\nSyr Vondam, Sunstar Exemplar\nMV: 2\nBlinkAristocratsTokens+7 more\n$0.35TCGPlayer\n€0.68CardMarket\nLoot, the Pathfinder\nMV: 5\nBlinkComboMidrange+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.85CardMarket\nShiko, Paragon of the Way\nMV: 5\nBlinkSpellslingerCombo+7 more\n$0.95TCGPlayer\n€1.25CardMarket\nBreya, Etherium Shaper\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboTokens+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Blink commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Blink commander?\nThe best blink commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you cast creatures whose enter the battlefield triggers draw remove ramp or make bodies.\nHow do Blink decks win?\nBlink decks win through a combination of Ephemerate, Thassa, Deep-Dwelling, Soulherder, and other synergistic pieces that blink decks are for players who want every creature to matter more than once.\nWhat are the best colors for Blink?\nAzorius pairs white flicker spells with blue draw, bounce, and counterspells for the cleanest blink shell. However, Bant adds green ramp and creature quality so blink engines curve out faster and rebuild larger boards.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Blink decks?\nEffects like Torpor Orb and Hushbringer shut off the enter-the-battlefield triggers the deck relies on. Blink can pressure life totals slowly if its value creatures do not convert into a win. Exile removal and repeated commander removal can leave the engine without a reusable target.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,card-draw,page_text,10912,"Best Card Draw Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nCard Draw\nBack to SearchBest Card Draw Commanders for EDH\nCard Draw decks are for players who want a full hand to be the engine, the protection, and eventually the win condition. This strategy wants to draw extra cards every turn, using card draw commanders, repeatable engines, wheels, and draw payoffs, to win through card advantage EDH plans such as damage triggers, stocked interaction, or superior late-game resources. Unlike Control, it is not only answering threats, and unlike Spellslinger it cares about the draw event more than the spell cast. The best card draw commanders either refill you directly or convert each extra card into pressure.\nBest Colors for Card Draw Decks\nMono-blue offers Rhystic Study, Mystic Remora, wheels, cantrips, and broad card advantage engines.\nIzzet pairs draw engines with Niv-Mizzet style damage payoffs and spell support.\nDimir adds drain payoffs, tutors, and wheels that punish opposing draws.\nSimic ties draw to ramp and creatures, turning extra cards into mana and board presence.\nHow Do Card Draw Decks Win in Commander?\nCard Draw decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy cheap draw engines early and let them accumulate cards.\n✓ You draw beyond your normal turn draw whenever the deck can afford it.\n✓ You convert extra cards into damage life drain tokens or stocked answers.\n✓ You hold up interaction because your engines refill what you spend.\n✓ You win late by turning more cards into more relevant resources.\nBest Cards for Card Draw EDH Decks\nRhystic Study\nRepeatable draw engine that taxes opponents or refills your hand.\nMystic Remora\nEarly draw engine that punishes opponents for casting noncreature spells.\nConsecrated Sphinx\nDraws two cards whenever an opponent draws, scaling in multiplayer.\nSheoldred, the Apocalypse\nTurns your draws into life and opponents' draws into damage.\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nConverts card draw and spell casts into direct damage.\nHow to Build a Card Draw Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 10-15 dedicated draw engines such as Rhystic Study, Mystic Remora, and Guardian Project\n✓Include 3-5 draw payoffs that convert cards into damage, tokens, or life drain\n✓Add cheap cantrips like Brainstorm and Ponder to trigger payoffs and smooth draws\n✓Use wheel effects such as Windfall to refill and trigger draw payoffs in bulk\nDeck Structure\n✓Raise the land count or ramp package because you will draw into spells quickly\n✓Include no maximum hand size effects like Reliquary Tower or Thought Vessel\n✓Keep interaction dense because a full hand matters only when you can spend it\n✓Prioritize low-cost engines so draw starts before opponents establish their plans\nWin Conditions\n✓Use Niv-Mizzet, Parun or Psychosis Crawler to turn draws into damage\n✓Pair Sheoldred, the Apocalypse with wheel effects for table-wide drain\n✓Use a stocked hand to out-resource opponents in long games\n✓Some lists win with Laboratory Maniac effects after drawing the library\nWeaknesses of Card Draw Decks\n⚠Draw engines attract removal and table attention, especially when they come down early.\n⚠Raw cards are not a board state, so the deck can struggle against fast creature pressure.\n⚠The deck is vulnerable to effects like Notion Thief or Narset that stop or steal extra draws.\n⚠Careless drawing can deck you or force key cards into discard without a payoff in play.\nIs Card Draw Right for You?\nCard Draw is a friendly engine strategy because extra cards forgive some deckbuilding mistakes and keep options open. Budget engines exist, though premium staples like Rhystic Study can be expensive. It suits players who enjoy planning with a full hand and turning options into a late-game win.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nControl\nControl draws to answer threats; Card Draw makes the draw triggers central.\nSpellslinger\nSpellslinger rewards casting cantrips; Card Draw rewards the cards drawn.\nGroup Hug\nGroup Hug shares resources politically; Card Draw keeps the extra cards mostly private.\nEnchantress\nEnchantress draws from enchantment casts; Card Draw uses broader draw engines and payoffs.\nBlink\nBlink reuses enter triggers for cards; Card Draw runs draw as the main engine.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Card Draw CommandersCantripsWheels\nTop Card Draw Commanders\nNekusar, the Mindrazer\nMV: 5\nWheelsGroup SlugBurn+7 more\n$4.00TCGPlayer\n€1.23CardMarket\nMs. Bumbleflower\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersGroup HugWheels+7 more\n$6.54TCGPlayer\n€15.35CardMarket\nXyris, the Writhing Storm\nMV: 5\nWheelsTokensGroup Hug+7 more\n$2.10TCGPlayer\n€0.97CardMarket\nQueza, Augur of Agonies\nMV: 4\nWheelsLifegainCard Draw+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.12CardMarket\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nMV: 6\nSpellslingerWheelsCombo+7 more\n$5.27TCGPlayer\n€2.87CardMarket\nVnwxt, Verbose Host\nMV: 2\nCard DrawWheelsMill+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.35CardMarket\nSheoldred, the Apocalypse\nMV: 4\nWheelsLifegainCard Draw+7 more\n$101.76TCGPlayer\n€82.45CardMarket\nTemmet, Naktamun's Will\nMV: 5\nZombiesTokensCard Draw+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.59CardMarket\nJudith, Carnage Connoisseur\nMV: 5\nBurnSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nMinn, Wily Illusionist\nMV: 3\nWheelsIllusionsCard Draw+7 more\n$9.04TCGPlayer\n€10.19CardMarket\nKefka, Court Mage // Kefka, Ruler of Ruin\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nDiscardComboControl+7 more\n$6.86TCGPlayer\n€6.13CardMarket\nMorska, Undersea Sleuth\nMV: 3\nCluesTokensArtifacts+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKwain, Itinerant Meddler\nMV: 2\nWheelsGroup HugCard Draw+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.31CardMarket\nThe Locust God\nMV: 6\nWheelsTokensCard Draw+7 more\n$7.55TCGPlayer\n€6.66CardMarket\nShorikai, Genesis Engine\nMV: 4\nVehiclesArtifactsControl+7 more\n$2.86TCGPlayer\n€2.66CardMarket\nKynaios and Tiro of Meletis\nMV: 4\nGroup HugWheelsLands Matter+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait\nMV: 6\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$12.07TCGPlayer\n€13.93CardMarket\nThe Council of Four\nMV: 5\nWheelsTokensGroup Hug+7 more\n$3.77TCGPlayer\n€1.45CardMarket\nMarneus Calgar\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nTokensComboAristocrats+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKami of the Crescent Moon\nMV: 2\nWheelsGroup HugCard Draw+7 more\n$7.65TCGPlayer\n€5.36CardMarket\nCaptain Howler, Sea Scourge\nMV: 4\nDiscardWheelsAggro+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nAzami, Lady of Scrolls\nMV: 5\nWizardsComboControl+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.36CardMarket\nSokrates, Athenian Teacher\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nWheelsGroup HugCard Draw+7 more\n$0.95TCGPlayer\n€0.56CardMarket\nThe Watcher in the Water\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nWheelsTokensCantrips+7 more\n$2.52TCGPlayer\n€1.74CardMarket\nMr. Foxglove\nMV: 5\nWheelsCard DrawCantrips+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nFilter and browse all Card Draw commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Card Draw commander?\nThe best card draw commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy cheap draw engines early and let them accumulate cards.\nHow do Card Draw decks win?\nCard Draw decks win through a combination of Rhystic Study, Mystic Remora, Consecrated Sphinx, and other synergistic pieces that card draw decks are for players who want a full hand to be the engine, the protection, and eventually the win condition.\nWhat are the best colors for Card Draw?\nMono-blue offers Rhystic Study, Mystic Remora, wheels, cantrips, and broad card advantage engines. However, Izzet pairs draw engines with Niv-Mizzet style damage payoffs and spell support.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Card Draw decks?\nDraw engines attract removal and table attention, especially when they come down early. Raw cards are not a board state, so the deck can struggle against fast creature pressure. The deck is vulnerable to effects like Notion Thief or Narset that stop or steal extra draws. Careless drawing can deck you or force key cards into discard without a payoff in play.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,combo,page_text,10701,"Best Combo Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nCombo\nBack to SearchBest Combo Commanders for EDH\nCombo decks are for players who enjoy finding a narrow line, protecting it, and ending the game with specific cards working together. This strategy wants to assemble combo commanders, tutors, draw, and compact two-card or three-card packages, using infinite combos or deterministic loops, to win in one decisive moment. Unlike Spellslinger it is not paid for every spell cast, unlike Storm it does not need a long chain turn, and unlike Big Mana it does not rely on casting one enormous payoff. The best combo decks EDH players build balance speed with enough interaction to survive the table's disruption.\nBest Colors for Combo Decks\nDimir offers tutors, counterspells, and compact wins like Thassa's Oracle lines.\nIzzet supports copy loops and Dramatic Scepter mana with blue protection.\nOrzhov uses lifegain loops, enchantment tutors, and Silence effects to protect wins.\nGolgari builds creature loops with persist, undying, sacrifice outlets, and death triggers.\nHow Do Combo Decks Win in Commander?\nCombo decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You build around a small number of card combinations that win once assembled.\n✓ You spend early turns ramping drawing and tutoring toward those pieces.\n✓ You hold protection to push the combo through disruption.\n✓ You read the table before committing because a failed attempt changes how opponents treat you.\n✓ You win in one decisive moment rather than through combat pressure.\nBest Cards for Combo EDH Decks\nDemonic Tutor\nFinds the missing piece that turns a partial setup into a win attempt.\nThassa's Oracle\nWins with an empty library after Consultation-style or self-mill setups.\nKiki-Jiki, Mirror Breaker\nCombines with untappers to create unlimited hasty attackers.\nIsochron Scepter\nPairs with Dramatic Reversal and mana rocks to produce unlimited mana.\nExquisite Blood\nForms an infinite drain loop with Sanguine Bond.\nHow to Build a Combo Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Choose 2-3 compact combos that share colors or pieces, such as Exquisite Blood lines\n✓Run 5-10 tutors such as Demonic Tutor, Mystical Tutor, and Enlightened Tutor\n✓Include 8-12 card draw and selection effects to dig when tutors run out\n✓Add a clear outlet for infinite mana or triggers, such as Walking Ballista\nProtection & Interaction\n✓Reserve 6-10 slots for counterspells, Silence effects, or protection pieces\n✓Use cheap removal to buy time and break up opposing combo attempts\n✓Play redundant pieces because one removal spell can stop a narrow line\nWin Conditions\n✓Turn infinite mana into an X spell or activated ability that ends the game\n✓Use Kiki-Jiki style loops for unlimited attackers or repeated death triggers\n✓Use Thassa's Oracle packages when the deck wants a compact noncombat finish\nWeaknesses of Combo Decks\n⚠A single counterspell or removal spell at the wrong moment can stop the whole plan without backup pieces.\n⚠Once the table knows your combo, opponents often save interaction for you first.\n⚠Stax pieces and tax effects can slow tutors and spells enough to leave the deck stranded.\n⚠Tutor-heavy builds can feel repetitive if they find the same line every game.\nIs Combo Right for You?\nCombo suits players who enjoy deckbuilding puzzles, sequencing, and choosing the right window for a win attempt. Power level varies widely, so match your combo speed and tutor density to the table before the game starts. Budget entry points exist, but fast tutor-heavy builds get expensive quickly.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nSpellslinger\nSpellslinger earns per-cast value; Combo assembles specific pieces for a finish.\nStorm\nStorm chains many spells in one turn; Combo assembles fixed pieces.\nControl\nControl answers first and may finish with combo; Combo builds around the pieces.\nStax\nStax locks resources down; Combo uses protection to resolve its own pieces.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Combo CommandersWheelsTreasure\nTop Combo Commanders\nK'rrik, Son of Yawgmoth\nMV: 7\nLifegainComboSelf-Damage+7 more\n$1.12TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nBreya, Etherium Shaper\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboTokens+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nVivi Ornitier\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerStormBurn+7 more\n$34.43TCGPlayer\n€30.58CardMarket\nKinnan, Bonder Prodigy\nMV: 2\nComboRampMidrange+7 more\n$16.80TCGPlayer\n€12.90CardMarket\nUrza, Lord High Artificer\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$16.28TCGPlayer\n€14.42CardMarket\nChatterfang, Squirrel General\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$11.09TCGPlayer\n€7.02CardMarket\nSisay, Weatherlight Captain\nMV: 3\nLegendsHistoricCombo+7 more\n$2.50TCGPlayer\n€1.48CardMarket\nKefka, Court Mage // Kefka, Ruler of Ruin\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nDiscardComboControl+7 more\n$6.86TCGPlayer\n€6.13CardMarket\nStella Lee, Wild Card\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerComboStorm+7 more\n$5.05TCGPlayer\n€3.70CardMarket\nMagda, Brazen Outlaw\nMV: 2\nTreasureDwarvesArtifacts+7 more\n$8.78TCGPlayer\n€6.70CardMarket\nKrenko, Mob Boss\nMV: 4\nGoblinsTokensAggro+7 more\n$2.80TCGPlayer\n€1.72CardMarket\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver\nMV: 4\nZombiesTokensAristocrats+7 more\n$9.30TCGPlayer\n€10.24CardMarket\nKorvold, Fae-Cursed King\nMV: 5\nSacrificeTreasureAristocrats+7 more\n$0.41TCGPlayer\n€1.13CardMarket\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nMV: 6\nSpellslingerWheelsCombo+7 more\n$5.27TCGPlayer\n€2.87CardMarket\nFire Lord Azula\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerComboSpell Copy+7 more\n$1.16TCGPlayer\n€1.05CardMarket\nKenrith, the Returned King\nMV: 5\nGroup HugComboPolitics+7 more\n$3.03TCGPlayer\n€1.08CardMarket\nCeles, Rune Knight\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nReanimatorCombo+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.89TCGPlayer\n€0.82CardMarket\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nControlSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$5.35TCGPlayer\n€2.75CardMarket\nSephiroth, Fabled SOLDIER // Sephiroth, One-Winged Angel\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nAristocratsSacrificeCombo+7 more\n$28.89TCGPlayer\n€27.97CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nEtali, Primal Conqueror // Etali, Primal Sickness\nMV: 7\nClonesComboRamp+7 more\n$30.15TCGPlayer\n€21.25CardMarket\nInalla, Archmage Ritualist\nMV: 5\nWizardsComboSpellslinger+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAtraxa, Grand Unifier\nMV: 7\nBlinkComboInfect+7 more\n$18.36TCGPlayer\n€20.82CardMarket\nTayam, Luminous Enigma\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersComboReanimator+7 more\n$3.31TCGPlayer\n€1.38CardMarket\nGlarb, Calamity's Augur\nMV: 3\nLands MatterFrogsReanimator+7 more\n$3.82TCGPlayer\n€4.07CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Combo commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Combo commander?\nThe best combo commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you build around a small number of card combinations that win once assembled.\nHow do Combo decks win?\nCombo decks win through a combination of Demonic Tutor, Thassa's Oracle, Kiki-Jiki, Mirror Breaker, and other synergistic pieces that combo decks are for players who enjoy finding a narrow line, protecting it, and ending the game with specific cards working together.\nWhat are the best colors for Combo?\nDimir offers tutors, counterspells, and compact wins like Thassa's Oracle lines. However, Izzet supports copy loops and Dramatic Scepter mana with blue protection.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Combo decks?\nA single counterspell or removal spell at the wrong moment can stop the whole plan without backup pieces. Once the table knows your combo, opponents often save interaction for you first. Stax pieces and tax effects can slow tutors and spells enough to leave the deck stranded. Tutor-heavy builds can feel repetitive if they find the same line every game.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,control,page_text,11221,"Best Control Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nControl\nBack to SearchBest Control Commanders for EDH\nControl decks are for players who enjoy deciding which threats matter, spending answers carefully, and winning after the table has slowed down. This strategy wants to stabilize the game, using control commanders, counterspells, removal, sweepers, and card-draw engines, to win through a protected finisher, planeswalker, combo finish, or long-term card advantage. Unlike Stax, Control answers threats on the stack or battlefield instead of locking everyone out of resources. The best control decks EDH players build accept that multiplayer control cannot counter everything, so they lean on sweepers and engines that trade across several opponents.\nBest Colors for Control Decks\nAzorius combines blue counters and draw with white sweepers, removal, and taxes.\nDimir trades white sweepers for black removal, discard, tutors, and graveyard value.\nEsper combines sweepers, counterspells, removal, and finishers across three answer colors.\nIzzet plays spell-dense control with counters, red sweepers, burn, and impulse draw.\nHow Do Control Decks Win in Commander?\nControl decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You hold up mana and answer the threats that actually matter.\n✓ You reset crowded boards with sweepers and rebuild through card draw.\n✓ You trade one for one sparingly because three opponents can exhaust your answers.\n✓ You accumulate card advantage until opponents run low on resources.\n✓ You close with a protected finisher planeswalker combo or long game engine.\nBest Cards for Control EDH Decks\nCyclonic Rift\nInstant-speed overloaded bounce that resets opposing boards before a key turn.\nRhystic Study\nTurns opponents' spells into taxes or cards for your answer engine.\nSwords to Plowshares\nOne-mana exile removal that answers many creature threats efficiently.\nSupreme Verdict\nUncounterable sweeper that clears creature boards through countermagic.\nTeferi, Hero of Dominaria\nDraws cards, answers permanents, and supplies a standalone finisher.\nHow to Build a Control Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 6-10 board wipes because one-for-one removal falls behind against three opponents\n✓Include 8-12 flexible answers split between counterspells and spot removal like Swords to Plowshares\n✓Favor repeatable draw engines such as Rhystic Study or Mystic Remora over one-shot draw\n✓Play 36-38 lands plus ramp so you can hold up interaction and still develop\nWin Conditions\n✓Pick 2-3 compact finishers that win from a stabilized board\n✓Use commanders that draw cards or answer threats as accessible win conditions\n✓Some lists close with a two-card combo after opposing interaction is spent\n✓Treat repeatable card advantage as a finisher when it keeps producing resources\nMultiplayer Adjustments\n✓Prioritize answers that hit multiple players, especially sweepers over narrow spot removal\n✓Save counterspells for game-ending threats because three opponents can overload your hand\n✓Let opponents trade resources early, then stabilize once the board is committed\n✓Include recursion or flash threats so unused mana still becomes progress\nWeaknesses of Control Decks\n⚠One-for-one answers lose value against three opponents, so control can run out of interaction.\n⚠Slow clocks invite the table to focus you once you become the obstacle to their plans.\n⚠Control can struggle against decks that rebuild quickly from sweepers, such as tokens and graveyard engines.\n⚠Long games and repeated answers can create social pressure at casual tables.\nIs Control Right for You?\nControl rewards patience, threat assessment, and knowledge of common Commander win attempts, so its skill floor is higher than most strategies. Budget builds are possible because many sweepers and removal spells are cheap, though premium counters and draw engines add up. It suits players who enjoy reacting, reading the table, and winning after the game slows down.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nCard Draw\nControl draws to keep answering; Card Draw makes drawing the main engine.\nBlink\nBlink reuses enter triggers for control effects instead of relying on instants.\nMill\nMill also grinds long games, but it attacks libraries as the win condition.\nSpellslinger\nSpellslinger turns spells into proactive triggers; Control spends spells as answers.\nCombo\nCombo builds around pieces first; Control may use combo after stabilizing.\nStax\nControl answers threats directly; Stax taxes resources to stop actions.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Control CommandersWheelsTheft\nTop Control Commanders\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nControlSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$5.35TCGPlayer\n€2.75CardMarket\nAlela, Cunning Conqueror\nMV: 4\nFaeriesControlFlying+7 more\n$13.20TCGPlayer\n€9.66CardMarket\nHylda of the Icy Crown\nMV: 4\nControlTap / UntapSnow+7 more\n$3.55TCGPlayer\n€1.96CardMarket\nMs. Bumbleflower\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersGroup HugWheels+7 more\n$6.54TCGPlayer\n€15.35CardMarket\nShorikai, Genesis Engine\nMV: 4\nVehiclesArtifactsControl+7 more\n$2.86TCGPlayer\n€2.66CardMarket\nKefka, Court Mage // Kefka, Ruler of Ruin\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nDiscardComboControl+7 more\n$6.86TCGPlayer\n€6.13CardMarket\nEluge, the Shoreless Sea\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerControlExtra Turns+7 more\n$4.99TCGPlayer\n€3.57CardMarket\nZur the Enchanter\nMV: 4\nEnchantressAurasStax+7 more\n$1.55TCGPlayer\n€0.71CardMarket\nQueen Marchesa\nMV: 4\nMonarchAikidoForced Combat+7 more\n$2.40TCGPlayer\n€0.89CardMarket\nTalion, the Kindly Lord\nMV: 4\nControlFaeriesClones+7 more\n$8.93TCGPlayer\n€10.52CardMarket\nOloro, Ageless Ascetic\nMV: 6\nLifegainControlCombo+7 more\n$18.51TCGPlayer\n€11.21CardMarket\nUrza, Lord High Artificer\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$16.28TCGPlayer\n€14.42CardMarket\nGrand Arbiter Augustin IV\nMV: 4\nStaxControlHatebears+7 more\n$19.85TCGPlayer\n€10.90CardMarket\nBrago, King Eternal\nMV: 4\nBlinkControlCombo+7 more\n$3.13TCGPlayer\n€2.23CardMarket\nTivit, Seller of Secrets\nMV: 6\nPoliticsArtifactsCombo+7 more\n$8.86TCGPlayer\n€5.89CardMarket\nVren, the Relentless\nMV: 4\nRatsRat ColonyTokens+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.41CardMarket\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nMV: 6\nSpellslingerWheelsCombo+7 more\n$5.27TCGPlayer\n€2.87CardMarket\nSen Triplets\nMV: 5\nTheftControlStax+7 more\n$8.58TCGPlayer\n€3.30CardMarket\nEriette of the Charmed Apple\nMV: 3\nAurasEnchantressLifegain+7 more\n$1.20TCGPlayer\n€1.73CardMarket\nYuriko, the Tiger's Shadow\nMV: 3\nNinjutsuNinjasTempo+7 more\n$2.95TCGPlayer\n€2.80CardMarket\nNicol Bolas, the Ravager // Nicol Bolas, the Arisen\nMV: 4\nPlaneswalkersDiscardControl+7 more\n$17.48TCGPlayer\n€14.47CardMarket\nGlarb, Calamity's Augur\nMV: 3\nLands MatterFrogsReanimator+7 more\n$3.82TCGPlayer\n€4.07CardMarket\nLord of the Nazgûl\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerCantripsTokens+7 more\n$4.07TCGPlayer\n€4.03CardMarket\nAtraxa, Grand Unifier\nMV: 7\nBlinkComboInfect+7 more\n$18.36TCGPlayer\n€20.82CardMarket\nBaral, Chief of Compliance\nMV: 2\nCounterspellsControlSpellslinger+7 more\n$3.64TCGPlayer\n€1.66CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Control commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Control commander?\nThe best control commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you hold up mana and answer the threats that actually matter.\nHow do Control decks win?\nControl decks win through a combination of Cyclonic Rift, Rhystic Study, Swords to Plowshares, and other synergistic pieces that control decks are for players who enjoy deciding which threats matter, spending answers carefully, and winning after the table has slowed down.\nWhat are the best colors for Control?\nAzorius combines blue counters and draw with white sweepers, removal, and taxes. However, Dimir trades white sweepers for black removal, discard, tutors, and graveyard value.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Control decks?\nOne-for-one answers lose value against three opponents, so control can run out of interaction. Slow clocks invite the table to focus you once you become the obstacle to their plans. Control can struggle against decks that rebuild quickly from sweepers, such as tokens and graveyard engines. Long games and repeated answers can create social pressure at casual tables.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,counters,page_text,11648,"Best +1/+1 Counters Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\n+1/+1 Counters\nBack to SearchBest +1/+1 Counters Commanders for EDH\n+1/+1 counter decks are for players who want small creature upgrades to become permanent pressure across the board. This strategy wants to grow creatures over several turns, using +1/+1 counter commanders, counter doublers, and counter payoffs, to win through combat damage from creatures that keep getting larger. Unlike Tokens, Counters grows creatures over time instead of making many bodies, and unlike Voltron it spreads power beyond one attacker; unlike Proliferate, it focuses on +1/+1 growth rather than every counter type, while Infect is a separate poison plan. Counters matter decks reward pilots who sequence early creatures, multipliers, and protection so each trigger converts into lasting board presence.\nBest Colors for +1/+1 Counters Decks\nSelesnya pairs green counter doublers with white team growth, protection, and combat support.\nSimic combines evolve, adapt, proliferate, and card draw so creature growth keeps refilling the hand.\nAbzan adds black removal and recursion to white-green counters for longer midrange games.\nHow Do +1/+1 Counters Decks Win in Commander?\n+1/+1 Counters decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You play creatures that enter with counters or grow when counters are placed.\n✓ You add multipliers like Hardened Scales so each trigger produces extra growth.\n✓ You spread counters across several bodies to avoid relying on one attacker.\n✓ You convert large creatures into cards mana life or combat pressure.\n✓ You protect engines and preserve counters when removal clears the board.\n✓ You close by attacking with creatures that have grown beyond profitable blocks.\nBest Cards for +1/+1 Counters EDH Decks\nHardened Scales\nAdds one extra +1/+1 counter to each placement, making small triggers scale.\nBranching Evolution\nDoubles +1/+1 counter placement so each growth trigger changes combat math quickly.\nThe Ozolith\nStores counters from dying creatures and moves them back onto the next threat.\nCathars' Crusade\nPlaces counters across the board whenever creatures enter, scaling especially well with tokens.\nInspiring Call\nDraws from countered creatures while giving them instant-speed protection from many sweepers.\nHow to Build a +1/+1 Counters Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 3-5 counter multipliers such as Hardened Scales, Branching Evolution, or Conclave Mentor\n✓Include 15-20 creatures that enter with counters or trigger when counters are placed\n✓Add 4-6 proliferate effects like Evolution Sage or Inexorable Tide as growth accelerators\n✓Play 6-8 draw payoffs such as Inspiring Call or Rishkar's Expertise for large creatures\n✓Keep 8-10 ramp pieces so threats and activation costs fit into the same turns\nWin Conditions\n✓Attack with a tall board and trample so counters translate into actual damage\n✓Use Simic Ascendancy or Herd Baloth style payoffs as alternate routes\n✓Cast overrun effects like Overwhelming Stampede that scale with your largest creature\n✓Add flying, menace, fight effects, or removal so large creatures do not stall on blockers\nProtecting the Engine\n✓Run The Ozolith and Nesting Grounds so counters can survive creature removal\n✓Include 3-5 protection spells like Heroic Intervention for board wipes\n✓Avoid putting every counter on one creature when opponents have exile removal\n✓Use enchantment and artifact growth engines because they dodge many creature sweepers\nWeaknesses of +1/+1 Counters Decks\n⚠Counters decks can struggle against board wipes because most counters leave with the creatures.\n⚠The strategy needs setup turns, so fast combo or aggressive starts can move faster than its growth.\n⚠Targeted removal on counter doublers, draw payoffs, or protection engines can slow the deck sharply.\n⚠Large creatures without trample, evasion, or removal support can be held off by small blockers.\nIs +1/+1 Counters Right for You?\n+1/+1 counters is approachable because the progress is visible and many core cards are inexpensive. The main skill is deciding when to spread counters across the board and when to stack one creature for pressure. It suits players who enjoy incremental growth, combat math, and engines that convert repeated triggers into permanent size.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nLifegain\nLifegain can place counters, but its engine starts with life gained.\nTokens\nTokens makes many bodies; Counters grows existing creatures over time.\nVoltron\nVoltron builds one threat; Counters distributes lasting growth across multiple creatures.\nProliferate\nProliferate multiplies any counter type; Counters focuses on +1/+1 growth.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop +1/+1 Counters CommandersProliferateFlying\nTop +1/+1 Counters Commanders\nThe Wise Mothman\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nMill+1/+1 CountersMutants+7 more\n$11.98TCGPlayer\n€9.71CardMarket\nMs. Bumbleflower\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersGroup HugWheels+7 more\n$6.54TCGPlayer\n€15.35CardMarket\nHakbal of the Surging Soul\nMV: 4\nMerfolk+1/+1 CountersExplore+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€1.44CardMarket\nAtraxa, Praetors' Voice\nMV: 4\nInfectPlaneswalkers+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$27.86TCGPlayer\n€21.67CardMarket\nShalai and Hallar\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersBurnCombo+7 more\n$6.63TCGPlayer\n€4.35CardMarket\nTidus, Yuna's Guardian\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\n+1/+1 CountersProliferateCounters Matter+7 more\n$2.75TCGPlayer\n€1.63CardMarket\nHelga, Skittish Seer\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersRampBig Mana+7 more\n$4.42TCGPlayer\n€4.09CardMarket\nMagus Lucea Kane\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nX Spells+1/+1 CountersTyranids+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAnimar, Soul of Elements\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersComboBirthing Pod+7 more\n$31.28TCGPlayer\n€27.39CardMarket\nGiada, Font of Hope\nMV: 2\nAngelsLifegain+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.96TCGPlayer\n€0.53CardMarket\nSkullbriar, the Walking Grave\nMV: 2\n+1/+1 CountersVoltronAggro+7 more\n$2.13TCGPlayer\n€0.47CardMarket\nMarchesa, the Black Rose\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersAristocratsReanimator+7 more\n$3.19TCGPlayer\n€1.23CardMarket\nEzuri, Claw of Progress\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersInfectElves+7 more\n$2.37TCGPlayer\n€1.33CardMarket\nBetor, Ancestor's Voice\nMV: 5\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersReanimator+7 more\n$3.47TCGPlayer\n€2.52CardMarket\nZaxara, the Exemplary\nMV: 4\nX Spells+1/+1 CountersHydras+7 more\n$1.98TCGPlayer\n€1.32CardMarket\nRocco, Street Chef\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersExileTokens+7 more\n$0.41TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nGargos, Vicious Watcher\nMV: 6\n+1/+1 CountersHydrasX Spells+7 more\n$1.36TCGPlayer\n€1.29CardMarket\nToph, the First Metalbender\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nLands MatterArtifacts+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.28TCGPlayer\n€0.75CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nGaladriel, Light of Valinor\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nElves+1/+1 CountersTokens+7 more\n$27.13TCGPlayer\n€23.87CardMarket\nVoja, Jaws of the Conclave\nMV: 5\nElves+1/+1 CountersAggro+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nBristly Bill, Spine Sower\nMV: 2\n+1/+1 CountersLands MatterLandfall+7 more\n$36.61TCGPlayer\n€25.49CardMarket\nChishiro, the Shattered Blade\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersEquipmentAuras+7 more\n$0.93TCGPlayer\n€0.30CardMarket\nHamza, Guardian of Arashin\nMV: 6\n+1/+1 CountersAggroElephants+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nYuna, Grand Summoner\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\n+1/+1 CountersComboEnchantress+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nFilter and browse all +1/+1 Counters commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good +1/+1 Counters commander?\nThe best +1/+1 counters commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you play creatures that enter with counters or grow when counters are placed.\nHow do +1/+1 Counters decks win?\n+1/+1 Counters decks win through a combination of Hardened Scales, Branching Evolution, The Ozolith, and other synergistic pieces that +1/+1 counter decks are for players who want small creature upgrades to become permanent pressure across the board.\nWhat are the best colors for +1/+1 Counters?\nSelesnya pairs green counter doublers with white team growth, protection, and combat support. However, Simic combines evolve, adapt, proliferate, and card draw so creature growth keeps refilling the hand.\nWhat are the weaknesses of +1/+1 Counters decks?\nCounters decks can struggle against board wipes because most counters leave with the creatures. The strategy needs setup turns, so fast combo or aggressive starts can move faster than its growth. Targeted removal on counter doublers, draw payoffs, or protection engines can slow the deck sharply. Large creatures without trample, evasion, or removal support can be held off by small blockers.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademark