Jund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nEquipment\nBack to SearchBest Equipment Commanders for EDH\nEquipment decks are for players who want the gear itself to be the engine, turning ordinary creatures into credible attackers. This strategy wants to assemble weapons, tutors, and equip-cost shortcuts, using equipment commanders that find or attach gear, to win through upgraded combat, commander damage, or a team carrying shared tools. Unlike Voltron, Equipment does not have to put every card on one creature, and unlike Artifacts it focuses on weapons rather than every artifact type. The best equipment decks EDH players build recover well after creature sweepers because the gear usually stays on the battlefield.\nBest Colors for Equipment Decks\nMono-white supplies Sram, Kemba, tutors, cost reducers, and protection for steady equipment engines.\nBoros adds haste, extra combats, and red pressure to white tutors and protection.\nMono-red uses Godo, Treasure, and extra combats to pay equip costs and end games quickly.\nHow Do Equipment Decks Win in Commander?\nEquipment decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy creatures that carry gear well and still matter after removal.\n✓ You tutor or draw equipment so the right tool appears on time.\n✓ You reduce equip costs or attach gear for free to keep mana open.\n✓ You move weapons between carriers as combat and removal change the board.\n✓ You win through commander damage one upgraded threat or a spread-out team.\nBest Cards for Equipment EDH Decks\nStoneforge Mystic\nFinds the right equipment and can put it onto the battlefield at instant speed.\nPuresteel Paladin\nDraws from equipment and turns metalcraft into free equip costs.\nSigarda's Aid\nLets equipment flash in and attach for free during combat.\nColossus Hammer\nAdds huge power when free-attach effects remove the equip burden.\nSword of Feast and Famine\nConnects for discard and land untaps, converting combat into mana and disruption.\nHow to Build a Equipment Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 10-14 equipment, split between cheap value pieces and finishers like Colossus Hammer or the swords\n✓Include tutors such as Stoneforge Mystic, Steelshaper's Gift, and Open the Armory\n✓Use cost reducers and free-attach cards like Puresteel Paladin, Sigarda's Aid, and Brass Squire\n✓Choose carriers with evasion, double strike, protection, or attack triggers\nDeck Structure\n✓Play 34-36 lands plus mana rocks because casting and equipping both tax your mana\n✓Run 12-18 creatures that either carry gear well or reward equipment entering and attaching\n✓Include 6-10 protection spells for the creature carrying your best gear\n✓Add draw tied to equipment, such as Sram, Senior Edificer or Puresteel Paladin\nWin Conditions\n✓Deal 21 commander damage when one carrier is safest and fastest\n✓Spread weapons across tokens or small creatures to pressure multiple opponents\n✓Use Sword triggers and extra combats to convert attacks into mana, cards, and disruption\nWeaknesses of Equipment Decks\n⚠Artifact removal and mass artifact wipes can remove the gear that makes the deck function.\n⚠Equip costs are mana intensive, especially after your best carrier dies.\n⚠Equipment without creatures can leave you with artifacts that do not affect combat.\n⚠Leaning on one carrier exposes you to the same removal tax Voltron decks face.\nIs Equipment Right for You?\nEquipment is approachable because the main decisions happen in combat and your gear often survives creature wipes. It suits players who like choosing the right tool, building one attacker, or spreading upgrades across a team. Budget lists work without premium swords, but the deck needs enough mana to cast and move equipment.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nVoltron\nVoltron builds one lethal creature; Equipment is the reusable gear engine that may spread out.\nAggro\nAggro floods attackers early; Equipment upgrades fewer bodies and keeps gear after sweepers.\nArtifacts\nArtifacts cares about every machine; Equipment focuses on weapons and their carriers.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Equipment CommandersAurasExtra Combats\nTop Equipment Commanders\nCaptain America, First Avenger\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$31.03TCGPlayer\n€16.64CardMarket\nCloud, Ex-SOLDIER\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$1.44TCGPlayer\n€0.73CardMarket\nSyr Gwyn, Hero of Ashvale\nMV: 6\nEquipmentKnightsVoltron+7 more\n$2.88TCGPlayer\n€3.10CardMarket\nNahiri, Forged in Fury\nMV: 6\nEquipmentAggroArtifacts+7 more\n$7.83TCGPlayer\n€7.72CardMarket\nArna Kennerüd, Skycaptain\nMV: 5\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.42CardMarket\nDogmeat, Ever Loyal\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAlexios, Deimos of Kosmos\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$0.58TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nMabel, Heir to Cragflame\nMV: 3\nEquipmentMiceVoltron+7 more\n$0.29TCGPlayer\n€0.35CardMarket\nGalea, Kindler of Hope\nMV: 4\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nBruenor Battlehammer\nMV: 4\nEquipmentVoltronDwarves+7 more\n$1.90TCGPlayer\n€3.07CardMarket\nSlicer, Hired Muscle // Slicer, High-Speed Antagonist\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$7.70TCGPlayer\n€5.59CardMarket\nBalan, Wandering Knight\nMV: 4\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.47CardMarket\nLightning, Army of One\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronExtra Combats+7 more\n$9.59TCGPlayer\n€8.55CardMarket\nChishiro, the Shattered Blade\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersEquipmentAuras+7 more\n$0.93TCGPlayer\n€0.30CardMarket\nZurgo Helmsmasher\nMV: 5\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$0.53TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nTetsuo, Imperial Champion\nMV: 3\nEquipmentVoltronSpellslinger+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.73CardMarket\nWyleth, Soul of Steel\nMV: 3\nEquipmentVoltronAuras+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nRatonhnhaké꞉ton\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentAssassinsVoltron+7 more\n$0.30TCGPlayer\n€0.42CardMarket\nKellan, the Fae-Blooded // Birthright Boon\nMV: 3\nEquipmentVoltronSunforger+7 more\n$6.17TCGPlayer\n€5.38CardMarket\nKassandra, Eagle Bearer\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronLegends+7 more\n$2.30TCGPlayer\n€3.32CardMarket\nCloud, Midgar Mercenary\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$21.14TCGPlayer\n€22.67CardMarket\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One\nMV: 3\nAggroTokensAttack Triggers+7 more\n$3.76TCGPlayer\n€2.99CardMarket\nEivor, Battle-Ready\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nJin Sakai, Ghost of Tsushima\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$101.01TCGPlayer\n€89.25CardMarket\nValduk, Keeper of the Flame\nMV: 3\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$0.20TCGPlayer\n€0.07CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Equipment commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Equipment commander?\nThe best equipment commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy creatures that carry gear well and still matter after removal.\nHow do Equipment decks win?\nEquipment decks win through a combination of Stoneforge Mystic, Puresteel Paladin, Sigarda's Aid, and other synergistic pieces that equipment decks are for players who want the gear itself to be the engine, turning ordinary creatures into credible attackers.\nWhat are the best colors for Equipment?\nMono-white supplies Sram, Kemba, tutors, cost reducers, and protection for steady equipment engines. However, Boros adds haste, extra combats, and red pressure to white tutors and protection.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Equipment decks?\nArtifact removal and mass artifact wipes can remove the gear that makes the deck function. Equip costs are mana intensive, especially after your best carrier dies. Equipment without creatures can leave you with artifacts that do not affect combat. Leaning on one carrier exposes you to the same removal tax Voltron decks face.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,graveyard,page_text,11327,"Best Graveyard Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nGraveyard\nBack to SearchBest Graveyard Commanders for EDH\nGraveyard decks are for players who want every discarded, milled, or destroyed card to remain part of the game plan. This strategy wants to stock the graveyard, using self-mill, discard outlets, sacrifice, and dredge-style cards, to win by recurring threats, lands, and value pieces until opponents run out of clean answers. Unlike Reanimator, graveyard value decks EDH players build are not centered on one huge creature arriving early, and unlike Mill they use self-mill as fuel rather than treating libraries as the main target. The best graveyard commanders turn the graveyard into a steady resource engine that can rebuild after removal.\nBest Colors for Graveyard Decks\nGolgari combines black recursion and sacrifice with green self-mill, regrowth, and creature value.\nSultai adds blue card selection and self-mill to Golgari recursion for broader long-game engines.\nDimir uses blue-black mill, discard, and interaction to play a slower graveyard control game.\nAbzan adds white mass recursion, protection, and resilient boards to black-green graveyard engines.\nHow Do Graveyard Decks Win in Commander?\nGraveyard decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You treat the graveyard as a resource engine instead of a discard pile.\n✓ You stock it with self-mill discard outlets sacrifice effects and dredge cards.\n✓ You replay creatures lands and spells so removal becomes a delay rather than an ending.\n✓ You convert recursion into cards mana drain or repeatable board control.\n✓ You rebuild through sweepers by returning the pieces opponents already answered.\n✓ You close by grinding the table out or looping a recursion payoff.\nBest Cards for Graveyard EDH Decks\nEternal Witness\nReturns any card from the graveyard and becomes repeatable value when recurred.\nLife from the Loam\nDredges cards while returning lands, keeping both mana and graveyard resources moving.\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide\nLets you play several permanent types from the graveyard each turn as a built-in engine.\nSyr Konrad, the Grim\nConverts creature deaths and graveyard movement into drain across the table.\nGenesis\nSits in the graveyard and turns each upkeep into another creature returned.\nHow to Build a Graveyard Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-10 graveyard fillers such as Stitcher's Supplier, Fauna Shaman, and dredge cards\n✓Include 8-12 recursion pieces across creatures, lands, and spells so different resources return\n✓Add payoffs that reward cards entering or leaving graveyards, such as Syr Konrad, the Grim\n✓Pack sacrifice outlets so your creatures reach the graveyard on your schedule\nDeck Structure\n✓Favor creatures with enter or death triggers because each recursion loop reuses their text\n✓Choose a commander that recurs cards itself, like Meren of Clan Nel Toth or Muldrotha, the Gravetide\n✓Keep some ramp and interaction in hand because not every answer should wait in the graveyard\n✓Include one or two backup engines in case your main loop is exiled\nWin Conditions\n✓Drain the table with recursion payoffs like Syr Konrad or aristocrats-style bleeders\n✓Grind opponents out of answers, then attack with threats that keep returning\n✓Loop value creatures with a sacrifice outlet and recursion piece until the payoff ends the game\nWeaknesses of Graveyard Decks\n⚠Dedicated graveyard hate like Rest in Peace or Bojuka Bog can shut off the engine at once.\n⚠Exile removal permanently answers threats and engines that would otherwise keep coming back.\n⚠The deck can spend early turns setting up while faster strategies build pressure or combo pieces.\n⚠Recursive lines are visible in the graveyard, so opponents often know which piece to answer.\nIs Graveyard Right for You?\nGraveyard value suits players who like patient games where resources are spent, returned, and spent again. The skill floor is moderate because you need to track the graveyard and sequence recursion without walking into hate. Budget builds work well because many self-mill cards and value creatures are inexpensive.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nAristocrats\nBoth use deaths, but Aristocrats wins from triggers while Graveyard reuses the cards afterward.\nSacrifice\nSacrifice puts cards in the graveyard; Graveyard decks make recurring them the engine.\nReanimator\nReanimator cheats large threats into play; Graveyard grinds many resources over time.\nMill\nMill weaponizes libraries; Graveyard uses self-mill to stock its own recursion engine.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Graveyard CommandersSelf-MillDiscard\nTop Graveyard Commanders\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nDisa the Restless\nMV: 5\nGraveyardMillReanimator+7 more\n$0.14TCGPlayer\n€0.25CardMarket\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide\nMV: 6\nReanimatorMillSacrifice+7 more\n$0.61TCGPlayer\n€0.81CardMarket\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth\nMV: 4\nAristocratsReanimatorSacrifice+7 more\n$2.38TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nTerra, Herald of Hope\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nReanimatorGraveyardSelf-Mill+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.82CardMarket\nThe Necrobloom\nMV: 4\nLands MatterTokensDredge+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nQuintorius, History Chaser\nMV: 4\nReanimatorTokensGraveyard+7 more\n$0.70TCGPlayer\n€1.03CardMarket\nHearthhull, the Worldseed\nMV: 4\nLands MatterRampLand Destruction+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nCoram, the Undertaker\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.26CardMarket\nImotekh the Stormlord\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nArtifactsReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nJarad, Golgari Lich Lord\nMV: 4\nGraveyardReanimatorMill+7 more\n$8.64TCGPlayer\n€4.06CardMarket\nKarador, Ghost Chieftain\nMV: 8\nReanimatorGraveyardBirthing Pod+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.32CardMarket\nSidisi, Brood Tyrant\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\n$2.30TCGPlayer\n€0.80CardMarket\nThe Mycotyrant\nMV: 3\nMillTokensSelf-Mill+7 more\n$1.18TCGPlayer\n€0.89CardMarket\nGlarb, Calamity's Augur\nMV: 3\nLands MatterFrogsReanimator+7 more\n$3.82TCGPlayer\n€4.07CardMarket\nThe Gitrog Monster\nMV: 5\nLands MatterDredgeCombo+7 more\n$0.45TCGPlayer\n€1.05CardMarket\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver\nMV: 4\nZombiesTokensAristocrats+7 more\n$9.30TCGPlayer\n€10.24CardMarket\nSefris of the Hidden Ways\nMV: 3\nReanimatorDungeonDiscard+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nNorman Osborn // Green Goblin\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nDiscardWheelsCombo+7 more\n$14.09TCGPlayer\n€10.08CardMarket\nGisa and Geralf\nMV: 4\nZombiesReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nSyrix, Carrier of the Flame\nMV: 4\nPhoenixesGraveyardReanimator+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.22CardMarket\nThe Wise Mothman\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nMill+1/+1 CountersMutants+7 more\n$11.98TCGPlayer\n€9.71CardMarket\nGrist, the Hunger Tide\nMV: 3\nInsectsSelf-MillMill+7 more\n$0.47TCGPlayer\n€0.58CardMarket\nThe Mimeoplasm\nMV: 5\nMillReanimator+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.27TCGPlayer\n€0.19CardMarket\nCeles, Rune Knight\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nReanimatorCombo+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.89TCGPlayer\n€0.82CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Graveyard commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Graveyard commander?\nThe best graveyard commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you treat the graveyard as a resource engine instead of a discard pile.\nHow do Graveyard decks win?\nGraveyard decks win through a combination of Eternal Witness, Life from the Loam, Muldrotha, the Gravetide, and other synergistic pieces that graveyard decks are for players who want every discarded, milled, or destroyed card to remain part of the game plan.\nWhat are the best colors for Graveyard?\nGolgari combines black recursion and sacrifice with green self-mill, regrowth, and creature value. However, Sultai adds blue card selection and self-mill to Golgari recursion for broader long-game engines.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Graveyard decks?\nDedicated graveyard hate like Rest in Peace or Bojuka Bog can shut off the engine at once. Exile removal permanently answers threats and engines that would otherwise keep coming back. The deck can spend early turns setting up while faster strategies build pressure or combo pieces. Recursive lines are visible in the graveyard, so opponents often know which piece to answer.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,group-hug,page_text,10960,"Best Group Hug Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nGroup Hug\nBack to SearchBest Group Hug Commanders for EDH\nGroup Hug decks are for players who want table politics to matter as much as board position. This strategy wants to give everyone extra cards, lands, or mana, using group hug commanders and symmetrical resource effects, to win through political leverage, hidden payoffs, or a carefully timed finish. Unlike Lifegain, it does not make your own life total the engine; it gives resources away and tries to profit from who uses them. The best political decks EDH players remember are frank about the risk: your gifts can help the wrong player first.\nBest Colors for Group Hug Decks\nMono-blue supplies Howling Mine effects, Kami of the Crescent Moon, and counterspells for protecting a hidden finish.\nSimic adds group land ramp and draw, then uses green engines to profit from the larger table.\nJeskai gives Zedruu donation lines, red chaos tools, and white protection for political deals.\nFour-color Kynaios and Tiro decks combine gifts, ramp, and interaction for long negotiated games.\nHow Do Group Hug Decks Win in Commander?\nGroup Hug decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You give the table cards lands or mana before asking for anything back.\n✓ You turn those gifts into time by steering attacks and removal elsewhere.\n✓ You break symmetry with payoffs that reward every draw land drop or donation.\n✓ You keep interaction ready for the opponent who uses your gifts too well.\n✓ You win through politics a hidden spell or a late payoff after the table weakens.\nBest Cards for Group Hug EDH Decks\nHowling Mine\nKeeps every hand full, buying goodwill while your payoff plan develops.\nRites of Flourishing\nAdds shared draws and land drops, accelerating the table into a political midgame.\nZedruu the Greathearted\nTurns donated permanents into cards and life while making alliances concrete.\nSmothering Tithe\nConverts opponents' extra draws into Treasure, letting your gifts pay you back.\nApproach of the Second Sun\nRewards deep card draw with a hidden second cast that can end long games.\nHow to Build a Group Hug Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 shared resource effects such as Howling Mine, Temple Bell, and Rites of Flourishing\n✓Include 4-6 symmetry breakers like Smothering Tithe, Consecrated Sphinx, or Zedruu\n✓Use pillowfort pieces such as Propaganda and Ghostly Prison to redirect attacks\n✓Keep instant-speed interaction for combo decks that convert your gifts too quickly\nDeck Structure\n✓Play 36-38 lands because the deck expects longer games and repeated land drops\n✓Lead with small gifts and save big group ramp for moments you can negotiate\n✓Protect or recur your hidden win condition so one counterspell does not erase the plan\n✓Include sweepers for opponents who use your resources to build the fastest board\nWin Conditions\n✓Approach of the Second Sun converts deep draw into a clean two-cast finish\n✓Alternate-win cards reward the huge hands created by repeated draw engines\n✓Zedruu can win by turning donations into steady cards and life over time\n✓Politics matters: guide the midgame so the final opponent is one you can beat\nWeaknesses of Group Hug Decks\n⚠Group Hug accelerates opponents, so combo and stax decks may use your gifts better than you do.\n⚠It often develops little pressure, which can make you easy to remove once trust fades.\n⚠Hidden win conditions draw immediate interaction once opponents understand the plan.\n⚠The deck is political by nature and can be treated as a kingmaker at experienced tables.\nIs Group Hug Right for You?\nGroup Hug is mechanically approachable because many cards simply give resources to everyone. The hard part is politics: you need to know when a gift buys time and when it hands the game away. It suits social players who enjoy deals, shifting incentives, and honest table talk.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nLifegain\nLifegain keeps life as its own engine; Group Hug gives resources away for leverage.\nCard Draw\nCard Draw keeps extra cards private; Group Hug shares cards to shape decisions.\nRamp\nRamp accelerates itself; Group Hug accelerates everyone and negotiates from the fallout.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Group Hug CommandersGroup SlugChaos\nTop Group Hug Commanders\nMs. Bumbleflower\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersGroup HugWheels+7 more\n$6.54TCGPlayer\n€15.35CardMarket\nKynaios and Tiro of Meletis\nMV: 4\nGroup HugWheelsLands Matter+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nGluntch, the Bestower\nMV: 3\nGroup Hug+1/+1 CountersPillow Fort+7 more\n$0.50TCGPlayer\n€0.44CardMarket\nKwain, Itinerant Meddler\nMV: 2\nWheelsGroup HugCard Draw+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.31CardMarket\nKenrith, the Returned King\nMV: 5\nGroup HugComboPolitics+7 more\n$3.03TCGPlayer\n€1.08CardMarket\nXyris, the Writhing Storm\nMV: 5\nWheelsTokensGroup Hug+7 more\n$2.10TCGPlayer\n€0.97CardMarket\nPhelddagrif\nMV: 4\nGroup HugComboPillow Fort+7 more\n$10.08TCGPlayer\n€4.73CardMarket\nZedruu the Greathearted\nMV: 4\nChaosDonateGroup Hug+7 more\n$2.42TCGPlayer\n€1.42CardMarket\nRocco, Street Chef\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersExileTokens+7 more\n$0.41TCGPlayer\n€0.39