p Hug decks win through a combination of Howling Mine, Rites of Flourishing, Zedruu the Greathearted, and other synergistic pieces that group hug decks are for players who want table politics to matter as much as board position.\nWhat are the best colors for Group Hug?\nMono-blue supplies Howling Mine effects, Kami of the Crescent Moon, and counterspells for protecting a hidden finish. However, Simic adds group land ramp and draw, then uses green engines to profit from the larger table.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Group Hug decks?\nGroup Hug accelerates opponents, so combo and stax decks may use your gifts better than you do. It often develops little pressure, which can make you easy to remove once trust fades. Hidden win conditions draw immediate interaction once opponents understand the plan. The deck is political by nature and can be treated as a kingmaker at experienced tables.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,infect,page_text,11185,"Best Infect Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nInfect\nBack to SearchBest Infect Commanders for EDH\nInfect decks are for players who want life totals to matter less than reaching ten poison on each opponent. This strategy wants to connect with infect and toxic creatures, using pump spells, evasion, protection, and infect commanders, to win through poison counter decks EDH opponents have to answer early. In the Counters, Proliferate, and Infect overlap, Counters grows creatures, Proliferate multiplies many counter types, and Infect is the poison win condition that proliferate can accelerate. The social cost is real: once poison appears, the table often treats you as the first threat, and some pods restrict the mechanic before deck selection.\nBest Colors for Infect Decks\nGolgari pairs dense infect creatures with green pump, black removal, and recursion for lost threats.\nSimic adds unblockable effects, card draw, and proliferate engines that advance poison after contact.\nAbzan blends toxic commanders, corrupted payoffs, white protection, black removal, and green pump.\nHow Do Infect Decks Win in Commander?\nInfect decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You attack early with infect and toxic creatures that deal poison instead of regular damage.\n✓ You pump an evasive attacker so ten poison can arrive in one or two hits.\n✓ You use proliferate to move existing poison counters toward lethal without another attack.\n✓ You protect the key swing because one removal spell can erase the turn.\n✓ You plan around the table turning on you as soon as poison appears.\nBest Cards for Infect EDH Decks\nSkithiryx, the Blight Dragon\nFlying, haste, and self-pump let it threaten ten poison with protection.\nBlightsteel Colossus\nAn infect trampler that removes a player with one clean combat hit.\nTriumph of the Hordes\nGives your board infect and trample, converting combat damage into poison.\nInkmoth Nexus\nA land that becomes a flying infect attacker and dodges sorcery-speed sweepers.\nGrafted Exoskeleton\nTurns any creature, including your commander, into a poison threat.\nHow to Build a Infect Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 15-20 infect or toxic creatures, favoring evasion like flying, trample, or unblockable\n✓Include 6-10 cheap pump spells such as Groundswell, Might of Old Krosa, or Berserk\n✓Add 5-8 proliferate effects like Evolution Sage, Tezzeret's Gambit, or Contagion Engine\n✓Pack 6-8 protection spells because poison attackers draw removal immediately\nThe Ten-Poison Plan\n✓Ten poison eliminates a player regardless of life total, so lifegain does not change the clock\n✓One connected hit plus proliferate turns can finish a player without more combat\n✓Multiplayer means three opponents at ten poison each, so spreading counters matters\n✓Inkmoth Nexus and manlands keep threats available after board wipes\nSurviving the Table\n✓Expect focused removal and early attacks once the first poison counter lands\n✓Sequence so you threaten lethal before slowly handing out warning counters\n✓Use Triumph of the Hordes to convert any creature board into a poison finish\n✓Have the rule-zero conversation before game one because some pods house-rule poison\nWeaknesses of Infect Decks\n⚠Infect draws immediate table aggression, and playing one against three from turn two is a real cost of the archetype.\n⚠Dedicated infect creatures have weak stats for their cost, so the deck can struggle when poison stalls.\n⚠The strategy leans on combat, making it vulnerable to fog effects, deathtouch blockers, and repeated board wipes.\n⚠Some playgroups house-rule against poison entirely, so the deck may not be welcome at every table.\nIs Infect Right for You?\nInfect is mechanically simple and can be inexpensive, but it has a higher social cost than most combat decks. It suits players who enjoy racing under pressure and accept that one poison counter can reshape the whole table's threat assessment. Check with your pod first because the rule-zero conversation is part of playing poison.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nProliferate\nProliferate multiplies counters broadly; Infect uses it to advance poison.\nAggro\nAggro deals life-total damage; Infect races each opponent to ten poison.\nVoltron\nVoltron aims for commander damage; Infect needs ten poison per player.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Infect CommandersProliferateExtra Combats\nTop Infect Commanders\nAtraxa, Praetors' Voice\nMV: 4\nInfectPlaneswalkers+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$27.86TCGPlayer\n€21.67CardMarket\nFynn, the Fangbearer\nMV: 2\nDeathtouchInfectAggro+7 more\n$0.20TCGPlayer\n€0.24CardMarket\nIxhel, Scion of Atraxa\nMV: 4\nInfectPhyrexiansProliferate+7 more\n$3.01TCGPlayer\n€1.10CardMarket\nVishgraz, the Doomhive\nMV: 5\nInfectPhyrexiansTokens+7 more\n$1.43TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nSkithiryx, the Blight Dragon\nMV: 5\nInfectVoltronEquipment+7 more\n$13.01TCGPlayer\n€7.79CardMarket\nBrimaz, Blight of Oreskos\nMV: 4\nPhyrexiansInfectTokens+7 more\n$0.46TCGPlayer\n€0.41CardMarket\nKarumonix, the Rat King\nMV: 3\nRatsInfectRat Colony+7 more\n$1.12TCGPlayer\n€0.55CardMarket\nVenser, Corpse Puppet\nMV: 2\nInfectProliferateArtifacts+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.17CardMarket\nAtraxa, Grand Unifier\nMV: 7\nBlinkComboInfect+7 more\n$18.36TCGPlayer\n€20.82CardMarket\nSkrelv, Defector Mite\nMV: 1\nInfectPhyrexiansTokens+7 more\n$3.39TCGPlayer\n€3.57CardMarket\nKilo, Apogee Mind\nMV: 3\nArtifacts+1/+1 CountersProliferate+7 more\n$2.07TCGPlayer\n€0.66CardMarket\nHigh Perfect Morcant\nMV: 4\n-1/-1 CountersElvesTokens+7 more\n$1.20TCGPlayer\n€1.33CardMarket\nAgent Frank Horrigan\nMV: 7Universes Beyond\nInfect+1/+1 CountersProliferate+7 more\n$5.14TCGPlayer\n€7.81CardMarket\nXavier Sal, Infested Captain\nMV: 3\nTokensPlaneswalkers+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$2.79TCGPlayer\n€0.96CardMarket\nRia Ivor, Bane of Bladehold\nMV: 4\nInfectTokensPhyrexians+7 more\n$0.32TCGPlayer\n€0.19CardMarket\nEzuri, Stalker of Spheres\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersInfectProliferate+7 more\n$0.97TCGPlayer\n€0.53CardMarket\nTekuthal, Inquiry Dominus\nMV: 4\nPlaneswalkersInfectProliferate+7 more\n$1.59TCGPlayer\n€1.83CardMarket\nHapatra, Vizier of Poisons\nMV: 2\n-1/-1 CountersTokensInfect+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nTetsuko Umezawa, Fugitive\nMV: 2\nInfectWheelsAggro+7 more\n$0.15TCGPlayer\n€0.16CardMarket\nEzuri, Claw of Progress\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersInfectElves+7 more\n$2.37TCGPlayer\n€1.33CardMarket\nGlissa, Herald of Predation\nMV: 5\nInfectPhyrexians+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.17CardMarket\nJon Irenicus, Shattered One\nMV: 4\nDonateForced CombatStompy+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nToxrill, the Corrosive\nMV: 7\n-1/-1 CountersControlInfect+7 more\n$17.70TCGPlayer\n€15.75CardMarket\nVolrath, the Shapestealer\nMV: 5\n-1/-1 CountersClones+1/+1 Counters+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nEtali, Primal Conqueror // Etali, Primal Sickness\nMV: 7\nClonesComboRamp+7 more\n$30.15TCGPlayer\n€21.25CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Infect commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Infect commander?\nThe best infect commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you attack early with infect and toxic creatures that deal poison instead of regular damage.\nHow do Infect decks win?\nInfect decks win through a combination of Skithiryx, the Blight Dragon, Blightsteel Colossus, Triumph of the Hordes, and other synergistic pieces that infect decks are for players who want life totals to matter less than reaching ten poison on each opponent.\nWhat are the best colors for Infect?\nGolgari pairs dense infect creatures with green pump, black removal, and recursion for lost threats. However, Simic adds unblockable effects, card draw, and proliferate engines that advance poison after contact.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Infect decks?\nInfect draws immediate table aggression, and playing one against three from turn two is a real cost of the archetype. Dedicated infect creatures have weak stats for their cost, so the deck can struggle when poison stalls. The strategy leans on combat, making it vulnerable to fog effects, deathtouch blockers, and repeated board wipes. Some playgroups house-rule against poison entirely, so the deck may not be welcome at every table.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,landfall,page_text,10914,"Best Landfall Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nLandfall\nBack to SearchBest Landfall Commanders for EDH\nLandfall decks are for players who want every land drop to become a trigger, not just another mana source. This strategy wants to put lands onto the battlefield repeatedly, using fetch lands, extra land drops, land recursion, and landfall commanders, to win through tokens, counters, damage, cards, or other lands matter decks EDH payoffs. In the Ramp, Landfall, and Big Mana cluster, Ramp accelerates mana, Big Mana spends that mana on huge spells, and Landfall treats the land entering as the engine itself. The deck feels like turning the most reliable game action into a chain of visible rewards.\nBest Colors for Landfall Decks\nMono-green supplies extra land drops, land recursion, ramp creatures, and Avenger of Zendikar-style payoffs.\nGruul converts land drops into damage, hasty Elementals, extra combats, and pressure from Omnath-style triggers.\nSimic pairs extra land drops with card draw, letting lands replace themselves and refuel the engine.\nSelesnya adds token payoffs, counters, protection, and recursion engines such as Emeria Shepherd.\nHow Do Landfall Decks Win in Commander?\nLandfall decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You play lands as engine pieces and turn each land entering into a trigger.\n✓ You use fetch lands and sacrifice lands so one land slot creates multiple triggers.\n✓ You stack extra land drop effects to fire payoffs several times in one turn.\n✓ You recur lands from the graveyard to keep the engine supplied.\n✓ You convert repeated triggers into tokens counters cards damage or extra combats.\nBest Cards for Landfall EDH Decks\nLotus Cobra\nConverts each land entering into mana, letting fetch lands extend the turn.\nScute Swarm\nCopies itself from repeated landfall triggers, building a wide token board.\nAvenger of Zendikar\nMakes Plants from your lands, then grows them with each landfall trigger.\nAzusa, Lost but Seeking\nAdds two land drops each turn, multiplying triggers and mana development.\nRamunap Excavator\nReplays lands from your graveyard so fetches and sacrifice lands keep triggering landfall.\nHow to Build a Landfall Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-10 landfall payoffs across tokens, counters, damage, and draw so triggers become real resources\n✓Include 4-6 extra land drop effects like Exploration, Azusa, or Wayward Swordtooth\n✓Add fetch effects, including budget options like Evolving Wilds, to double triggers in a turn\n✓Slot 2-3 land recursion pieces such as Ramunap Excavator or Crucible of Worlds\nDeck Structure\n✓Push land counts to 40 or more because extra land drops need a steady supply\n✓Prioritize card draw tied to lands, such as Tireless Tracker or Tatyova, to keep drops flowing\n✓Keep 5-7 interaction slots because the engine needs time to assemble\nWin Conditions\n✓Go wide with Scute Swarm and Avenger of Zendikar, then attack with a grown board\n✓Use Valakut Exploration or Omnath, Locus of Rage to convert landfall into damage\n✓Use Moraug, Fury of Akoum to turn land drops into extra combat steps\nWeaknesses of Landfall Decks\n⚠Board wipes can erase a token board built over several turns, forcing the engine to rebuild.\n⚠The deck can struggle when it misses land drops because payoffs need lands entering play.\n⚠Key engine pieces like Lotus Cobra and Azusa draw removal early, and the deck slows without them.\n⚠Mass land destruction or effects that punish extra lands can set the strategy back more than most decks.\nIs Landfall Right for You?\nLandfall is friendly to newer players because the core loop is natural: play a land and get a reward. Budget versions work well with Evolving Wilds, Rampaging Baloths, and basic ramp, while premium fetch lands mainly raise consistency. It suits players who enjoy incremental engines that visibly build from turn to turn.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nRamp\nRamp plays lands for acceleration; Landfall plays them for the triggers.\nBig Mana\nBig Mana spends the mana; Landfall wins from land drops entering.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Landfall CommandersLands MatterRamp\nTop Landfall Commanders\nOmnath, Locus of Creation\nMV: 4\nLandfallLands MatterRamp+7 more\n$4.94TCGPlayer\n€3.36CardMarket\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait\nMV: 6\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$12.07TCGPlayer\n€13.93CardMarket\nThe Necrobloom\nMV: 4\nLands MatterTokensDredge+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nFlubs, the Fool\nMV: 3\nLands MatterCheeriosLandfall+7 more\n$2.89TCGPlayer\n€0.61CardMarket\nHearthhull, the Worldseed\nMV: 4\nLands MatterRampLand Destruction+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of Rage\nMV: 7\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nYarok, the Desecrated\nMV: 5\nLands MatterLandfallETB+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nThalia and The Gitrog Monster\nMV: 4\nLands MatterLandfallHatebears+7 more\n$6.02TCGPlayer\n€3.19CardMarket\nTatyova, Benthic Druid\nMV: 5\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$0.29TCGPlayer\n€0.14CardMarket\nZimone, Mystery Unraveler\nMV: 4\nLands MatterMorphLandfall+7 more\n$0.51TCGPlayer\n€0.63CardMarket\nToph, the First Metalbender\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nLands MatterArtifacts+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.28TCGPlayer\n€0.75CardMarket\nLord Windgrace\nMV: 5\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nZimone and Dina\nMV: 3\nLands MatterLandfallAristocrats+7 more\n$0.51TCGPlayer\n€0.46CardMarket\nTifa Lockhart\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nLands MatterVoltronRamp+7 more\n$2.51TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nObuun, Mul Daya Ancestor\nMV: 4\nLandfallLands Matter+1/+1 Counters+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKarametra, God of Harvests\nMV: 5\nLandfallLands MatterRamp+7 more\n$5.16TCGPlayer\n€2.86CardMarket\nChoco, Seeker of Paradise\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nLands MatterBirdsFlying+7 more\n$0.51TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nAzusa, Lost but Seeking\nMV: 3\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$13.07TCGPlayer\n€8.80CardMarket\nSméagol, Helpful Guide\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nLands MatterLandfallSacrifice+7 more\n$0.38TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nTannuk, Memorial Ensign\nMV: 3\nLands MatterBurnLandfall+7 more\n$0.95TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nBristly Bill, Spine Sower\nMV: 2\n+1/+1 CountersLands MatterLandfall+7 more\n$36.61TCGPlayer\n€25.49CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of the Roil\nMV: 4\nLands MatterElementalsLandfall+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.38CardMarket\nBonny Pall, Clearcutter\nMV: 6\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$0.38TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nThe Wandering Minstrel\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nLands MatterRampCombo+7 more\n$0.32TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Landfall commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Landfall commander?\nThe best landfall commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you play lands as engine pieces and turn each land entering into a trigger.\nHow do Landfall decks win?\nLandfall decks win through a combination of Lotus Cobra, Scute Swarm, Avenger of Zendikar, and other synergistic pieces that landfall decks are for players who want every land drop to become a trigger, not just another mana source.\nWhat are the best colors for Landfall?\nMono-green supplies extra land drops, land recursion, ramp creatures, and Avenger of Zendikar-style payoffs. However, Gruul converts land drops into damage, hasty Elementals, extra combats, and pressure from Omnath-style triggers.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Landfall decks?\nBoard wipes can erase a token board built over several turns, forcing the engine to rebuild. The deck can struggle when it misses land drops because payoffs need lands entering play. Key engine pieces like Lotus Cobra and Azusa draw removal early, and the deck slows without them. Mass land destruction or effects that punish extra lands can set the strategy back more than most decks.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,lifegain,page_text,11294,"Best Lifegain Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nLifegain\nBack to SearchBest Lifegain Commanders for EDH\nLifegain decks are for players who want each point of life to become a resource, not just a larger cushion against attacks. This strategy wants to gain life in steady repeatable triggers, using lifegain commanders, Soul Warden effects, and payoff cards, to win through life drain, oversized creatures, or a charged Aetherflux Reservoir. Unlike Group Hug, it keeps the resources for itself, and unlike Aristocrats it is driven by life gained rather than death triggers. The best lifegain decks EDH players build feel patient early, then convert a safe life total into pressure.\nBest Colors for Lifegain Decks\nMono-white supplies Soul Warden effects, Pridemate payoffs, protection, and steady board rebuilding.\nOrzhov pairs white lifegain with black drain, converting each trigger into pressure.\nSelesnya turns life into board presence with +1/+1 counters, tokens, and creature-based engines.\nAbzan combines lifegain, sacrifice-drain loops, and counters for a flexible midrange engine.\nHow Do Lifegain Decks Win in Commander?\nLifegain decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You gain life in small repeatable increments from creatures spells and combat.\n✓ You deploy payoffs that convert each lifegain trigger into counters cards or drain damage.\n✓ You use the life buffer to play patiently while opponents spend resources elsewhere.\n✓ You grow threats or drain the table as the same triggers repeat each turn.\n✓ You close with a drain loop an oversized board or an Aetherflux Reservoir shot.\nBest Cards for Lifegain EDH Decks\nSoul Warden\nOne-mana trigger engine that gains life whenever any creature enters the battlefield.\nAjani's Pridemate\nClassic payoff that converts repeated lifegain triggers into a steadily growing attacker.\nAetherflux Reservoir\nStores life from spell chains, then converts fifty life into direct damage.\nHeliod, Sun-Crowned\nTurns each lifegain trigger into a +1/+1 counter for creature growth.\nVito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose\nConverts your life gained into life drain against each opponent.\nHow to Build a Lifegain Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 repeatable lifegain sources, especially Soul Warden effects that trigger several times each turn\n✓Include 6-10 payoffs that convert life into counters, cards, tokens, or drain damage\n✓Use lifelink creatures as early blockers that still feed the engine\n✓Add draw tied to lifegain or life totals, such as Well of Lost Dreams\nDeck Structure\n✓Balance lifegain sources with payoffs so the deck does more than raise its life total\n✓Include 10-12 ramp pieces and mana sources to deploy an engine and payoff together\n✓Run 6-8 removal or sweeper slots because a life buffer does not answer combo or commander damage\n✓Add redundant Pridemate, Vito, and Heliod effects before adding more plain lifegain\nWin Conditions\n✓Pair Vito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose or Sanguine Bond with steady lifegain for drain wins\n✓Grow payoff creatures large enough to pressure players through combat\n✓Use Aetherflux Reservoir once your life total can absorb the payment\n✓Consider Felidar Sovereign effects only in builds that can protect them\nWeaknesses of Lifegain Decks\n⚠Lifegain without payoffs does not advance the game, so the deck can stall after key engines are removed.\n⚠A high life total does not protect against commander damage, poison counters, or combo finishes.\n⚠Payoff creatures attract removal, and the deck can lose momentum when several are answered in sequence.\n⚠The strategy can spend early turns gaining life while faster decks develop stronger engines.\nIs Lifegain Right for You?\nLifegain is approachable because the triggers are visible and many core cards are inexpensive in white or Orzhov. It suits players who want a patient engine deck that converts a large life total into damage, cards, or creature growth. The main lesson is that life is a resource to spend or convert, not a score to protect.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nAristocrats\nAristocrats gains life through death triggers; Lifegain starts with life gained and converts it.\n+1/+1 Counters\nCounters decks grow creatures from many sources; Lifegain uses life triggers as the counter engine.\nGroup Hug\nGroup Hug gives everyone resources; Lifegain keeps its life total as a private engine.\nEnchantress\nEnchantress draws from casting enchantments; Lifegain cares about the life those cards produce.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Lifegain CommandersBurnFlying\nTop Lifegain Commanders\nOloro, Ageless Ascetic\nMV: 6\nLifegainControlCombo+7 more\n$18.51TCGPlayer\n€11.21CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nGiada, Font of Hope\nMV: 2\nAngelsLifegain+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.96TCGPlayer\n€0.53CardMarket\nLiesa, Shroud of Dusk\nMV: 5\nLifegainLifedrainHatebears+7 more\n$0.91TCGPlayer\n€0.97CardMarket\nZoraline, Cosmos Caller\nMV: 3\nLifegainBatsFlying+7 more\n$0.26TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nDina, Soul Steeper\nMV: 2\nLifegainAristocratsLifedrain+7 more\n$0.21TCGPlayer\n€0.18CardMarket\nAstarion, the Decadent\nMV: 6\nLifegainVampiresLifedrain+7 more\n$1.67TCGPlayer\n€1.06CardMarket\nSorin of House Markov // Sorin, Ravenous Neonate\nMV: 2\nLifegainVampiresLifedrain+7 more\n$4.28TCGPlayer\n€3.64CardMarket\nK'rrik, Son of Yawgmoth\nMV: 7\nLifegainComboSelf-Damage+7 more\n$1.12TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nBetor, Ancestor's Voice\nMV: 5\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersReanimator+7 more\n$3.47TCGPlayer\n€2.52CardMarket\nBilbo, Birthday Celebrant\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nLifegainTokensCombo+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.24CardMarket\nLathiel, the Bounteous Dawn\nMV: 4\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersUnicorns+7 more\n$0.85TCGPlayer\n€0.32CardMarket\nClavileño, First of the Blessed\nMV: 3\nVampiresLifegainAristocrats+7 more\n$0.71TCGPlayer\n€0.86CardMarket\nHope Estheim\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nMillLifegainControl+7 more\n$0.38TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nKarlov of the Ghost Council\nMV: 2\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersVoltron+7 more\n$13.96TCGPlayer\n€10.30CardMarket\nAmalia Benavides Aguirre\nMV: 2\nLifegainReanimatorAristocrats+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.55CardMarket\nWill, Scion of Peace\nMV: 3\nLifegainX SpellsCombo+7 more\n$1.39TCGPlayer\n€0.64CardMarket\nVito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose\nMV: 3\nLifegainVampiresLifedrain+7 more\n$11.57TCGPlayer\n€8.71CardMarket\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nControlSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$5.35TCGPlayer\n€2.75CardMarket\nHeliod, Sun-Crowned\nMV: 3\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersStax+7 more\n$22.28TCGPlayer\n€16.39CardMarket\nElas il-Kor, Sadistic Pilgrim\nMV: 2\nLifegainAristocratsTokens+7 more\n$2.85TCGPlayer\n€1.22CardMarket\nBlech, Loafing Pest\nMV: 3\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersTokens+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nLicia, Sanguine Tribune\nMV: 8\nLifegainVampiresSelf-Damage+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nElenda, Saint of Dusk\nMV: 4\nLifegainVampiresVoltron+7 more\n$0.37TCGPlayer\n€0.30CardMarket\nDina, Essence Brewer\nMV: 3\nLifegainAristocrats+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$2.29TCGPlayer\n€1.89CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Lifegain commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Lifegain commander?\nThe best lifegain commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you gain life in small repeatable increments from creatures spells and combat.\nHow do Lifegain decks win?\nLifegain decks win through a combination of Soul Warden, Ajani's Pridemate, Aetherflux Reservoir, and other synergistic pieces that lifegain decks are for players who want each point of life to become a resource, not just a larger cushion against attacks.\nWhat are the best colors for Lifegain?\nMono-white supplies Soul Warden effects, Pridemate payoffs, protection, and steady board rebuilding. However, Orzhov pairs white lifegain with black drain, converting each trigger into pressure.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Lifegain decks?\nLifegain without payoffs does not advance the game, so the deck can stall after key engines are removed. A high life total does not protect against commander damage, poison counters, or combo finishes. Payoff creatures attract removal, and the deck can lose momentum when several are answered in sequence. The strategy can spend early turns gaining life while faster decks develop stronger engines.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,mill,page_text,10976,"Best Mill Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nMill\nBack to SearchBest Mill Commanders for EDH\nMill decks are for players who want to pressure a different resource: the cards left in each library. This strategy wants to put cards from libraries into graveyards, using repeatable mill commanders, doublers, and self-mill engines, to win by emptying opponents' decks or by converting stocked graveyards into value. Unlike Reanimator, Mill is not mainly trying to cheat one creature into play, and unlike Graveyard value it treats milling as the weapon or fuel source rather than the recursion engine itself. The best mill decks EDH players build respect the multiplayer math by multiplying output, protecting engines, or turning every milled card into another resource.\nBest Colors for Mill Decks\nMono-blue supplies mill engines, Bruvac-style doublers, card draw, and counterspells for protection.\nDimir adds black removal, graveyard theft, reanimation, and payoffs for cards milled.\nSultai turns self-mill into recursion, delve, land engines, and green-black graveyard value.\nHow Do Mill Decks Win in Commander?\nMill decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You decide whether milling opponents is the win condition or whether self-mill fuels your own engine.\n✓ You deploy repeatable mill effects that chip away at libraries across turn cycles.\n✓ You add doublers so each spell or trigger removes a meaningful number of cards.\n✓ You protect the engine with interaction while opponents race your library clock.\n✓ You convert stocked graveyards into recursion theft drain or reanimation value.\n✓ You close by emptying libraries or cashing in the graveyards you filled.\nBest Cards for Mill EDH Decks\nBruvac the Grandiloquent\nDoubles mill output so repeatable engines matter against multiple Commander libraries.\nMaddening Cacophony\nScales from early mill to a late kicked spell that can finish with a doubler.\nMesmeric Orb\nMills every player through normal untaps, feeding both opponent pressure and self-mill plans.\nFleet Swallower\nTurns one combat connection into half a library, or more with mill doublers.\nAltar of Dementia\nFree sacrifice outlet that converts creature size into targeted mill or self-mill.\nHow to Build a Mill Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 repeatable mill engines like Mesmeric Orb, Ruin Crab, and Sphinx's Tutelage\n✓Include 2-3 mill multipliers such as Bruvac the Grandiloquent and Fraying Sanity\n✓Add payoffs that reward milling, such as Consuming Aberration or Bloodchief Ascension\n✓Choose your split between opponent mill and self-mill before selecting payoffs\nDeck Structure\n✓Reserve 8-10 interaction slots because mill often needs several turns to finish a table\n✓Include graveyard exile or theft so your milling does not only help recursion opponents\n✓Choose a commander that mills repeatedly, doubles output, or pays you for cards milled\n✓Add draw engines that pair with wheels, draw triggers, or opponents' cards entering graveyards\nWin Conditions\n✓Empty libraries with doubled Maddening Cacophony, Fleet Swallower attacks, or Traumatize effects\n✓Win through Jace, Wielder of Mysteries or Laboratory Maniac after milling yourself\n✓Use stocked graveyards for mass reanimation, theft, or drain when full decking is too slow\nWeaknesses of Mill Decks\n⚠Milling three 99-card libraries can be slow, and faster tables may win before the clock matters.\n⚠Shuffle-back effects can undo large amounts of mill progress with one graveyard trigger.\n⚠Mill can help Reanimator and Graveyard opponents by putting their best cards where they want them.\n⚠Graveyard hate can shut off self-mill value plans and many payoffs tied to milled cards.\nIs Mill Right for You?\nMill suits players who enjoy attacking a resource most decks do not defend directly. It has a higher planning requirement in Commander because three libraries are a lot of cards, so dedicated engines matter more than incidental mill. Budget builds are realistic when they focus on repeatable effects, self-mill value, and a few strong finishers.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nReanimator\nMill stocks graveyards; Reanimator uses them to return large creatures.\nGraveyard\nSelf-mill fills the graveyard; Graveyard decks make recursion the central engine.\nControl\nMill borrows answers to buy time; Control wins through card advantage and finishers.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Mill CommandersSelf-MillWheels\nTop Mill Commanders\nThe Wise Mothman\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nMill+1/+1 CountersMutants+7 more\n$11.98TCGPlayer\n€9.71CardMarket\nCaptain N'ghathrod\nMV: 5\nMillHorrorsTheft+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nThe Mindskinner\nMV: 3\nMillEquipmentVoltron+7 more\n$3.40TCGPlayer\n€0.86CardMarket\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nHope Estheim\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nMillLifegainControl+7 more\n$0.38TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nPhenax, God of Deception\nMV: 5\nMillToughness MattersCombo+7 more\n$12.52TCGPlayer\n€10.43CardMarket\nCoram, the Undertaker\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.26CardMarket\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide\nMV: 6\nReanimatorMillSacrifice+7 more\n$0.61TCGPlayer\n€0.81CardMarket\nBruvac the Grandiloquent\nMV: 3\nMillPersistent PetitionersCombo+7 more\n$46.20TCGPlayer\n€36.04CardMarket\nSidisi, Brood Tyrant\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\n$2.30TCGPlayer\n€0.80CardMarket\nWinter, Cynical Opportunist\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nThe Mycotyrant\nMV: 3\nMillTokensSelf-Mill+7 more\n$1.18TCGPlayer\n€0.89CardMarket\nAnowon, the Ruin Thief\nMV: 4\nRoguesMillTheft+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nDisa the Restless\nMV: 5\nGraveyardMillReanimator+7 more\n$0.14TCGPlayer\n€0.25CardMarket\nUmbris, Fear Manifest\nMV: 5\nHorrorsExileMill+7 more\n$12.47TCGPlayer\n€5.36CardMarket\nThe Master, Transcendent\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nGrolnok, the Omnivore\nMV: 4\nFrogsMillSelf-Mill+7 more\n$0.54TCGPlayer\n€0.30CardMarket\nSaruman of Many Colors\nMV: 6Universes Beyond\nMillSpellslingerControl+7 more\n$0.96TCGPlayer\n€1.11CardMarket\nThe Mimeoplasm\nMV: 5\nMillReanimator+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.27TCGPlayer\n€0.19CardMarket\nSyr Konrad, the Grim\nMV: 5\nMillAristocratsReanimator+7 more\n$1.76TCGPlayer\n€0.90CardMarket\nThe Ancient One\nMV: 2\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\n$1.63TCGPlayer\n€0.99CardMarket\nLazav, Dimir Mastermind\nMV: 4\nMillClonesControl+7 more\n$0.70TCGPlayer\n€0.85CardMarket\nLord Xander, the Collector\nMV: 7\nMillDiscardClones+7 more\n$2.35TCGPlayer\n€1.50CardMarket\nTasha, the Witch Queen\nMV: 5\nExileTheftMill+7 more\n$6.44TCGPlayer\n€5.97CardMarket\nNeerdiv, Devious Diver\nMV: 3\nMillSelf-MillGraveyard+7 more\n$1.43TCGPlayer\n€1.48CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Mill commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Mill commander?\nThe best mill commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you decide whether milling opponents is the win condition or whether self-mill fuels your own engine.\nHow do Mill decks win?\nMill decks win through a combination of Bruvac the Grandiloquent, Maddening Cacophony, Mesmeric Orb, and other synergistic pieces that mill decks are for players who want to pressure a different resource: the cards left in each library.\nWhat are the best colors for Mill?\nMono-blue supplies mill engines, Bruvac-style doublers, card draw, and counterspells for protection. However, Dimir adds black removal, graveyard theft, reanimation, and payoffs for cards milled.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Mill decks?\nMilling three 99-card libraries can be slow, and faster tables may win before the clock matters. Shuffle-back effects can undo large amounts of mill progress with one graveyard trigger. Mill can help Reanimator and Graveyard opponents by putting their best cards where they want them. Graveyard hate can shut off self-mill value plans and many payoffs tied to milled cards.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
