ns\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nProliferate\nBack to SearchBest Proliferate Commanders for EDH\nProliferate decks are for players who want one trigger to move several counter-based plans forward at the same time. This strategy wants to place useful counters first, using +1/+1 counters, poison, loyalty, charge counters, and repeatable proliferate commanders, to win through a grown board, planeswalker ultimates, or the final poison counter. Unlike +1/+1 Counters, Proliferate multiplies any counter type rather than only creature size, and unlike Infect it can use poison as one route without making ten poison the whole plan. Counter synergy EDH decks built around proliferate reward pilots who seed the right counters, then chain small increases until every permanent matters.\nBest Colors for Proliferate Decks\nSimic pairs green counter growth with blue proliferate, card draw, and engine protection.\nAtraxa colors support creature counters, superfriends, poison, removal, and a commander that proliferates every turn.\nAzorius uses blue-white control and planeswalkers, then proliferates loyalty toward ultimates.\nDimir supports poison and control, placing early counters before proliferating toward ten.\nHow Do Proliferate Decks Win in Commander?\nProliferate decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You place the first counters before proliferate effects can multiply them.\n✓ You use repeatable proliferate engines from spells land drops and activated abilities.\n✓ You stack counter types so one trigger grows creatures planeswalkers poison and artifacts together.\n✓ You protect the board because incremental counters disappear quickly to sweepers.\n✓ You choose whether the table is vulnerable to combat loyalty ultimates or poison.\n✓ You close by adding the last counters needed for your chosen route.\nBest Cards for Proliferate EDH Decks\nEvolution Sage\nTurns each land drop into another proliferate trigger for every counter you have seeded.\nFlux Channeler\nRewards cantrips, removal, and interaction by proliferating after each noncreature spell.\nInexorable Tide\nProliferates from every spell cast, making long turns add counters across the board.\nContagion Engine\nPlaces -1/-1 counters first, then becomes a repeatable double-proliferate mana sink.\nKarn's Bastion\nAdds repeatable proliferate from a land slot when you have spare mana.\nHow to Build a Proliferate Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 repeatable proliferate engines like Evolution Sage, Flux Channeler, and Inexorable Tide\n✓Include utility lands such as Karn's Bastion that proliferate without taking a spell slot\n✓Add 10-15 permanents that create their own counters so proliferate has targets\n✓Play 8-10 card draw effects because the engine needs a steady stream of spells\nCounter Payoffs\n✓Choose payoffs across counter types, such as +1/+1 threats, planeswalkers, and charge artifacts\n✓Use sagas, vanishing, and fading carefully because proliferate advances their counters too\n✓Add one or two poison sources only if your table accepts poison as a backup route\n✓Include effects like The Ozolith so creature counters can survive removal\nWin Conditions\n✓Grow a counter-heavy board and finish with trample or evasion support\n✓Use repeated proliferate triggers to move planeswalkers toward ultimates ahead of schedule\n✓Deal one poison counter to each opponent, then proliferate toward ten without more combat\n✓Use Simic Ascendancy or similar alternate wins when creature combat stalls\nWeaknesses of Proliferate Decks\n⚠Proliferate multiplies existing counters but does not create them, so the deck stalls without seed counters.\n⚠Board wipes can erase several turns of incremental growth, especially when counters are mostly on creatures.\n⚠The engine takes time to compound, so fast combo or combat pressure can move under it.\n⚠Poison counters often draw table attention long before you have enough proliferate triggers to finish.\nIs Proliferate Right for You?\nProliferate suits players who enjoy engines where several small counters advance together. It asks more tracking than a straight +1/+1 counters deck because poison, loyalty, charge, and creature counters can all matter at once. Budget builds work well around Evolution Sage, Flux Channeler, and focused counter payoffs.\nRelated Commander Strategies\n+1/+1 Counters\nCounters focuses on +1/+1 growth; Proliferate multiplies any counter type.\nInfect\nInfect aims at ten poison; Proliferate can use poison beside other counter plans.\nTokens\nTokens makes bodies; Proliferate grows counters already placed on those bodies.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Proliferate CommandersInfectSuperfriends\nTop Proliferate Commanders\nAtraxa, Praetors' Voice\nMV: 4\nInfectPlaneswalkers+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$27.86TCGPlayer\n€21.67CardMarket\nKilo, Apogee Mind\nMV: 3\nArtifacts+1/+1 CountersProliferate+7 more\n$2.07TCGPlayer\n€0.66CardMarket\nTidus, Yuna's Guardian\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\n+1/+1 CountersProliferateCounters Matter+7 more\n$2.75TCGPlayer\n€1.63CardMarket\nThe Wise Mothman\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nMill+1/+1 CountersMutants+7 more\n$11.98TCGPlayer\n€9.71CardMarket\nHigh Perfect Morcant\nMV: 4\n-1/-1 CountersElvesTokens+7 more\n$1.20TCGPlayer\n€1.33CardMarket\nInspirit, Flagship Vessel\nMV: 3\nArtifacts+1/+1 CountersCharge Counters+7 more\n$1.20TCGPlayer\n€0.76CardMarket\nXavier Sal, Infested Captain\nMV: 3\nTokensPlaneswalkers+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$2.79TCGPlayer\n€0.96CardMarket\nBrimaz, Blight of Oreskos\nMV: 4\nPhyrexiansInfectTokens+7 more\n$0.46TCGPlayer\n€0.41CardMarket\nFynn, the Fangbearer\nMV: 2\nDeathtouchInfectAggro+7 more\n$0.20TCGPlayer\n€0.24CardMarket\nIxhel, Scion of Atraxa\nMV: 4\nInfectPhyrexiansProliferate+7 more\n$3.01TCGPlayer\n€1.10CardMarket\nAuntie Ool, Cursewretch\nMV: 4\n-1/-1 CountersComboControl+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.32CardMarket\nTekuthal, Inquiry Dominus\nMV: 4\nPlaneswalkersInfectProliferate+7 more\n$1.59TCGPlayer\n€1.83CardMarket\nKros, Defense Contractor\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersForced CombatGroup Hug+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.42CardMarket\nEzuri, Stalker of Spheres\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersInfectProliferate+7 more\n$0.97TCGPlayer\n€0.53CardMarket\nCommodore Guff\nMV: 4\nPlaneswalkersControlProliferate+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nVishgraz, the Doomhive\nMV: 5\nInfectPhyrexiansTokens+7 more\n$1.43TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nToxrill, the Corrosive\nMV: 7\n-1/-1 CountersControlInfect+7 more\n$17.70TCGPlayer\n€15.75CardMarket\nVenser, Corpse Puppet\nMV: 2\nInfectProliferateArtifacts+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.17CardMarket\nAgent Frank Horrigan\nMV: 7Universes Beyond\nInfect+1/+1 CountersProliferate+7 more\n$5.14TCGPlayer\n€7.81CardMarket\nMinthara, Merciless Soul\nMV: 4\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$0.23TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nYuna, Grand Summoner\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\n+1/+1 CountersComboEnchantress+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nEzuri, Claw of Progress\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersInfectElves+7 more\n$2.37TCGPlayer\n€1.33CardMarket\nHapatra, Vizier of Poisons\nMV: 2\n-1/-1 CountersTokensInfect+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nJin-Gitaxias // The Great Synthesis\nMV: 5\nControlCantripsCard Draw+7 more\n$12.44TCGPlayer\n€8.81CardMarket\nFalco Spara, Pactweaver\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersBirdsCombo+7 more\n$2.62TCGPlayer\n€2.44CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Proliferate commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Proliferate commander?\nThe best proliferate commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you place the first counters before proliferate effects can multiply them.\nHow do Proliferate decks win?\nProliferate decks win through a combination of Evolution Sage, Flux Channeler, Inexorable Tide, and other synergistic pieces that proliferate decks are for players who want one trigger to move several counter-based plans forward at the same time.\nWhat are the best colors for Proliferate?\nSimic pairs green counter growth with blue proliferate, card draw, and engine protection. However, Atraxa colors support creature counters, superfriends, poison, removal, and a commander that proliferates every turn.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Proliferate decks?\nProliferate multiplies existing counters but does not create them, so the deck stalls without seed counters. Board wipes can erase several turns of incremental growth, especially when counters are mostly on creatures. The engine takes time to compound, so fast combo or combat pressure can move under it. Poison counters often draw table attention long before you have enough proliferate triggers to finish.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,ramp,page_text,10551,"Best Ramp Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nRamp\nBack to SearchBest Ramp Commanders for EDH\nRamp decks are for players who want to spend the early game accelerating so later turns happen ahead of schedule. This strategy wants to build more mana than the table, using mana ramp EDH staples, extra land drops, dorks, and rocks, to win through earlier threats, large spells, or a resource lead opponents must answer. Ramp is different from Landfall because acceleration is the point, and different from Big Mana because it is the engine rather than the payoff. The best ramp commanders reward extra mana sources while still giving you something meaningful to cast.\nBest Colors for Ramp Decks\nMono-green has the deepest pool of land ramp, mana dorks, and mana doubling effects.\nSimic pairs ramp with card draw and extra land drops for steady acceleration.\nGruul converts early mana leads into hasty creatures and large combat turns.\nSelesnya adds catch-up ramp, tax pieces, and token boards to the acceleration package.\nHow Do Ramp Decks Win in Commander?\nRamp decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You spend early turns converting cards into extra mana with rocks dorks and land ramp.\n✓ You aim to have more mana available than the table on key turns.\n✓ You deploy threats ahead of the natural curve.\n✓ You refill with payoffs that reward lands and mana sources.\n✓ You close by converting the mana lead into large spells or a board opponents cannot ignore.\nBest Cards for Ramp EDH Decks\nSol Ring\nEfficient mana rock that accelerates most Commander decks from turn one.\nCultivate\nFinds lands to fix colors and keep future land drops steady.\nSakura-Tribe Elder\nBlocks early, then sacrifices itself for a land at instant speed.\nSmothering Tithe\nGenerates Treasure from opposing draws and turns table activity into mana.\nNyxbloom Ancient\nTriples mana production so late-game spells scale much higher.\nHow to Build a Ramp Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 10-12 ramp pieces including Cultivate, Llanowar Elves, and cheap rocks like Arcane Signet\n✓Prioritize two-mana acceleration so ramp arrives before opponents can pressure it\n✓Include a few mana multipliers such as Zendikar Resurgent or Mana Reflection\n✓Favor land ramp in sweeper-heavy metas because lands survive most board wipes\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep land counts around 37-40 because ramp decks still want every land drop\n✓Add 8-10 card draw effects so the deck has spells to cast once mana arrives\n✓Reserve 8-10 interaction slots because a mana lead does not stop an opposing combo\nWin Conditions\n✓Pack top-end threats that scale with mana, like Craterhoof Behemoth or big X spells\n✓Choose payoffs across multiple angles so one removal spell does not answer everything\n✓Use commanders like Kinnan or Azusa as both engine and payoff when possible\nWeaknesses of Ramp Decks\n⚠Spending early turns on mana instead of interaction can leave you exposed to fast aggro and combo starts.\n⚠Ramping into one large threat is vulnerable to removal or counterspells that trade up on mana.\n⚠Board wipes that hit mana dorks and rocks can erase several turns of investment.\n⚠A visible mana lead draws attention, and some ramp decks lack enough tools to defend it.\nIs Ramp Right for You?\nRamp is easy to learn because the core plan is direct: make mana, then spend it on cards that matter. Budget builds work well since staples like Cultivate, Kodama's Reach, and Rampant Growth are inexpensive. It suits players who enjoy setting up early and converting a mana lead into larger turns.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nBig Mana\nRamp builds the mana; Big Mana focuses on the enormous spells it enables.\nLandfall\nLandfall treats land drops as triggers; Ramp uses them mainly for acceleration.\nArtifacts\nArtifacts decks make mana rocks synergy pieces; Ramp uses them to get ahead.\nGroup Hug\nGroup Hug accelerates everyone; Ramp keeps the mana advantage for its own payoff.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Ramp CommandersLands MatterTreasure\nTop Ramp Commanders\nPantlaza, Sun-Favored\nMV: 5\nDinosaursDiscoverRamp+7 more\n$5.75TCGPlayer\n€5.93CardMarket\nHearthhull, the Worldseed\nMV: 4\nLands MatterRampLand Destruction+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait\nMV: 6\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$12.07TCGPlayer\n€13.93CardMarket\nGishath, Sun's Avatar\nMV: 8\nDinosaursRampAggro+7 more\n$5.34TCGPlayer\n€4.66CardMarket\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of Creation\nMV: 4\nLandfallLands MatterRamp+7 more\n$4.94TCGPlayer\n€3.36CardMarket\nUreni of the Unwritten\nMV: 7\nDragonsFlyingRamp+7 more\n$18.24TCGPlayer\n€14.57CardMarket\nThe Ur-Dragon\nMV: 9\nDragonsFlyingShapeshifters+7 more\n$37.54TCGPlayer\n€30.89CardMarket\nThe Necrobloom\nMV: 4\nLands MatterTokensDredge+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nTifa Lockhart\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nLands MatterVoltronRamp+7 more\n$2.51TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of Mana\nMV: 3\nRampBig ManaVoltron+7 more\n$4.18TCGPlayer\n€1.80CardMarket\nLathril, Blade of the Elves\nMV: 4\nElvesTokensAggro+7 more\n$1.78TCGPlayer\n€1.45CardMarket\nOmnath, Locus of Rage\nMV: 7\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nOmo, Queen of Vesuva\nMV: 3\nLands MatterRampShapeshifters+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nKinnan, Bonder Prodigy\nMV: 2\nComboRampMidrange+7 more\n$16.80TCGPlayer\n€12.90CardMarket\nLord Windgrace\nMV: 5\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nEtali, Primal Conqueror // Etali, Primal Sickness\nMV: 7\nClonesComboRamp+7 more\n$30.15TCGPlayer\n€21.25CardMarket\nZhulodok, Void Gorger\nMV: 6\nEldraziBig ManaCascade+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nThe Wandering Minstrel\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nLands MatterRampCombo+7 more\n$0.32TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nHelga, Skittish Seer\nMV: 3\n+1/+1 CountersRampBig Mana+7 more\n$4.42TCGPlayer\n€4.09CardMarket\nZimone, Mystery Unraveler\nMV: 4\nLands MatterMorphLandfall+7 more\n$0.51TCGPlayer\n€0.63CardMarket\nAzusa, Lost but Seeking\nMV: 3\nLands MatterLandfallRamp+7 more\n$13.07TCGPlayer\n€8.80CardMarket\nRaggadragga, Goreguts Boss\nMV: 4\nRampAggroBig Mana+7 more\n$0.25TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nMiirym, Sentinel Wyrm\nMV: 6\nDragonsClonesTokens+7 more\n$6.17TCGPlayer\n€5.79CardMarket\nFlubs, the Fool\nMV: 3\nLands MatterCheeriosLandfall+7 more\n$2.89TCGPlayer\n€0.61CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Ramp commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Ramp commander?\nThe best ramp commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you spend early turns converting cards into extra mana with rocks dorks and land ramp.\nHow do Ramp decks win?\nRamp decks win through a combination of Sol Ring, Cultivate, Sakura-Tribe Elder, and other synergistic pieces that ramp decks are for players who want to spend the early game accelerating so later turns happen ahead of schedule.\nWhat are the best colors for Ramp?\nMono-green has the deepest pool of land ramp, mana dorks, and mana doubling effects. However, Simic pairs ramp with card draw and extra land drops for steady acceleration.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Ramp decks?\nSpending early turns on mana instead of interaction can leave you exposed to fast aggro and combo starts. Ramping into one large threat is vulnerable to removal or counterspells that trade up on mana. Board wipes that hit mana dorks and rocks can erase several turns of investment. A visible mana lead draws attention, and some ramp decks lack enough tools to defend it.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,reanimator,page_text,11361,"Best Reanimator Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nReanimator\nBack to SearchBest Reanimator Commanders for EDH\nReanimator decks are for players who want to turn the graveyard into a shortcut for creatures that should cost far more mana. This strategy wants to load its own graveyard with high-impact targets, using discard, self-mill, and Entomb-style tutors, to win by casting cheap reanimation spells that cheat creatures into play ahead of schedule. Unlike Graveyard value decks, Reanimator is less about recurring small resources every turn, and unlike Mill it is not trying to empty libraries as the primary weapon. The best reanimator commanders and graveyard commanders help discard threats, return them, or convert each reanimated creature into immediate pressure.\nBest Colors for Reanimator Decks\nMono-black supplies the cheapest reanimation spells, discard outlets, tutors, and life-for-mana exchanges.\nDimir adds blue self-mill, card selection, and counterspells to protect the reanimation turn.\nOrzhov pairs black recursion with white protection, removal, and resilient creature returns.\nGolgari adds green self-mill, creature tutors, and regrowth effects that reuse spent recursion.\nHow Do Reanimator Decks Win in Commander?\nReanimator decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You put expensive creatures into your graveyard before you could cast them normally.\n✓ You use discard self-mill and tutors to find the right target for the table.\n✓ You cast cheap reanimation spells that convert the graveyard into battlefield pressure.\n✓ You choose creatures with enter triggers attack triggers or static effects that matter right away.\n✓ You hold backup recursion and answers for graveyard hate before committing.\n✓ You close with repeated reanimation or one mass return spell.\nBest Cards for Reanimator EDH Decks\nReanimate\nTrades life for mana efficiency by returning the best creature in any graveyard.\nAnimate Dead\nLow-cost aura recursion that brings back a creature and supports repeatable sacrifice loops.\nEntomb\nPuts the exact creature you need into the graveyard at instant speed.\nBuried Alive\nStocks three creature targets at once for single-target or mass reanimation turns.\nLiving Death\nTurns stocked graveyards into a new battlefield while clearing creatures already in play.\nHow to Build a Reanimator Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 reanimation spells such as Reanimate, Animate Dead, Necromancy, and Victimize\n✓Include 6-8 graveyard setup cards like Entomb, Buried Alive, looting spells, and self-mill\n✓Choose 6-10 targets with immediate impact, such as Archon of Cruelty or Ashen Rider\n✓Add cheap creatures that block early and give Victimize or sacrifice outlets usable fuel\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep setup and recursion costs low so your first threat arrives before normal casting curves\n✓Favor enter-the-battlefield triggers because removal cannot undo the value already gained\n✓Reserve interaction for graveyard hate, exile removal, and counterspells on the reanimation spell\n✓Mix targets between attackers, engines, and answer creatures so the graveyard remains flexible\nWin Conditions\n✓Reanimate an early threat that attacks resources, life totals, or opposing boards immediately\n✓Use Living Death style effects when your graveyard contains several creatures worth returning\n✓Loop creatures with sacrifice outlets and reanimation when one large target cannot finish alone\nWeaknesses of Reanimator Decks\n⚠Graveyard hate like Rest in Peace or Bojuka Bog can remove every stocked target at once.\n⚠Instant-speed exile can answer the reanimated creature after you spend both setup and recursion.\n⚠Hands with reanimation but no target, or targets with no discard outlet, can stall badly.\n⚠The key turn is easy to read, so opponents often save interaction for the first recursion spell.\nIs Reanimator Right for You?\nReanimator has a clear loop: move a creature to the graveyard, then return it for less mana than it should cost. It suits players who enjoy planning one strong turn and can accept that graveyard hate sometimes resets the work. Budget builds can use cheaper targets and recursion, while premium tutors and namesake spells raise the ceiling.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nSacrifice\nSacrifice spends creatures for value; Reanimator uses the graveyard to return larger threats.\nGraveyard\nGraveyard decks recur many resources; Reanimator cheats high-impact creatures into play.\nMill\nMill attacks libraries or fuels engines; Reanimator stocks its own graveyard with targets.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Reanimator CommandersSelf-MillDiscard\nTop Reanimator Commanders\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nTerra, Herald of Hope\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nReanimatorGraveyardSelf-Mill+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.82CardMarket\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth\nMV: 4\nAristocratsReanimatorSacrifice+7 more\n$2.38TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nSefris of the Hidden Ways\nMV: 3\nReanimatorDungeonDiscard+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide\nMV: 6\nReanimatorMillSacrifice+7 more\n$0.61TCGPlayer\n€0.81CardMarket\nHashaton, Scarab's Fist\nMV: 2\nDiscardReanimatorTokens+7 more\n$0.88TCGPlayer\n€1.23CardMarket\nBetor, Ancestor's Voice\nMV: 5\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersReanimator+7 more\n$3.47TCGPlayer\n€2.52CardMarket\nSauron, the Dark Lord\nMV: 6Universes Beyond\nZombiesReanimatorAmass+7 more\n$21.68TCGPlayer\n€21.08CardMarket\nRaffine, Scheming Seer\nMV: 3\nReanimatorWheels+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$7.31TCGPlayer\n€4.50CardMarket\nCeles, Rune Knight\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nReanimatorCombo+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.89TCGPlayer\n€0.82CardMarket\nSidar Jabari of Zhalfir\nMV: 4\nKnightsReanimatorAggro+7 more\n$0.52TCGPlayer\n€0.46CardMarket\nHenzie \"Toolbox\" Torre\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAggroAristocrats+7 more\n$1.00TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nMirko, Obsessive Theorist\nMV: 3\nTopdeckReanimatorSurveil+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nAnikthea, Hand of Erebos\nMV: 5\nEnchantressReanimatorTokens+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver\nMV: 4\nZombiesTokensAristocrats+7 more\n$9.30TCGPlayer\n€10.24CardMarket\nSlimefoot and Squee\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAristocratsSaprolings+7 more\n$3.90TCGPlayer\n€2.69CardMarket\nGlarb, Calamity's Augur\nMV: 3\nLands MatterFrogsReanimator+7 more\n$3.82TCGPlayer\n€4.07CardMarket\nShilgengar, Sire of Famine\nMV: 5\nAngelsReanimatorLifegain+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nImotekh the Stormlord\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nArtifactsReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKarador, Ghost Chieftain\nMV: 8\nReanimatorGraveyardBirthing Pod+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.32CardMarket\nDisa the Restless\nMV: 5\nGraveyardMillReanimator+7 more\n$0.14TCGPlayer\n€0.25CardMarket\nNethroi, Apex of Death\nMV: 5\nReanimatorMutateMill+7 more\n$2.36TCGPlayer\n€2.22CardMarket\nWinter, Cynical Opportunist\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorSelf-Mill+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nK'rrik, Son of Yawgmoth\nMV: 7\nLifegainComboSelf-Damage+7 more\n$1.12TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nCoram, the Undertaker\nMV: 4\nMillReanimatorGraveyard+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.26CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Reanimator commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Reanimator commander?\nThe best reanimator commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you put expensive creatures into your graveyard before you could cast them normally.\nHow do Reanimator decks win?\nReanimator decks win through a combination of Reanimate, Animate Dead, Entomb, and other synergistic pieces that reanimator decks are for players who want to turn the graveyard into a shortcut for creatures that should cost far more mana.\nWhat are the best colors for Reanimator?\nMono-black supplies the cheapest reanimation spells, discard outlets, tutors, and life-for-mana exchanges. However, Dimir adds blue self-mill, card selection, and counterspells to protect the reanimation turn.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Reanimator decks?\nGraveyard hate like Rest in Peace or Bojuka Bog can remove every stocked target at once. Instant-speed exile can answer the reanimated creature after you spend both setup and recursion. Hands with reanimation but no target, or targets with no discard outlet, can stall badly. The key turn is easy to read, so opponents often save interaction for the first recursion spell.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,sacrifice,page_text,11051,"Best Sacrifice Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nSacrifice\nBack to SearchBest Sacrifice Commanders for EDH\nSacrifice decks are for players who want their own permanents to be flexible resources rather than pieces to protect at all costs. This strategy wants to feed creatures, artifacts, and tokens into a sacrifice outlet, using fodder makers, recursion, and sacrifice commanders, to win through mana loops, edicts, drain payoffs, or a steady resource lead. Unlike Aristocrats, it is broader than death trigger drain, and unlike Tokens it values bodies mainly as expendable fuel. The best sacrifice commanders keep the engine moving even when opponents point removal at your board.\nBest Colors for Sacrifice Decks\nMono-black supplies free outlets, edicts, recursion, and drain payoffs for sacrifice engines.\nGolgari adds green token production and recursion to reuse creatures after they die.\nRakdos uses Treasure, impulse draw, and damage payoffs to convert sacrifices into pressure.\nJund combines fodder, outlets, recursion, and payoffs for commanders that reward repeated deaths.\nHow Do Sacrifice Decks Win in Commander?\nSacrifice decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You assemble cheap fodder producers and at least one free sacrifice outlet.\n✓ You feed your own permanents into outlets for mana cards removal or triggers.\n✓ You sacrifice targeted creatures in response so removal produces value for you.\n✓ You rebuild after sweepers with recursion and token generation.\n✓ You convert the resource lead into drain loops edicts or a decisive attack.\nBest Cards for Sacrifice EDH Decks\nAshnod's Altar\nFree outlet that turns creatures into mana for loops and larger turns.\nPhyrexian Altar\nConverts creatures into colored mana for token and recursion loops.\nViscera Seer\nOne-mana free outlet that scries while letting creatures dodge removal.\nGrave Pact\nTurns your sacrifices into edicts that pressure every opponent's board.\nPitiless Plunderer\nMakes Treasure when your creatures die, keeping sacrifice chains funded.\nHow to Build a Sacrifice Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 3-5 free sacrifice outlets like Viscera Seer, Carrion Feeder, and Ashnod's Altar\n✓Include 8-10 fodder sources such as token makers, Reassembling Skeleton, and Treasure producers\n✓Add payoffs that trigger when your permanents die or leave play, such as Grave Pact\n✓Prioritize Meren-style recursion to reuse your best sacrifice pieces each turn\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep the mana curve low so fodder and outlets arrive early and rebuild after sweepers\n✓Choose a commander that generates fodder or rewards deaths so one removal spell does not stop the deck\n✓Include 8-10 card advantage effects tied to sacrifice like Village Rites and Deadly Dispute\n✓Save 6-8 interaction slots, leaning on edicts and sacrifice-based removal\nWin Conditions\n✓Drain the table by stacking death payoffs onto a steady stream of sacrifices\n✓Loop free outlets, recursion, and Treasure or mana producers where your table allows\n✓Grind boards down with edicts and recursion, then close with a rebuilt board\nWeaknesses of Sacrifice Decks\n⚠Graveyard hate like Rest in Peace can shut off recursion and make sacrifices cost real cards.\n⚠The deck can struggle against fast combo because its value plan usually needs several turns.\n⚠Losing key outlets or payoff enchantments can stall the engine until replacements arrive.\n⚠Token-light draws leave too little fodder, making early sacrifices costly.\nIs Sacrifice Right for You?\nSacrifice is approachable because your creatures dying is part of the plan, so removal and sweepers hurt less than they do in many creature decks. It rewards players who enjoy instant-speed decisions and converting small resources into cards, mana, or removal. Budget versions work well, though premium altar effects can raise the price.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nAristocrats\nAristocrats focuses on death trigger drain; Sacrifice uses more permanent types as resources.\nTokens\nTokens makes bodies for width; Sacrifice spends those bodies as fuel.\nGraveyard\nSacrifice fills the graveyard; Graveyard decks make that zone the engine.\nReanimator\nReanimator cheats big creatures back; Sacrifice recurs small pieces and fodder.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Sacrifice CommandersTreasureAristocrats\nTop Sacrifice Commanders\nYgra, Eater of All\nMV: 5\nSacrificeAristocratsTokens+7 more\n$11.96TCGPlayer\n€8.01CardMarket\nKorvold, Fae-Cursed King\nMV: 5\nSacrificeTreasureAristocrats+7 more\n$0.41TCGPlayer\n€1.13CardMarket\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide\nMV: 6\nReanimatorMillSacrifice+7 more\n$0.61TCGPlayer\n€0.81CardMarket\nCamellia, the Seedmiser\nMV: 3\nSacrificeTokensSquirrels+7 more\n$2.80TCGPlayer\n€1.10CardMarket\nTergrid, God of Fright // Tergrid's Lantern\nMV: 5\nDiscardSacrificeGroup Slug+7 more\n$19.20TCGPlayer\n€16.12CardMarket\nHearthhull, the Worldseed\nMV: 4\nLands MatterRampLand Destruction+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.67CardMarket\nRakdos, the Muscle\nMV: 5\nSacrificeAristocratsCombo+7 more\n$2.19TCGPlayer\n€1.54CardMarket\nSephiroth, Fabled SOLDIER // Sephiroth, One-Winged Angel\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nAristocratsSacrificeCombo+7 more\n$28.89TCGPlayer\n€27.97CardMarket\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth\nMV: 4\nAristocratsReanimatorSacrifice+7 more\n$2.38TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nTeysa Karlov\nMV: 4\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$2.65TCGPlayer\n€1.68CardMarket\nChatterfang, Squirrel General\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$11.09TCGPlayer\n€7.02CardMarket\nJuri, Master of the Revue\nMV: 2\nTreasureSacrificeAristocrats+7 more\n$0.30TCGPlayer\n€0.20CardMarket\nCarmen, Cruel Skymarcher\nMV: 5\nAristocratsSacrificeVampires+7 more\n$6.47TCGPlayer\n€1.83CardMarket\nUlalek, Fused Atrocity\nMV: 5\nEldraziBig ManaSacrifice+7 more\n$0.82TCGPlayer\n€0.45CardMarket\nVren, the Relentless\nMV: 4\nRatsRat ColonyTokens+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.41CardMarket\nMazirek, Kraul Death Priest\nMV: 5\nSacrifice+1/+1 CountersAristocrats+7 more\n$0.30TCGPlayer\n€0.49CardMarket\nAshnod the Uncaring\nMV: 5\nArtifactsSacrificeAristocrats+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.29CardMarket\nGyome, Master Chef\nMV: 4\nSacrificeTokensFood+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nSméagol, Helpful Guide\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nLands MatterLandfallSacrifice+7 more\n$0.38TCGPlayer\n€0.39CardMarket\nThe Necrobloom\nMV: 4\nLands MatterTokensDredge+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nBraids, Arisen Nightmare\nMV: 3\nAristocratsSacrificeReanimator+7 more\n$4.61TCGPlayer\n€4.68CardMarket\nHenzie \"Toolbox\" Torre\nMV: 3\nReanimatorAggroAristocrats+7 more\n$1.00TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nZiatora, the Incinerator\nMV: 6\nTreasureSacrificeAristocrats+7 more\n$0.69TCGPlayer\n€1.10CardMarket\nProssh, Skyraider of Kher\nMV: 6\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.87CardMarket\nMinthara, Merciless Soul\nMV: 4\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$0.23TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Sacrifice commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Sacrifice commander?\nThe best sacrifice commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you assemble cheap fodder producers and at least one free sacrifice outlet.\nHow do Sacrifice decks win?\nSacrifice decks win through a combination of Ashnod's Altar, Phyrexian Altar, Viscera Seer, and other synergistic pieces that sacrifice decks are for players who want their own permanents to be flexible resources rather than pieces to protect at all costs.\nWhat are the best colors for Sacrifice?\nMono-black supplies free outlets, edicts, recursion, and drain payoffs for sacrifice engines. However, Golgari adds green token production and recursion to reuse creatures after they die.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Sacrifice decks?\nGraveyard hate like Rest in Peace can shut off recursion and make sacrifices cost real cards. The deck can struggle against fast combo because its value plan usually needs several turns. Losing key outlets or payoff enchantments can stall the engine until replacements arrive. Token-light draws leave too little fodder, making early sacrifices costly.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,spellslinger,page_text,11015,"Best Spellslinger Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nSpellslinger\nBack to SearchBest Spellslinger Commanders for EDH\nSpellslinger decks are for players who want each instant and sorcery to advance the board, refill the hand, or pressure life totals. This strategy wants to cast cheap spells across every turn cycle, using spellslinger commanders, magecraft payoffs, and copy effects, to win through accumulated damage, spell-made tokens, or card advantage. Unlike Storm, it does not need one huge chain turn, and unlike Combo it is not waiting for specific pieces to end the game at once. The best instants and sorceries decks reward steady sequencing and careful use of interaction.\nBest Colors for Spellslinger Decks\nIzzet combines blue cantrips and counters with red burn, copy effects, and impulse draw.\nGrixis adds tutors, recursion, and drain so spell chains can find and reuse key pieces.\nJeskai adds protection, tax pieces, and token payoffs to the core Izzet spell engine.\nHow Do Spellslinger Decks Win in Commander?\nSpellslinger decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You fill your deck with cheap instants and sorceries that matter in many game states.\n✓ You convert each cast into damage tokens cards or mana through payoff permanents.\n✓ You hold up interaction so defending yourself still advances the engine.\n✓ You refill with cantrips wheels and recursion to keep casting.\n✓ You close through accumulated triggers a token air force or a large spell from your resource lead.\nBest Cards for Spellslinger EDH Decks\nArchmage Emeritus\nMagecraft engine that draws whenever you cast or copy an instant or sorcery.\nGuttersnipe\nTurns instants and sorceries into damage against each opponent.\nTalrand, Sky Summoner\nMakes a flying Drake for each instant or sorcery you cast.\nStorm-Kiln Artist\nGenerates Treasure from spell casts to fund longer turns.\nYoung Pyromancer\nLow-cost payoff that converts instants and sorceries into creature tokens.\nHow to Build a Spellslinger Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 25-35 instants and sorceries so payoff permanents trigger every turn cycle\n✓Include 6-10 payoffs like Guttersnipe, Talrand, and Archmage Emeritus that reward each cast\n✓Pack 8-12 cheap cantrips such as Opt, Brainstorm, and Frantic Search to keep the engine moving\n✓Add cost reducers and Treasure makers like Storm-Kiln Artist to cast multiple spells per turn\nDeck Structure\n✓Keep the mana curve low so most spells cost one to three mana\n✓Include Mystic Sanctuary effects or flashback spells to rebuy your best casts\n✓Reserve 8-10 slots for interaction that doubles as engine fuel\n✓Use copy effects and rituals to stretch turns without lowering the spell count\nWin Conditions\n✓Use burn payoffs to convert repeated casts into table-wide damage\n✓Build a board through Drakes, Elementals, or other spell-made tokens\n✓Close with copied finishers or a large X spell when your resources are ahead\nWeaknesses of Spellslinger Decks\n⚠Payoff creatures like Guttersnipe and Archmage Emeritus are fragile, and the deck can stall when they are removed.\n⚠Spellslinger decks can struggle to rebuild after discard or effects that stop extra draw.\n⚠Taxing pieces that raise spell costs can make cheap cantrips and interaction much harder to chain.\n⚠The deck may affect the battlefield slowly, inviting attacks before its payoffs are online.\nIs Spellslinger Right for You?\nSpellslinger fits players who like holding up mana, playing on the stack, and having useful decisions on each turn. The basic loop is accessible, but sequencing cantrips, removal, and protection rewards practice. Budget builds work well because many cantrips and payoff creatures are inexpensive.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nStorm\nStorm chains one large turn; Spellslinger earns steady value from each cast.\nCombo\nCombo assembles fixed pieces; Spellslinger wins by repeating cast triggers.\nControl\nControl answers first; Spellslinger turns answers and cantrips into proactive triggers.\nCard Draw\nCard Draw makes drawing the engine; Spellslinger spends cards to trigger spell payoffs.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Spellslinger CommandersCantripsBurn\nTop Spellslinger Commanders\nStorm, Force of Nature\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerStormSpell Copy+7 more\n$23.80TCGPlayer\n€20.93CardMarket\nStella Lee, Wild Card\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerComboStorm+7 more\n$5.05TCGPlayer\n€3.70CardMarket\nVivi Ornitier\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerStormBurn+7 more\n$34.43TCGPlayer\n€30.58CardMarket\nVeyran, Voice of Duality\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerSpell CopyStorm+7 more\n$2.39TCGPlayer\n€3.28CardMarket\nFire Lord Azula\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerComboSpell Copy+7 more\n$1.16TCGPlayer\n€1.05CardMarket\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nMV: 6\nSpellslingerWheelsCombo+7 more\n$5.27TCGPlayer\n€2.87CardMarket\nAlania, Divergent Storm\nMV: 5\nSpellslingerOttersProwess+7 more\n$0.35TCGPlayer\n€0.21CardMarket\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nControlSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$5.35TCGPlayer\n€2.75CardMarket\nBria, Riptide Rogue\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerProwessWizards+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nGhyrson Starn, Kelermorph\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nBurnSpellslingerPingers+7 more\n$33.42TCGPlayer\n€27.79CardMarket\nEluge, the Shoreless Sea\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerControlExtra Turns+7 more\n$4.99TCGPlayer\n€3.57CardMarket\nRiku of Many Paths\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerTokens+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$0.26TCGPlayer\n€0.10CardMarket\nKuja, Genome Sorcerer // Trance Kuja, Fate Defied\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nBurnSpellslingerWizards+7 more\n$1.50TCGPlayer\n€0.96CardMarket\nFeather, the Redeemed\nMV: 3\nSunforgerSpellslingerVoltron+7 more\n$0.99TCGPlayer\n€0.60CardMarket\nKykar, Wind's Fury\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerTokensPolymorph+7 more\n$0.46TCGPlayer\n€0.56CardMarket\nMizzix of the Izmagnus\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerX SpellsStorm+7 more\n$1.15TCGPlayer\n€0.44CardMarket\nAnhelo, the Painter\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerSpell CopyTokens+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nKalamax, the Stormsire\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerSpell CopyCombo+7 more\n$0.76TCGPlayer\n€0.50CardMarket\nNarset, Enlightened Exile\nMV: 4\nProwessSpellslingerTokens+7 more\n$7.63TCGPlayer\n€7.53CardMarket\nKess, Dissident Mage\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerComboReanimator+7 more\n$3.83TCGPlayer\n€3.03CardMarket\nZethi, Arcane Blademaster\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerControlCantrips+7 more\n$2.82TCGPlayer\n€0.95CardMarket\nLord of the Nazgûl\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerCantripsTokens+7 more\n$4.07TCGPlayer\n€4.03CardMarket\nCodie, Vociferous Codex\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerComboBig Mana+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.19CardMarket\nIroh, Grand Lotus\nMV: 6Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerLessonsStorm+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.31CardMarket\nJudith, Carnage Connoisseur\nMV: 5\nBurnSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Spellslinger commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Spellslinger commander?\nThe best spellslinger commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you fill your deck with cheap instants and sorceries that matter in many game states.\nHow do Spellslinger decks win?\nSpellslinger decks win through a combination of Archmage Emeritus, Guttersnipe, Talrand, Sky Summoner, and other synergistic pieces that spellslinger decks are for players who want each instant and sorcery to advance the board, refill the hand, or pressure life totals.\nWhat are the best colors for Spellslinger?\nIzzet combines blue cantrips and counters with red burn, copy effects, and impulse draw. However, Grixis adds tutors, recursion, and drain so spell chains can find and reuse key pieces.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Spellslinger decks?\nPayoff creatures like Guttersnipe and Archmage Emeritus are fragile, and the deck can stall when they are removed. Spellslinger decks can struggle to rebuild after discard or effects that stop extra draw. Taxing pieces that raise spell costs can make cheap cantrips and interaction much harder to chain. The deck may affect the battlefield slowly, inviting attacks before its payoffs are online.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,stax,page_text,10867,"Best Stax Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nStax\nBack to SearchBest Stax Commanders for EDH\nStax decks are for players who want to win by deciding which resources matter before opponents can use them. This strategy wants to tax mana, cards, combat, and casting windows, using stax commanders and resource denial EDH pieces like Winter Orb and Smokestack, to win through a board state where your deck still functions and opponents cannot rebuild. Unlike Control, which answers threats after they appear, Stax locks the costs and rules of the game so many threats never get cast. The best stax commanders break parity, then convert the slowed table into combat damage, attrition, or a compact combo.\nBest Colors for Stax Decks\nMono-white supplies Thalia effects, hatebears, Rule of Law pieces, and clean taxes that slow every opponent.\nAzorius pairs white taxes with blue card draw and counterspells to protect a parity-breaking lock.\nOrzhov adds discard, sacrifice pressure, and drain so the resource squeeze turns into a clock.\nHow Do Stax Decks Win in Commander?\nStax decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy taxes and lock pieces before opponents spend their best turns.\n✓ You build around your own restrictions with mana rocks tokens untappers or commander discounts.\n✓ You sequence resource denial so each opponent rebuilds slower than you do.\n✓ You protect the lock with removal for the few threats that slip through.\n✓ You finish with attrition combat damage or a compact combo before the table resets.\nBest Cards for Stax EDH Decks\nWinter Orb\nRestricts untaps so mana stays scarce while your rocks, untappers, or commander break parity.\nSmokestack\nTurns expendable permanents into a sacrifice clock that grinds opponents out of real resources.\nGrand Arbiter Augustin IV\nTaxes opposing spells and discounts yours, giving Azorius stax a built-in parity break.\nDrannith Magistrate\nStops commanders and exile casting, cutting off key Commander resources from the command zone.\nThalia, Guardian of Thraben\nAdds an early tax to noncreature spells and slows fast spell-heavy starts.\nHow to Build a Stax Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 8-12 tax or lock pieces such as Winter Orb, Static Orb, Sphere of Resistance, and Thalia effects\n✓Include 4-6 parity breakers that make your commander, rocks, tokens, or untappers work through the lock\n✓Use mana rocks and Treasure sources that operate under land untap restrictions and sphere taxes\n✓Choose a clear win condition before adding more denial pieces\nBreaking Parity\n✓Favor permanents over spell chains so Rule of Law effects slow opponents more than you\n✓Pair Winter Orb and Static Orb with untappers, vigilance, or artifact mana\n✓Feed Smokestack with tokens, Treasures, Clues, or recursive permanents instead of real cards\n✓Sequence softer taxes first when you need the table to keep playing\nWin Conditions\n✓Attack with a resilient commander once taxes keep blockers and removal scarce\n✓Deploy a two-card combo after opponents are locked out of efficient interaction\n✓Grind through Smokestack or forced sacrifice after opponents run low on permanents\n✓Do not stop at the lock; include enough finishers to end stalled games\nWeaknesses of Stax Decks\n⚠Stax can struggle when opponents ramp ahead before the first taxes resolve.\n⚠Artifact and enchantment removal can dismantle the lock one piece at a time.\n⚠Without a parity breaker, your own restrictions can slow you as much as the table.\n⚠The strategy carries a real table-feel cost because many pods dislike playing under locks.\nIs Stax Right for You?\nStax fits patient players who enjoy sequencing constraints and building around awkward rules. It has a higher social cost than most archetypes, so talk with the pod before bringing hard locks to casual games. Start with soft taxes and a visible win condition before moving into heavier resource denial.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nControl\nControl answers threats after they appear; Stax taxes resources so many threats never resolve.\nCombo\nCombo can finish behind locks; Stax uses denial first, then a compact endgame.\nArtifacts\nArtifacts supply many lock pieces, but artifacts decks build engines beyond denial.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Stax CommandersDiscardTheft\nTop Stax Commanders\nGrand Arbiter Augustin IV\nMV: 4\nStaxControlHatebears+7 more\n$19.85TCGPlayer\n€10.90CardMarket\nWinota, Joiner of Forces\nMV: 4\nHatebearsAggroStax+7 more\n$4.34TCGPlayer\n€4.46CardMarket\nZur the Enchanter\nMV: 4\nEnchantressAurasStax+7 more\n$1.55TCGPlayer\n€0.71CardMarket\nUrza, Lord High Artificer\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$16.28TCGPlayer\n€14.42CardMarket\nLavinia, Azorius Renegade\nMV: 2\nStaxControlHatebears+7 more\n$0.73TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nControlSpellslingerLifegain+7 more\n$5.35TCGPlayer\n€2.75CardMarket\nDerevi, Empyrial Tactician\nMV: 3\nBirthing PodBirdsCombo+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nLiesa, Shroud of Dusk\nMV: 5\nLifegainLifedrainHatebears+7 more\n$0.91TCGPlayer\n€0.97CardMarket\nEllivere of the Wild Court\nMV: 4\nAurasEnchantressHatebears+7 more\n$0.42TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nOloro, Ageless Ascetic\nMV: 6\nLifegainControlCombo+7 more\n$18.51TCGPlayer\n€11.21CardMarket\nThalia and The Gitrog Monster\nMV: 4\nLands MatterLandfallHatebears+7 more\n$6.02TCGPlayer\n€3.19CardMarket\nMagda, Brazen Outlaw\nMV: 2\nTreasureDwarvesArtifacts+7 more\n$8.78TCGPlayer\n€6.70CardMarket\nTayam, Luminous Enigma\nMV: 4\n+1/+1 CountersComboReanimator+7 more\n$3.31TCGPlayer\n€1.38CardMarket\nSen Triplets\nMV: 5\nTheftControlStax+7 more\n$8.58TCGPlayer\n€3.30CardMarket\nHeliod, Sun-Crowned\nMV: 3\nLifegain+1/+1 CountersStax+7 more\n$22.28TCGPlayer\n€16.39CardMarket\nSythis, Harvest's Hand\nMV: 2\nEnchantressAurasLifegain+7 more\n$4.32TCGPlayer\n€2.83CardMarket\nHylda of the Icy Crown\nMV: 4\nControlTap / UntapSnow+7 more\n$3.55TCGPlayer\n€1.96CardMarket\nBrago, King Eternal\nMV: 4\nBlinkControlCombo+7 more\n$3.13TCGPlayer\n€2.23CardMarket\nShorikai, Genesis Engine\nMV: 4\nVehiclesArtifactsControl+7 more\n$2.86TCGPlayer\n€2.66CardMarket\nHokori, Dust Drinker\nMV: 4\nStaxHatebearsArtifacts+7 more\n$1.91TCGPlayer\n€0.96CardMarket\nMyrel, Shield of Argive\nMV: 4\nSoldiersTokensAggro+7 more\n$18.47TCGPlayer\n€16.11CardMarket\nJorn, God of Winter // Kaldring, the Rimestaff\nMV: 3\nSnowStaxRamp+7 more\n$0.33TCGPlayer\n€0.29CardMarket\nKudo, King Among Bears\nMV: 2\n+1/+1 CountersTokensBears+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.18CardMarket\nElesh Norn, Mother of Machines\nMV: 5\nBlinkTokensETB+7 more\n$25.34TCGPlayer\n€21.01CardMarket\nSlicer, Hired Muscle // Slicer, High-Speed Antagonist\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$7.70TCGPlayer\n€5.59CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Stax commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Stax commander?\nThe best stax commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy taxes and lock pieces before opponents spend their best turns.\nHow do Stax decks win?\nStax decks win through a combination of Winter Orb, Smokestack, Grand Arbiter Augustin IV, and other synergistic pieces that stax decks are for players who want to win by deciding which resources matter before opponents can use them.\nWhat are the best colors for Stax?\nMono-white supplies Thalia effects, hatebears, Rule of Law pieces, and clean taxes that slow every opponent. However, Azorius pairs white taxes with blue card draw and counterspells to protect a parity-breaking lock.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Stax decks?\nStax can struggle when opponents ramp ahead before the first taxes resolve. Artifact and enchantment removal can dismantle the lock one piece at a time. Without a parity breaker, your own restrictions can slow you as much as the table. The strategy carries a real table-feel cost because many pods dislike playing under locks.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,storm,page_text,10928,"Best Storm Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nStorm\nBack to SearchBest Storm Commanders for EDH\nStorm decks are for players who want to sculpt a hand, pick one decisive turn, and chain cheap spells until the count becomes lethal. This strategy wants to cast many spells in one turn, using cantrips, rituals, cost reducers, and storm commanders, to win through Grapeshot, Tendrils of Agony, Aetherflux Reservoir, or another storm-count payoff. In the Spellslinger, Storm, Big Mana, and Combo cluster, Spellslinger grinds value from each instant or sorcery, Big Mana spends on fewer huge spells, Combo assembles fixed pieces, and Storm turns spell volume into the win. The best storm combo EDH decks reward careful sequencing because every card has to convert into mana, cards, count, or a protected finish.\nBest Colors for Storm Decks\nIzzet pairs blue cantrips and copy effects with red rituals, burn payoffs, and cost reduction.\nGrixis adds black tutors, graveyard recursion, and Tendrils of Agony lines to Izzet storm shells.\nMono-red leans on rituals, impulse draw, Treasure, and commanders that refund mana as spells resolve.\nHow Do Storm Decks Win in Commander?\nStorm decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You sculpt your hand with cantrips and hold rituals until one turn can carry the chain.\n✓ You count mana before committing because each spell must feed the next cast.\n✓ You use cost reducers commanders and rituals to convert small spells into more mana and cards.\n✓ You choose a payoff only when the count and protection are ready.\n✓ You finish with Grapeshot Tendrils of Agony Aetherflux Reservoir or another storm route.\nBest Cards for Storm EDH Decks\nGrapeshot\nCopies itself for each prior spell, converting storm count into direct damage.\nAetherflux Reservoir\nConverts a long spell chain into life, then spends fifty life on damage.\nJeska's Will\nCombines mana and impulse draw, often starting the turn that chains spells.\nMind's Desire\nConverts storm count into free card access from the top of your library.\nTendrils of Agony\nTurns each storm copy into life drain for black-based storm shells.\nHow to Build a Storm Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 25-35 cheap instants and sorceries, mostly one or two mana, so the chain has enough casts\n✓Include rituals like Jeska's Will, Pyretic Ritual, and Cabal Ritual to gain mana mid-turn\n✓Use cost reducers and per-cast mana engines such as Birgi, God of Storytelling\n✓Pack cantrips like Ponder, Preordain, and Brainstorm to dig toward payoffs\nEnablers & Refuel\n✓Use wheel effects like Windfall to refill your hand while raising storm count\n✓Add copy effects and magecraft triggers when they convert each cast into cards or mana\n✓Run a few tutors to find the payoff once count and mana are ready\n✓Keep recursion like Past in Flames to rebuild after a stalled chain\nWin Conditions\n✓Use Grapeshot or Tendrils of Agony to convert storm count into damage or drain\n✓Let Aetherflux Reservoir turn repeated casts into life and a direct finish\n✓Use Mind's Desire or Brain Freeze when your list supports card-access or mill routes\n✓Run two or three payoffs so one answer does not strand the whole turn\nWeaknesses of Storm Decks\n⚠A single counterspell or removal spell aimed at the payoff can strand a full turn of setup.\n⚠Rule of Law, Thalia-style taxes, and graveyard hate can break the chain before it starts.\n⚠The deck often spends several turns visibly sculpting, which can draw pressure before it is ready.\n⚠Long combo turns can test casual tables if the pod did not agree to that pace.\nIs Storm Right for You?\nStorm has a high skill floor because you track mana, cards, storm count, and protection through one long turn. It suits players who like sequencing puzzles and are willing to practice lines before a pod game. Budget versions can work, but clean play matters more than expensive finishers.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nSpellslinger\nSpellslinger grinds each cast for value; Storm banks casts for one lethal turn.\nCombo\nCombo assembles fixed pieces; Storm needs enough mana, cards, and spell count.\nBig Mana\nBig Mana casts fewer huge spells; Storm chains many cheap ones.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Storm CommandersCantripsWheels\nTop Storm Commanders\nStorm, Force of Nature\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerStormSpell Copy+7 more\n$23.80TCGPlayer\n€20.93CardMarket\nVivi Ornitier\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerStormBurn+7 more\n$34.43TCGPlayer\n€30.58CardMarket\nStella Lee, Wild Card\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerComboStorm+7 more\n$5.05TCGPlayer\n€3.70CardMarket\nPrismari, the Inspiration\nMV: 7\nStormSpellslingerBurn+7 more\n$3.52TCGPlayer\n€3.62CardMarket\nRal, Monsoon Mage // Ral, Leyline Prodigy\nMV: 2\nSpellslingerStormCombo+7 more\n$6.86TCGPlayer\n€4.20CardMarket\nFlubs, the Fool\nMV: 3\nLands MatterCheeriosLandfall+7 more\n$2.89TCGPlayer\n€0.61CardMarket\nZada, Hedron Grinder\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerGoblinsTokens+7 more\n$0.17TCGPlayer\n€0.12CardMarket\nVeyran, Voice of Duality\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerSpell CopyStorm+7 more\n$2.39TCGPlayer\n€3.28CardMarket\nJhoira, Weatherlight Captain\nMV: 4\nArtifactsStormCheerios+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.89CardMarket\nFire Lord Azula\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerComboSpell Copy+7 more\n$1.16TCGPlayer\n€1.05CardMarket\nUrza, Lord High Artificer\nMV: 4\nArtifactsComboControl+7 more\n$16.28TCGPlayer\n€14.42CardMarket\nIroh, Grand Lotus\nMV: 6Universes Beyond\nSpellslingerLessonsStorm+7 more\n$0.39TCGPlayer\n€0.31CardMarket\nKykar, Wind's Fury\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerTokensPolymorph+7 more\n$0.46TCGPlayer\n€0.56CardMarket\nAlania, Divergent Storm\nMV: 5\nSpellslingerOttersProwess+7 more\n$0.35TCGPlayer\n€0.21CardMarket\nUrabrask // The Great Work\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerBurnStorm+7 more\n$15.81TCGPlayer\n€14.23CardMarket\nGhyrson Starn, Kelermorph\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nBurnSpellslingerPingers+7 more\n$33.42TCGPlayer\n€27.79CardMarket\nK'rrik, Son of Yawgmoth\nMV: 7\nLifegainComboSelf-Damage+7 more\n$1.12TCGPlayer\n€0.65CardMarket\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun\nMV: 6\nSpellslingerWheelsCombo+7 more\n$5.27TCGPlayer\n€2.87CardMarket\nAeve, Progenitor Ooze\nMV: 5\nOozes+1/+1 CountersStorm+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.16CardMarket\nBria, Riptide Rogue\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerProwessWizards+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nElsha of the Infinite\nMV: 5\nComboSpellslingerStorm+7 more\n$0.66TCGPlayer\n€0.31CardMarket\nOb Nixilis, Captive Kingpin\nMV: 4\nCombo+1/+1 CountersBurn+7 more\n$15.49TCGPlayer\n€13.39CardMarket\nAshling, Flame Dancer\nMV: 4\nSpell CopySpellslingerBurn+7 more\n$5.63TCGPlayer\n€6.35CardMarket\nVadrik, Astral Archmage\nMV: 3\nSpellslingerTreasureStorm+7 more\n$0.34TCGPlayer\n€0.16CardMarket\nMizzix of the Izmagnus\nMV: 4\nSpellslingerX SpellsStorm+7 more\n$1.15TCGPlayer\n€0.44CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Storm commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Storm commander?\nThe best storm commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you sculpt your hand with cantrips and hold rituals until one turn can carry the chain.\nHow do Storm decks win?\nStorm decks win through a combination of Grapeshot, Aetherflux Reservoir, Jeska's Will, and other synergistic pieces that storm decks are for players who want to sculpt a hand, pick one decisive turn, and chain cheap spells until the count becomes lethal.\nWhat are the best colors for Storm?\nIzzet pairs blue cantrips and copy effects with red rituals, burn payoffs, and cost reduction. However, Grixis adds black tutors, graveyard recursion, and Tendrils of Agony lines to Izzet storm shells.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Storm decks?\nA single counterspell or removal spell aimed at the payoff can strand a full turn of setup. Rule of Law, Thalia-style taxes, and graveyard hate can break the chain before it starts. The deck often spends several turns visibly sculpting, which can draw pressure before it is ready. Long combo turns can test casual tables if the pod did not agree to that pace.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,tokens,page_text,11241,"Best Token Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nTokens\nBack to SearchBest Tokens Commanders for EDH\nToken decks are for players who want one card to become many bodies and then decide whether those bodies attack, block, draw cards, or fuel another engine. This strategy wants to flood the battlefield with creatures, using token commanders, repeatable generators, and anthem effects, to win through a go wide attack or by converting the swarm into damage, drain, or mana. Tokens is different from Aristocrats because making bodies comes first, from Counters because it grows wide instead of tall, and from Voltron because it spreads pressure across many creatures. The best token decks EDH players build reward patience against sweepers and careful timing on the finishing turn.\nBest Colors for Tokens Decks\nSelesnya pairs white generators and anthems with green doublers and overrun finishers.\nOrzhov blends tokens with aristocrats payoffs, draining opponents as bodies enter and die.\nGruul makes hasty tokens and converts a fast board into combat pressure.\nIzzet creates tokens from spell casts and copies, linking swarms to spellslinger turns.\nHow Do Tokens Decks Win in Commander?\nTokens decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You deploy repeatable token generators early to establish a wide board.\n✓ You convert spells attacks and triggers into extra bodies through doubling effects.\n✓ You grow the swarm with anthems and overrun effects when attacks matter.\n✓ You hold back a generator so a board wipe does not end your game.\n✓ You close by going wide or by converting tokens into damage mana or cards.\nBest Cards for Tokens EDH Decks\nDoubling Season\nDoubles token creation so repeatable generators scale faster.\nAnointed Procession\nWhite token doubler that supports go-wide boards at four mana.\nParallel Lives\nGreen token doubler that stacks with other token multipliers.\nCraterhoof Behemoth\nConverts a wide board into a lethal trample attack.\nSkullclamp\nTurns small expendable tokens into repeatable card draw.\nHow to Build a Tokens Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 10-14 repeatable token generators, favoring permanents like Tendershoot Dryad over one-shot spells\n✓Include 2-4 token doublers such as Anointed Procession, Parallel Lives, or Mondrak, Glory Dominus\n✓Add 4-6 anthem or buff effects so small tokens pressure life totals\n✓Use Skullclamp plus 6-8 draw engines that reward having many creatures\n✓Play 8-10 ramp pieces because generators and finishers need mana\nWin Conditions\n✓Use overrun effects like Craterhoof Behemoth or Overwhelming Stampede for one large attack\n✓Play drain payoffs such as Impact Tremors or Purphoros, God of the Forge to skip combat\n✓Add sacrifice payoffs that convert the swarm into damage or card advantage\n✓Use extra combat spells after anthem effects make the board dangerous\nProtecting the Swarm\n✓Run 3-5 board wipe answers like Heroic Intervention, Flawless Maneuver, or Selfless Spirit\n✓Prefer generators that survive sweepers, such as planeswalkers, enchantments, and lands\n✓Hold one generator in hand to rebuild after a sweeper\n✓Avoid committing every payoff into open mana from control-heavy opponents\nWeaknesses of Tokens Decks\n⚠Token decks can struggle against repeated board wipes because one sweeper can erase many turns of development.\n⚠Individual tokens are small, so large blockers or Propaganda effects can stall attacks.\n⚠The strategy leans on doublers and anthems, and targeted removal of those pieces slows the clock.\n⚠Going wide is visible, so the table may aim removal at you before the finishing turn.\nIs Tokens Right for You?\nTokens is friendly for newer Commander players because the board state shows your progress clearly and many generators are inexpensive. The main skill test is learning when to hold back against sweepers instead of committing every card. It suits players who enjoy building a board and choosing the right turn to make many creatures matter.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nAristocrats\nTokens makes the bodies; Aristocrats sacrifices them for death triggers and drain.\nSacrifice\nSacrifice uses tokens as fodder, while Tokens values the swarm itself.\n+1/+1 Counters\nCounters grows creatures taller over time; Tokens spreads power across many bodies.\nAggro\nAggro attacks early with pressure; Tokens builds an engine before the big attack.\nBlink\nBlink reuses token makers, but it values enter triggers more than the bodies.\nProliferate\nProliferate multiplies counters on tokens, adding growth to a wide board.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Tokens CommandersAristocratsExtra Combats\nTop Tokens Commanders\nBaylen, the Haymaker\nMV: 3\nTokensRabbits+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$1.13TCGPlayer\n€0.77CardMarket\nChatterfang, Squirrel General\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$11.09TCGPlayer\n€7.02CardMarket\nCaesar, Legion's Emperor\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nTokensAristocratsHumans+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nMarneus Calgar\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nTokensComboAristocrats+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nKrenko, Mob Boss\nMV: 4\nGoblinsTokensAggro+7 more\n$2.80TCGPlayer\n€1.72CardMarket\nArabella, Abandoned Doll\nMV: 2\nTokensAggroBurn+7 more\n$0.85TCGPlayer\n€0.28CardMarket\nHazel of the Rootbloom\nMV: 4\nTokensAristocratsSquirrels+7 more\n$3.22TCGPlayer\n€4.20CardMarket\nEdgar Markov\nMV: 6\nVampiresLifegainTokens+7 more\n$43.55TCGPlayer\n€35.69CardMarket\nZinnia, Valley's Voice\nMV: 3\nTokensOffspringClones+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.48CardMarket\nZurgo Stormrender\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsAggro+7 more\n$4.58TCGPlayer\n€3.43CardMarket\nLathril, Blade of the Elves\nMV: 4\nElvesTokensAggro+7 more\n$1.78TCGPlayer\n€1.45CardMarket\nAdrix and Nev, Twincasters\nMV: 4\nTokensClones+1/+1 Counters+7 more\n$8.61TCGPlayer\n€6.33CardMarket\nJetmir, Nexus of Revels\nMV: 4\nTokensAggroCats+7 more\n$9.90TCGPlayer\n€9.54CardMarket\nRin and Seri, Inseparable\nMV: 4\nCatsTokensDogs+7 more\n$26.49TCGPlayer\n€20.21CardMarket\nTeysa Karlov\nMV: 4\nAristocratsTokensSacrifice+7 more\n$2.65TCGPlayer\n€1.68CardMarket\nThe Necrobloom\nMV: 4\nLands MatterTokensDredge+7 more\n$0.36TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nKambal, Profiteering Mayor\nMV: 3\nTokensAristocratsLifegain+7 more\n$5.52TCGPlayer\n€4.79CardMarket\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One\nMV: 3\nAggroTokensAttack Triggers+7 more\n$3.76TCGPlayer\n€2.99CardMarket\nJinnie Fay, Jetmir's Second\nMV: 3\nTokensCatsTreasure+7 more\n$0.69TCGPlayer\n€0.49CardMarket\nBrudiclad, Telchor Engineer\nMV: 6\nTokensArtifactsClones+7 more\n$0.54TCGPlayer\n€0.38CardMarket\nMr. House, President and CEO\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nTreasureTokensArtifacts+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nXyris, the Writhing Storm\nMV: 5\nWheelsTokensGroup Hug+7 more\n$2.10TCGPlayer\n€0.97CardMarket\nBrenard, Ginger Sculptor\nMV: 4\nTokensBirthing PodArtifacts+7 more\n$1.17TCGPlayer\n€1.71CardMarket\nTeval, the Balanced Scale\nMV: 4\nReanimatorLands MatterMill+7 more\n$5.57TCGPlayer\n€6.42CardMarket\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver\nMV: 4\nZombiesTokensAristocrats+7 more\n$9.30TCGPlayer\n€10.24CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Tokens commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Tokens commander?\nThe best tokens commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you deploy repeatable token generators early to establish a wide board.\nHow do Tokens decks win?\nTokens decks win through a combination of Doubling Season, Anointed Procession, Parallel Lives, and other synergistic pieces that token decks are for players who want one card to become many bodies and then decide whether those bodies attack, block, draw cards, or fuel another engine.\nWhat are the best colors for Tokens?\nSelesnya pairs white generators and anthems with green doublers and overrun finishers. However, Orzhov blends tokens with aristocrats payoffs, draining opponents as bodies enter and die.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Tokens decks?\nToken decks can struggle against repeated board wipes because one sweeper can erase many turns of development. Individual tokens are small, so large blockers or Propaganda effects can stall attacks. The strategy leans on doublers and anthems, and targeted removal of those pieces slows the clock. Going wide is visible, so the table may aim removal at you before the finishing turn.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  em,voltron,page_text,11789,"Best Voltron Commanders for EDH | EDHMatch\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH\nEDHMatchFind commanders that match how you play\nHome\nFind CommandersCommander QuizCommander DNAWhat Should I Build Next?Advanced SearchCollectionsColor Combinations\nTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by SetBrowse All\nDiscover PlaystylePlayer Type QuizGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nSearch\nSearch\nHome\nFind Commanders\nCommander Quiz\nCommander DNA\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nAdvanced Search\nCollections\nColor CombinationsTwo-Color Guilds\nAzorius\nDimir\nRakdos\nGruul\nSelesnya\nOrzhov\nIzzet\nGolgari\nBoros\nSimic\nThree-Color Combinations\nJund\nEsper\nGrixis\nBant\nNaya\nAbzan\nJeskai\nSultai\nMardu\nTemur\nMono-Color\nMono-White\nMono-Blue\nMono-Black\nMono-Red\nMono-Green\nColorless\nColorless\nBrowse by Set\nBrowse All\nDiscover Playstyle\nPlayer Type Quiz\nGuild Quiz\nStrategies\nShortlist\nBlog\nNewsletter\nHome\nStrategies\nVoltron\nBack to SearchBest Voltron Commanders for EDH\nVoltron decks are for players who want the game to revolve around one protected attacker and the timing of each combat step. This strategy wants to build one creature, usually a commander, using equipment and auras for power, evasion, and protection, to win through 21 commander damage on each opponent. Unlike Tokens or Aggro, Voltron does not spread pressure across many bodies, and unlike broad Equipment decks the gear is focused on one threat rather than a wider artifact engine. The best Voltron commanders make commander damage decks feel like a resource puzzle: suit up, connect, protect the threat, then rebuild if the table answers it.\nBest Colors for Voltron Decks\nBoros turns red-white aggression into equipment cost reducers, double strike, and extra combats.\nAzorius pairs white auras with blue evasion and counterspells to keep one attacker connecting.\nSelesnya supports aura Voltron with hexproof bodies, enchantment draw, and combat-sized growth.\nIzzet uses prowess-style commanders, spell protection, and double strike to turn casts into damage.\nHow Do Voltron Decks Win in Commander?\nVoltron decks excel through a specific playstyle and win conditions. Here's how they work:\n✓ You choose one creature usually your commander and make each card support its attacks.\n✓ You add equipment and auras that convert mana into power evasion and protection.\n✓ You track commander damage for each opponent and pick attacks that can connect.\n✓ You hold protection so removal and bounce do not reset your whole turn.\n✓ You rebuild after answers by reusing equipment or recasting the commander.\n✓ You close by removing players one at a time through oversized combat damage.\nBest Cards for Voltron EDH Decks\nColossus Hammer\nAdds ten power cheaply when equip cost reducers or instant-speed attachment effects are online.\nSwiftfoot Boots\nGives haste and hexproof so the suited creature can attack before removal catches up.\nAll That Glitters\nTurns a board of artifacts and enchantments into a large power boost.\nSword of Feast and Famine\nAdds relevant protection and untaps lands so you can attack while keeping interaction available.\nRogue's Passage\nTurns spare mana into an unblockable commander damage attack from a land slot.\nHow to Build a Voltron Commander Deck\nCore Components\n✓Run 12-18 equipment or auras across power boosts, evasion, and protection\n✓Include 4-6 attachment helpers like Sigarda's Aid, Puresteel Paladin, or Hammer of Nazahn\n✓Add 6-8 protection spells such as Flawless Maneuver, Teferi's Protection, or Snakeskin Veil\n✓Use Steelshaper's Gift, Open the Armory, and similar tutors to find the right attachment\n✓Keep 8-10 ramp pieces so you can suit up while leaving protection mana open\nChoosing the Threat\n✓Favor commanders with evasion, ward, hexproof, or built-in attachment cost reduction\n✓Use aura packages with commanders that are hard to target because auras leave with the creature\n✓Lean on equipment when your commander is fragile because gear remains after removal\n✓Treat double strike as damage acceleration because it moves the 21 damage clock much faster\nWin Conditions\n✓Track commander damage separately for each opponent because life totals do not tell the whole story\n✓Use Rogue's Passage, flying, menace, or protection to force key attacks through blockers\n✓Add extra combat spells when your commander can remove more than one player in a turn\n✓Include a backup attacker so commander tax does not strand every attachment\nWeaknesses of Voltron Decks\n⚠Voltron puts most resources into one creature, so repeated removal and commander tax can exhaust its mana.\n⚠Sacrifice edicts and mass bounce can answer the threat without targeting through hexproof or protection.\n⚠The deck often removes players one at a time, which gives the rest of the table time to coordinate.\n⚠Aura-heavy builds can lose several cards when the creature dies, making rebuilds harder than equipment builds.\nIs Voltron Right for You?\nVoltron is approachable because the plan is visible on one creature and the commander gives the deck a reliable focal point. The main skill is sequencing attacks, protection, and rebuilds so you do not spend every resource into one answer. It suits players who enjoy combat math, clear pressure, and commander damage as a win route.\nRelated Commander Strategies\nEquipment\nEquipment can support many bodies; Voltron commits the gear to one commander damage threat.\n+1/+1 Counters\nCounters spreads permanent growth; Voltron stacks temporary and reusable buffs on one attacker.\nEnchantress\nAura Voltron uses enchantments for damage; Enchantress uses them as a draw engine.\nInfect\nInfect can use one attacker too, but it wins through ten poison instead of commander damage.\nBrowse Commander Lists\nTop Voltron CommandersAurasExtra Combats\nTop Voltron Commanders\nCloud, Ex-SOLDIER\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$1.44TCGPlayer\n€0.73CardMarket\nLight-Paws, Emperor's Voice\nMV: 2\nAurasVoltronEnchantress+7 more\n$1.66TCGPlayer\n€4.99CardMarket\nKotis, the Fangkeeper\nMV: 4\nVoltronTheftEquipment+7 more\n$0.43TCGPlayer\n€0.21CardMarket\nCaptain America, First Avenger\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$31.03TCGPlayer\n€16.64CardMarket\nLightning, Army of One\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronExtra Combats+7 more\n$9.59TCGPlayer\n€8.55CardMarket\nSkullbriar, the Walking Grave\nMV: 2\n+1/+1 CountersVoltronAggro+7 more\n$2.13TCGPlayer\n€0.47CardMarket\nTifa Lockhart\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nLands MatterVoltronRamp+7 more\n$2.51TCGPlayer\n€1.86CardMarket\nWolverine, Best There Is\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\n+1/+1 CountersFightVoltron+7 more\n$28.96TCGPlayer\n€13.67CardMarket\nFeather, the Redeemed\nMV: 3\nSunforgerSpellslingerVoltron+7 more\n$0.99TCGPlayer\n€0.60CardMarket\nUril, the Miststalker\nMV: 5\nAurasVoltronEnchantress+7 more\n$1.14TCGPlayer\n€0.73CardMarket\nAnti-Venom, Horrifying Healer\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nVoltron+1/+1 CountersEquipment+7 more\n$6.49TCGPlayer\n€3.68CardMarket\nAlexios, Deimos of Kosmos\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$0.58TCGPlayer\n€0.33CardMarket\nRafiq of the Many\nMV: 4\nVoltronAurasExalted+7 more\n$0.31TCGPlayer\n€0.13CardMarket\nDogmeat, Ever Loyal\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nGalea, Kindler of Hope\nMV: 4\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\nN/ATCGPlayer\nN/ACardMarket\nCloud, Midgar Mercenary\nMV: 2Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronArtifacts+7 more\n$21.14TCGPlayer\n€22.67CardMarket\nSquall, SeeD Mercenary\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nReanimatorVoltronEquipment+7 more\n$1.09TCGPlayer\n€0.59CardMarket\nJin Sakai, Ghost of Tsushima\nMV: 4Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$101.01TCGPlayer\n€89.25CardMarket\nSergeant John Benton\nMV: 3Universes Beyond\nVoltronAggroGroup Hug+7 more\n$1.26TCGPlayer\n€1.18CardMarket\nSyr Gwyn, Hero of Ashvale\nMV: 6\nEquipmentKnightsVoltron+7 more\n$2.88TCGPlayer\n€3.10CardMarket\nSlicer, Hired Muscle // Slicer, High-Speed Antagonist\nMV: 5Universes Beyond\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$7.70TCGPlayer\n€5.59CardMarket\nZurgo Helmsmasher\nMV: 5\nEquipmentVoltronAggro+7 more\n$0.53TCGPlayer\n€0.40CardMarket\nObeka, Splitter of Seconds\nMV: 4\nMonarchExtra UpkeepsVoltron+7 more\n$0.25TCGPlayer\n€0.23CardMarket\nArna Kennerüd, Skycaptain\nMV: 5\nEquipmentAurasVoltron+7 more\n$0.40TCGPlayer\n€0.42CardMarket\nZur the Enchanter\nMV: 4\nEnchantressAurasStax+7 more\n$1.55TCGPlayer\n€0.71CardMarket\nFilter and browse all Voltron commanders →\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat makes a good Voltron commander?\nThe best voltron commanders typically provide consistent access to the strategy's core mechanics. Look for commanders that you choose one creature usually your commander and make each card support its attacks.\nHow do Voltron decks win?\nVoltron decks win through a combination of Colossus Hammer, Swiftfoot Boots, All That Glitters, and other synergistic pieces that voltron decks are for players who want the game to revolve around one protected attacker and the timing of each combat step.\nWhat are the best colors for Voltron?\nBoros turns red-white aggression into equipment cost reducers, double strike, and extra combats. However, Azorius pairs white auras with blue evasion and counterspells to keep one attacker connecting.\nWhat are the weaknesses of Voltron decks?\nVoltron puts most resources into one creature, so repeated removal and commander tax can exhaust its mana. Sacrifice edicts and mass bounce can answer the threat without targeting through hexproof or protection. The deck often removes players one at a time, which gives the rest of the table time to coordinate. Aura-heavy builds can lose several cards when the creature dies, making rebuilds harder than equipment builds.\nEDHMatchThe data platform for Commander. Find commanders that fit how you actually play.\nExplore\nHome\nSearch\nQuiz\nWhat Should I Build Next?\nBlog\nDiscover\nCollections\nSets\nBudget\nStrategy Guides\nAll Themes\nTokens\nSacrifice\nLifegain\nCompany\nNewsletter\nAbout\nContact\nLegal\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms & Conditions\nCookie Settings\nMagic: The Gathering and its logos are trademarks of Wizards of the Coast LLC. This site is not affiliated with Wizards of the Coast LLC.© 2026 EDHMATCH"
  sw,_index,page_text,39834,"EDH Archetypes Guide: Every Commander Playstyle Explained | Spellweave\nGuidesPricingAboutEN\nLog InSign Up\nEN\nSign Up\nHome\nGuides\nEDH Archetypes Guide: Every Commander Playstyle Explained\nEDH Archetypes Guide: Every Commander Playstyle Explained\nStop reading decklists that feel wrong. Compare the archetypes you'll actually face at the table, find which one matches how you want to win, and build it in one click.\nFor new and returning Commander players choosing a deck identity.\nBased on 4,231 decks built on Spellweave in the last 90 days.\nFind my playstyleCompare archetypesBrowse all\nBuilt and updated by the Spellweave deck-engine team.\nHow decks win\nCombat\nValue engines\nGraveyard\nSpell chains\nResource denial\nPolitics\nThe fast answer\nAn archetype is your deck's main game plan. It defines how you generate value, defend yourself, and ultimately win. The most common Commander archetypes include tokens, aristocrats, voltron, spellslinger, reanimator, blink, lands matter, tribal, control, big mana, artifacts, combo, and stax. Most decks blend two engines, with one as the primary plan.\nBeginner-friendly: Tokens, Tribal, Voltron.  ·  Higher complexity / variance: Reanimator, Spellslinger, Combo, Stax.\nFind your playstyle\nThree questions. Three archetype matches. Twenty seconds.\nI want to win by\nCombatValue enginesGraveyardSpell chainsResource denialPoliticsCombo\nMy pace\nFastMidrangeGrindy\nHeat I can take\nLow (fly under the radar)MediumHigh (be the threat)\nPopular Right Now\nTop archetypes built on Spellweave this period. (90 day window)\nSpellslinger13.2%\nStompy10.0%\nAristocrats7.9%\n+1/+1 Counters6.1%\nTokens5.6%\nLifegain4.7%\nCompare the most-played archetypes\nFive fields, ten archetypes, one scan. Tap any row for a deeper read.\nArchetypeCore EngineBest ColorsSkillBest if you enjoy\nAristocratsSacrifice creatures for value.Mardu, Abzan, EsperIntermediateValue engines, Graveyard\nTokensCreate large numbers of creature tokens and overwhelm opponents with sheer board presence.Naya, Selesnya, BantBeginnerCombat, Value engines\nTribalBuild around a creature type.Any (color-locked to tribe)BeginnerCombat, Value engines\nVoltronSuit up one creature (usually your commander) and win through commander damage.Bant, Naya, EsperBeginnerCombat\nReanimatorPut powerful creatures into the graveyard and bring them back cheaply.Sultai, Esper, JunkIntermediateGraveyard, Value engines\nSpellslingerCast lots of instants and sorceries to generate value.Izzet, Jeskai, GrixisIntermediateSpell chains, Value engines\nComboAssemble specific card combinations that win the game on the spot or create an insurmountable advantage.Sultai, Temur, EsperAdvancedCombo\n+1/+1 CountersSynergize with placing and manipulating +1/+1 counters on your creatures.Simic, Bant, NayaBeginnerCombat, Value engines\nLifegainGain life and convert it into other resources.Orzhov, Abzan, BantBeginnerValue engines, Combat\nBlinkExile your own creatures and return them to the battlefield to reuse enter-the-battlefield (ETB) effects.Bant, Esper, JeskaiIntermediateValue engines\nLands MatterSynergize with playing lands.Gruul, Sultai, NayaIntermediateValue engines, Combat\nLandfallTrigger payoffs whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control.AnyBeginnerCombat, Value engines\nStaxSlow opponents asymmetrically with restrictive permanents that hurt you less than everyone else.Esper, Bant, mono-WAdvancedResource denial\nAristocrats\nIntermediate\nCore EngineSacrifice creatures for value.\nBest ColorsMardu, Abzan, Esper\nBest if you enjoyValue engines, Graveyard\nRead\nTokens\nBeginner\nCore EngineCreate large numbers of creature tokens and overwhelm opponents with sheer board presence.\nBest ColorsNaya, Selesnya, Bant\nBest if you enjoyCombat, Value engines\nRead\nTribal\nBeginner\nCore EngineBuild around a creature type.\nBest ColorsAny (color-locked to tribe)\nBest if you enjoyCombat, Value engines\nRead\nVoltron\nBeginner\nCore EngineSuit up one creature (usually your commander) and win through commander damage.\nBest ColorsBant, Naya, Esper\nBest if you enjoyCombat\nRead\nReanimator\nIntermediate\nCore EnginePut powerful creatures into the graveyard and bring them back cheaply.\nBest ColorsSultai, Esper, Junk\nBest if you enjoyGraveyard, Value engines\nRead\nSpellslinger\nIntermediate\nCore EngineCast lots of instants and sorceries to generate value.\nBest ColorsIzzet, Jeskai, Grixis\nBest if you enjoySpell chains, Value engines\nRead\nCombo\nAdvanced\nCore EngineAssemble specific card combinations that win the game on the spot or create an insurmountable advantage.\nBest ColorsSultai, Temur, Esper\nBest if you enjoyCombo\nRead\n+1/+1 Counters\nBeginner\nCore EngineSynergize with placing and manipulating +1/+1 counters on your creatures.\nBest ColorsSimic, Bant, Naya\nBest if you enjoyCombat, Value engines\nRead\nLifegain\nBeginner\nCore EngineGain life and convert it into other resources.\nBest ColorsOrzhov, Abzan, Bant\nBest if you enjoyValue engines, Combat\nRead\nBlink\nIntermediate\nCore EngineExile your own creatures and return them to the battlefield to reuse enter-the-battlefield (ETB) effects.\nBest ColorsBant, Esper, Jeskai\nBest if you enjoyValue engines\nRead\nLands Matter\nIntermediate\nCore EngineSynergize with playing lands.\nBest ColorsGruul, Sultai, Naya\nBest if you enjoyValue engines, Combat\nRead\nLandfall\nBeginner\nCore EngineTrigger payoffs whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control.\nBest ColorsAny\nBest if you enjoyCombat, Value engines\nRead\nStax\nAdvanced\nCore EngineSlow opponents asymmetrically with restrictive permanents that hurt you less than everyone else.\nBest ColorsEsper, Bant, mono-W\nBest if you enjoyResource denial\nRead\nThe deep dives\nFeatured archetypes\nEach archetype below has a full breakdown. These are what you'll see most at any Commander table.\nAristocrats\nIntermediateBracket 2 to 4\nSacrifice creatures for value. The key mechanic is death triggers. You win by draining opponents through effects that deal damage or drain life whenever a creature dies, turning every creature death into incremental advantage.\nBest for\nPlayers who like value engines, sacrifice loops, and grindy snowball games.\nAvoid if\nYou want fast combat kills or a low-complexity board.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Aristocrats\nHow it wins\nStack two or three drain triggers (Blood Artist, Zulaport Cutthroat, Bastion of Remembrance) with a free sacrifice outlet, then turn every creature death into damage. Loops with Reassembling Skeleton or Gravecrawler can drain three opponents in a single turn cycle without ever attacking. Edict pieces like Grave Pact and Dictate of Erebos punish opponents who try to stabilize.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Sacrifice outlets: Viscera Seer, Ashnod's Altar\n•Death payoffs: Blood Artist, Zulaport Cutthroat, Grave Pact\n•Recursive creatures: Reassembling Skeleton, Bloodghast\nCommanders\nSephiroth, Fabled SOLDIER // Sephiroth, One-Winged Angel43.5% (30)\nDina, Essence Brewer23.2% (16)\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth17.4% (12)\nTeysa Karlov15.9% (11)\nHow to beat it\nExile-based graveyard hate (Rest in Peace, Bojuka Bog, Leyline of the Void) shuts off recursion. Static hate pieces neuter the drain payoffs. Hushbringer is the cleanest single answer because it stops both ETB and dies triggers, and Yasharn, Implacable Earth stops nontoken sacrifice. Removing the sacrifice outlet at instant speed can break many loops, especially when the outlet is the only free sacrifice engine on the battlefield.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Aristocrats\nTokens\nBeginnerBracket 2 to 4\nCreate large numbers of creature tokens and overwhelm opponents with sheer board presence. Token strategies excel at going wide, making it difficult for opponents to block profitably or remove all your threats at once.\nBest for\nBeginners and visual-feedback players. You see your board grow every turn.\nAvoid if\nYou want surgical removal and tight resource hands.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Tokens\nHow it wins\nBuild a wide board across two or three turns, then close with a mass pump (Craterhoof Behemoth, Triumph of the Hordes) or a token doubler hitting multiple generators in the same turn. Many token commanders generate tokens repeatedly or convert routine game actions into tokens, helping the engine refill after each board wipe.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Token generators: Avenger of Zendikar, Secure the Wastes, Raise the Alarm\n•Token doublers: Doubling Season, Anointed Procession, Parallel Lives\n•Mass pump: Craterhoof Behemoth, Triumph of the Hordes\nCommanders\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One26.8% (11)\nZurgo Stormrender26.8% (11)\nChatterfang, Squirrel General24.4% (10)\nMr. House, President and CEO22.0% (9)\nHow to beat it\nCheap creature sweepers (Toxic Deluge, Damnation, Farewell, Wrath of God) reset the plan. Anti-go-wide tech specifically punishes tokens: Massacre Wurm and Meathook Massacre drain on wipe, Echoing Truth clears an entire swarm of same-named tokens for two mana (the bounced tokens cease to exist), Ratchet Bomb and Pernicious Deed scale into a wide answer, and Illness in the Ranks plus Curse of Death's Hold neutralize the engine permanently.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Tokens\nTribal\nBeginnerBracket 2 to 4\nBuild around a creature type. Most of your creatures share a type so lords, tribal payoffs, and type-locked anthems stack into a coherent engine. Tribal can run as aggro (Goblins, Elves), combo (Slivers, Wizards), or value (Merfolk, Faeries) depending on the tribe and commander.\nBest for\nFlavor-first players who love a chosen creature type and want a clean linear plan.\nAvoid if\nYou like building tight engines without a creature-type theme guiding card choices.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Tribal\nHow it wins\nDensity carries the strategy: every creature pulls double duty as a body and a payoff. Lords (Goblin King, Lord of Atlantis, Elvish Archdruid, Goblin Chieftain, Merrow Reejerey) stack anthems and turn small creatures into real threats. Tribal payoffs (Kindred Discovery, Herald's Horn, Urza's Incubator, Coat of Arms, Roaming Throne) draw cards, reduce costs, or compound triggers. Tribal commanders like The Ur-Dragon and Edgar Markov double as in-color payoff engines. Many modern tribal builds skew combo (Slivers, Wizards) or value-engine (Faeries, Merfolk) rather than pure aggro.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Lords: Creatures that give +1/+1 to all others of the same type\n•Tribal payoffs: Kindred Discovery, Herald's Horn, Vanquisher's Banner\n•Tribal-specific cards: Cards designed for your chosen creature type\nCommanders\nEdgar Markov, Krenko, Mob Boss, Lathril, Blade of the Elves\nHow to beat it\nRepeated board wipes are the cleanest answer because tribal density means every wipe two-for-ones at minimum. Type-specific hate like Engineered Plague and Extinction punishes the whole creature base, and targeted removal on the key lord or payoff engine slows the snowball. Removing the commander often cuts off the tribe's best card-advantage source. Mana-tax pieces like Damping Sphere matter against specific tribes (Elves, Wizards) but are not universal answers.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Tribal\nVoltron\nBeginnerBracket 2 to 3\nSuit up one creature (usually your commander) and win through commander damage. 21 combat damage from a single commander is lethal. The strategy focuses on making one creature as large and evasive as possible, then connecting for lethal swings.\nBest for\nNew players who want a single clear gameplan: suit up the commander, swing.\nAvoid if\nYou want resilience to spot removal or a backup plan if your commander gets stranded.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Voltron\nHow it wins\nStack equipment (Sword of the Animist, Commander's Plate, Blackblade Reforged), auras (Shielded by Faith, Battle Mastery, All That Glitters), and +1/+1 counters onto an evasive commander. Two clean attacks usually closes the first opponent (10-11 + 10-11 = 21). Double strike shortens the clock; infect, exalted, or trample plus a big buff can enable one-shot kills.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Equipment: Sword of Feast and Famine, Lightning Greaves\n•Auras: Ethereal Armor, All That Glitters\n•Protection: Swiftfoot Boots, Heroic Intervention\nCommanders\nCloud, Planet's Champion34.8% (8)\nCloud, Ex-SOLDIER30.4% (7)\nAurelia, the Warleader17.4% (4)\nCaptain America, First Avenger17.4% (4)\nHow to beat it\nEdict effects (Diabolic Edict, Fleshbag Marauder, Liliana's Triumph) bypass shroud and hexproof. Exile and bounce remove the commander even with indestructible. Maze of Ith, fog effects, and propaganda taxes stall the clock until removal lands.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Voltron\nReanimator\nIntermediateBracket 2 to 4\nPut powerful creatures into the graveyard and bring them back cheaply. Instead of paying full mana costs for expensive threats, you cheat them into play from the graveyard for a fraction of the cost.\nBest for\nSpike-leaning players who want haymakers in play 5 turns early.\nAvoid if\nYour pod runs heavy graveyard hate or you dislike the swing of binary lines.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Reanimator\nHow it wins\nPut a 7+ mana creature into the graveyard on turn 2-3 (Entomb, Buried Alive, discard outlet) and reanimate it for 2-3 mana. The flagship targets take over by themselves: Atraxa, Grand Unifier; Archon of Cruelty; Razaketh, the Foulblooded; Vilis, Broker of Blood; Jin-Gitaxias, Core Augur; Sheoldred, Whispering One. At higher power, reanimator often skips the haymaker step entirely and loops a Razaketh chain or assembles a compact combo.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Enablers: Entomb, Buried Alive, Faithless Looting\n•Reanimation spells: Reanimate, Animate Dead, Living Death\n•Big targets: Jin-Gitaxias, Core Augur, Razaketh, the Foulblooded\nCommanders\nTerra, Herald of Hope42.1% (16)\nTergrid, God of Fright // Tergrid's Lantern23.7% (9)\nKenrith, the Returned King18.4% (7)\nMirko, Obsessive Theorist15.8% (6)\nHow to beat it\nInstant-speed exile-style graveyard hate (Bojuka Bog, Tormod's Crypt, Soul-Guide Lantern) is often backbreaking if timed in response to the reanimation spell. Counterspells on the reanimation are equally clean. Forcing the player to cast their fatties from hand removes the cost-advantage entirely.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Reanimator\nSpellslinger\nIntermediateBracket 2 to 4\nCast lots of instants and sorceries to generate value. Every spell you cast triggers additional effects, creating a chain of advantage. Mana can be tight since you often need to hold up instant-speed interaction while also advancing your own game plan.\nBest for\nPlayers who like reactive lines, chaining cantrips, and copying spells.\nAvoid if\nYou want a creature-heavy board or to avoid mana-intensive turns with held-up instants.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Spellslinger\nHow it wins\nModern spellslinger is rarely a slow cantrip deck. Rituals (Jeska's Will, Mana Geyser, Birgi, God of Storytelling) and cost reducers (Goblin Electromancer, Baral, Chief of Compliance) fuel explosive chain turns. Payoffs (Storm-Kiln Artist, Archmage Emeritus, Young Pyromancer, Talrand, Sky Summoner) snowball value, copy effects (Galvanic Iteration, Twincast) double the output, and the kill comes from Aetherflux Reservoir, an Underworld Breach loop, or a wide token swing.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Spell payoffs: Guttersnipe, Talrand, Sky Summoner\n•Cost reducers: Goblin Electromancer, Baral, Chief of Compliance\n•Copy effects: Fork, Twincast\nCommanders\nY'shtola, Night's Blessed45.9% (68)\nFire Lord Azula22.3% (33)\nVivi Ornitier19.6% (29)\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun12.2% (18)\nHow to beat it\nRemoving the payoff creature stalls the snowball, but tax effects (Eidolon of Rhetoric, Vryn Wingmare, Damping Sphere) and Rule of Law style lockouts hit spellslinger harder than counterspell trades, since one-for-one counter trades against cheap chain spells usually favor the spellslinger player. Graveyard hate also disrupts flashback, escape, and Underworld Breach lines.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Spellslinger\nCombo\nAdvancedBracket 3 to 4\nAssemble specific card combinations that win the game on the spot or create an insurmountable advantage. Combo decks need tutors to find their pieces and protection to resolve them. Power level varies widely depending on how many cards the combo requires.\nBest for\nSpike-aligned players who enjoy tutoring, sequencing, and clean wins.\nAvoid if\nYou play with casual pods that dislike abrupt 'I win on my turn' resolutions.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Combo\nHow it wins\nTutor for the missing piece, protect the assembly with counterspells or hand disruption, and win in a single turn. At casual power, combos are typically 3-4 pieces (Heliod + Walking Ballista with a tutor, Kiki-Jiki + Zealous Conscripts as the kill). At cEDH, two-card combos dominate: Thassa's Oracle + Demonic Consultation or Tainted Pact is the canonical 'I win' button, and Underworld Breach + Brain Freeze is the current premier engine.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Thassa's Oracle + Demonic Consultation: Exile your library, then win with Oracle's enter-the-battlefield trigger\n•Kiki-Jiki + Zealous Conscripts: Infinite hasty attackers by copying Conscripts to untap Kiki-Jiki\n•Isochron Scepter + Dramatic Reversal: Infinite mana with mana rocks that produce at least three total mana\nCommanders\nKinnan, Bonder Prodigy, Yawgmoth, Thran Physician, Tasigur, the Golden Fang\nHow to beat it\nCounterspells on either piece break the assembly. Tutor punishment (Opposition Agent, Aven Mindcensor, Archivist of Oghma) flips the search step into a liability. Targeted hand attack (Thoughtseize, Praetor's Grasp) preempts the assembly. Stax pieces that disrupt mana production (Null Rod, Collector Ouphe, Cursed Totem) shut off many of the most efficient lines. At lower power, racing the combo player before piece-3 lands is also viable.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Combo\n+1/+1 Counters\nBeginnerBracket 2 to 4\nSynergize with placing and manipulating +1/+1 counters on your creatures. Counter strategies scale quickly, turning small creatures into massive threats while triggering powerful payoff effects along the way.\nBest for\nPlayers who love incremental scaling and exponential snowballs.\nAvoid if\nYour pod packs heavy creature wipes you can't recover from.\nOpen the Deck Builder for +1/+1 Counters\nHow it wins\nStack a doubler (Hardened Scales, Branching Evolution, Doubling Season) with a counter distributor and watch every trigger pay double. Mid-game your weakest creature is a 6/6 and your commander closes games on its own. Many builds layer proliferate (Inexorable Tide, Evolution Sage, Karn's Bastion) for compounding scaling.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Counter doublers: Doubling Season, Hardened Scales, Branching Evolution\n•Counter distributors: Rishkar, Peema Renegade, Mikaeus, the Lunarch\n•Counter payoffs: Fathom Mage, Champion of Lambholt, Simic Ascendancy\nCommanders\nTidus, Yuna's Guardian52.6% (40)\nLeonardo, the Balance22.4% (17)\nMs. Bumbleflower13.2% (10)\nBruce Banner // The Incredible Hulk11.8% (9)\nHow to beat it\nSolemnity is the hardest single lockout. Combined with the right commander it shuts off the entire engine. Exile-based wipes (Farewell) reset the board cleanly, and Toxic Deluge gets around indestructible by reducing toughness. Counter-removal effects (Vampire Hexmage and Hex Parasite to nuke a key creature, Black Sun's Zenith as a -X sweeper) directly strip counter value. Removing the doubler enchantment before counters snowball matters most.\nOpen the Deck Builder for +1/+1 Counters\nLifegain\nBeginnerBracket 1 to 3\nGain life and convert it into other resources. Lifegain on its own does not win games, but payoff cards turn each point of life gained into card draw, creature tokens, +1/+1 counters, or direct damage to opponents.\nBest for\nDefensive players who want a long-game cushion that converts to closing power.\nAvoid if\nYou want fast pressure or hate one-card-combo finishes (Sanguine + Exquisite).\nOpen the Deck Builder for Lifegain\nHow it wins\nRun small repeated triggers (Soul Warden, Essence Warden, Suture Priest) and a counter-creating payoff (Heliod, Sun-Crowned; Cleric Class; Trudge Garden). Sanguine Bond + Exquisite Blood is the classic two-card combo finish. Aetherflux Reservoir converts life gain directly into damage, and Well of Lost Dreams turns it into card draw. Modern lifegain often hybridizes with aristocrats (Vito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose) or cleric tribal.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Lifegain payoffs: Ajani's Pridemate, Archangel of Thune, Heliod, Sun-Crowned\n•Life drain: Sanguine Bond, Exquisite Blood, Vito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose\n•Lifegain engines: Soul Warden, Essence Warden, Authority of the Consuls\nCommanders\nFrodo, Adventurous Hobbit34.1% (15)\nKwia Vigorbloom25.0% (11)\nBlech, Loafing Pest20.5% (9)\nTrostani, Selesnya's Voice20.5% (9)\nHow to beat it\nStatic life-gain hate is brutal: Tainted Remedy and Rain of Gore invert every gain trigger into damage; Roiling Vortex, Sulfuric Vortex, and Erebos, God of the Dead prevent or punish gain entirely. Targeted removal on the payoff creature stalls the snowball, and Skullcrack-style effects answer combo-line gain spikes.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Lifegain\nBlink\nIntermediateBracket 2 to 4\nExile your own creatures and return them to the battlefield to reuse enter-the-battlefield (ETB) effects. With the right setup, a single blink on a draw creature becomes a repeatable card advantage engine that compounds every turn.\nBest for\nPlayers who love stacked triggers, ETB chains, and value-engine sequencing.\nAvoid if\nYour pod runs Torpor Orb or you dislike longer per-turn play sequences.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Blink\nHow it wins\nStack ETB value creatures (Mulldrifter, Ravenous Chupacabra, Reflector Mage, Eternal Witness, Sun Titan, Agent of Treachery) with a blink engine that triggers every turn (Soulherder, Conjurer's Closet, Ephemerate, Teleportation Circle). Panharmonicon doubles every trigger. The deck typically out-resources the table and closes via accumulated value, with finishers like Deadeye Navigator pairing with a payoff (Peregrine Drake, Palinchron) or an extra-turns chain off Time Stretch / Temporal Manipulation.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Blink engines: Ephemerate, Conjurer's Closet, Soulherder, Teleportation Circle\n•ETB value: Mulldrifter, Thragtusk, Ravenous Chupacabra\n•ETB doubler: Panharmonicon\nCommanders\nBrago, King Eternal33.3% (3)\nDon & Leo, Problem Solvers22.2% (2)\nDelney, Streetwise Lookout22.2% (2)\nPhelia, Exuberant Shepherd22.2% (2)\nHow to beat it\nCounter or remove the blink engine itself. Without it, the deck slows to a fair midrange ETB pile rather than a snowballing value engine. Static effects that disable ETB triggers (Torpor Orb, Hushwing Gryff, Tocatli Honor Guard) are devastating against the archetype.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Blink\nLands Matter\nIntermediateBracket 2 to 4\nSynergize with playing lands. Landfall triggers, land recursion, and land-based value engines turn the most basic game action into a powerful advantage. Every land drop becomes a spell.\nBest for\nMid-to-late-game players who like grinding inevitability through land drops.\nAvoid if\nYour pod packs Wasteland/Strip Mine effects or you hate slow openings.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Lands Matter\nHow it wins\nExtra-land-drop enablers (Exploration, Oracle of Mul Daya, Azusa Lost but Seeking, Dryad of the Ilysian Grove) layer with fetchland recursion (Crucible of Worlds, Ramunap Excavator, Life from the Loam) so each turn fires 3+ landfall triggers. Payoffs scale fast: Avenger of Zendikar, Scute Swarm, Omnath Locus of Creation, Field of the Dead, Splendid Reclamation. Decks built around The Gitrog Monster or Lord Windgrace turn fetch loops into draw, ramp, and combo lines.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Landfall payoffs: Avenger of Zendikar, Scute Swarm, Omnath, Locus of Creation\n•Extra land drops: Exploration, Oracle of Mul Daya, Azusa, Lost but Seeking\n•Land recursion: Crucible of Worlds, Ramunap Excavator\nCommanders\nToph, the First Metalbender42.4% (14)\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait21.2% (7)\nHearthhull, the Worldseed18.2% (6)\nAvatar Aang // Aang, Master of Elements18.2% (6)\nHow to beat it\nTargeted land hate (Strip Mine, Wasteland, Ruination, Blood Moon, Back to Basics) hits this archetype harder than any other. Aven Mindcensor and Opposition Agent both have flash, so they can be deployed to punish fetchlands and tutors at the worst possible moment. Graveyard hate disrupts Crucible / Ramunap Excavator loops. Fast pressure before the ramp threshold also works.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Lands Matter\nLandfall\nBeginnerBracket 1 to 4\nTrigger payoffs whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control. Landfall decks use extra land drops from Azusa, Lost but Seeking and Exploration, land ramp and search effects like Cultivate and Three Visits, and land recursion like Crucible of Worlds and Ramunap Excavator to chain triggers across turns and end games with a wave of damage or tokens.\nBest for\nPlayers who enjoy mid-to-late-game ramp-driven inevitability and explosive turns built around chained land drops.\nAvoid if\nYour pod runs Wasteland or Strip Mine effects, Blood Moon, or you dislike slow openings before the engine comes online.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Landfall\nHow it wins\nStack extra-land-drop enablers (Exploration, Oracle of Mul Daya, Azusa Lost but Seeking, Dryad of the Ilysian Grove) with fetchland recursion (Crucible of Worlds, Ramunap Excavator, Life from the Loam) so each turn fires 3+ landfall triggers. Payoffs scale fast: Avenger of Zendikar, Scute Swarm, Omnath Locus of Creation, Field of the Dead, Splendid Reclamation. Decks built around The Gitrog Monster or Lord Windgrace turn fetch loops into draw, ramp, and combo lines.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Landfall payoffs: Avenger of Zendikar, Scute Swarm, Lotus Cobra, Felidar Retreat\n•Extra land drops and fetchlands: Azusa, Lost but Seeking, Exploration, Oracle of Mul Daya\n•Land recursion: Crucible of Worlds, Ramunap Excavator, The Mending of Dominaria\nCommanders\nTifa Lockhart35.0% (7)\nTatyova, Benthic Druid25.0% (5)\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait20.0% (4)\nOmnath, Locus of Creation20.0% (4)\nHow to beat it\nTargeted land hate (Strip Mine, Wasteland, Ruination, Blood Moon, Back to Basics) hits this archetype harder than any other. Aven Mindcensor and Opposition Agent both have flash, so they can be deployed to punish fetchlands and tutors at the worst possible moment. Graveyard hate disrupts Crucible / Ramunap Excavator loops. Fast pressure before the ramp threshold also works.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Landfall\nStax\nAdvancedBracket 3 to 4\nSlow opponents asymmetrically with restrictive permanents that hurt you less than everyone else. Stax can grind the game out with a value finisher or use the lock to force through a compact combo opponents can't interact with. Either way, the play pattern is 'they can't, you can.'\nBest for\nAdvanced players comfortable with proactive resource denial and table heat.\nAvoid if\nYou want a positive-feeling table or play casual unrated pods (lock pieces frustrate quickly).\nOpen the Deck Builder for Stax\nHow it wins\nResolve asymmetric lock pieces (Drannith Magistrate, Collector Ouphe, Archon of Emeria, Sphere of Resistance, Rule of Law) so opponents can't execute their plans while you develop under your own locks. Rhystic Study and Smothering Tithe (treasures, not draw) turn opponents' constrained turns into your resources. Many modern stax lists transition into a protected combo (Thassa's Oracle, Breach lines) once the lock is stable rather than grinding to a value finish.\nCore synergy pieces\n•Tax and lock pieces: Thalia, Guardian of Thraben, Sphere of Resistance, Rule of Law\n•Resource denial: Winter Orb, Static Orb, Stasis\n•Value under locks: Smothering Tithe, Rhystic Study, Weathered Wayfarer\nCommanders\nGrand Arbiter Augustin IV45.5% (5)\nThalia and The Gitrog Monster18.2% (2)\nKetramose, the New Dawn18.2% (2)\nWinota, Joiner of Forces18.2% (2)\nHow to beat it\nFlexible permanent removal is the most direct answer because modern stax pieces are spread across creatures (Drannith Magistrate, Collector Ouphe, Archon of Emeria), artifacts (Sphere of Resistance, Winter Orb), and enchantments (Rule of Law, Deafening Silence). The deck is fragile when locks are stripped faster than they can be redeployed. At lower power, stax often falls apart because the rest of the table can race or out-resource it before the lockout completes.\nOpen the Deck Builder for Stax\nAlso worth knowing\nMore archetypes you'll meet\nThese show up regularly. Lighter writeups, same useful detail.\nGraveyard\nUse the graveyard as a resource. Graveyard strategies treat the graveyard as a second hand, fueling mechanics like dredge, flashback, escape, and recursion. The more cards in your graveyard, the more options you have.\nTop commanders: Sidisi, Brood Tyrant, Karador, Ghost Chieftain, The Gitrog Monster\nMill\nWin by putting all of an opponent's library into their graveyard. When a player must draw from an empty library, they lose. Mill strategies attack a resource most decks cannot protect, targeting the library directly rather than life totals.\nTop commanders: Phenax, God of Deception, Bruvac the Grandiloquent, Mirko Vosk, Mind Drinker\nStorm\nCast many cheap spells in a single turn, then finish with a spell that has the storm keyword. A spell with storm creates a copy of itself for each spell cast before it that turn. Cast 10 spells before your storm finisher and it produces 10 copies.\nTop commanders: Mizzix of the Izmagnus, Kess, Dissident Mage, Krark, the Thumbless\nBurn\nDeal direct damage to opponents and their creatures. Burn strategies accumulate incremental damage through damage doublers and triggered abilities. In Commander, burn needs to scale to deal with 120 total life points across three opponents.\nTop commanders: Torbran, Thane of Red Fell, Purphoros, God of the Forge, Neheb, the Eternal\nWheels\nForce all players to discard their hands and draw new cards. Wheels disrupt opponents by removing the answers and combos they were holding, while you benefit from symmetry-breaking effects that punish drawing or reward discarding.\nTop commanders: Nekusar, the Mindrazer, Xyris, the Writhing Storm, The Locust God\nImpulse Draw\nExile cards from the top of your library and play or cast them during a limited window, often this turn or until your next turn. Impulse draw bypasses effects that restrict or replace card draws, such as Narset, Parter of Veils and Notion Thief, works around hand-size restrictions, and turns the top of your library into a temporary resource. Most impulse commanders are red or have access to red, where the mechanic is most concentrated.\nTop commanders: Prosper, Tome-Bound, Birgi, God of Storytelling, Etali, Primal Storm\nEnchantress\nCast enchantments and draw cards from them. This creates a self-sustaining engine: play an enchantment, draw a card, play more enchantments. The strategy snowballs as each enchantress effect multiplies your card draw.\nTop commanders: Sythis, Harvest's Hand, Tuvasa the Sunlit, Go-Shintai of Life's Origin\nArtifacts\nBuild around artifact synergies. Generate value by casting, sacrificing, and recurring artifacts. Artifact strategies benefit from strong mana acceleration through mana rocks, and many powerful artifact payoffs are colorless, making this theme accessible to any color combination.\nTop commanders: Urza, Lord High Artificer, Breya, Etherium Shaper, Jhoira, Weatherlight Captain\nEquipment\nAttach equipment to creatures to enhance their combat abilities. Unlike auras, equipment stays on the battlefield when the equipped creature dies, giving the strategy resilience. Equipment decks combine cheap creatures with powerful equipment to create lethal threats.\nTop commanders: Sram, Senior Edificer, Wyleth, Soul of Steel, Akiri, Line-Slinger\nTreasure\nGenerate Treasure tokens and use them for mana advantage, artifact synergies, or sacrifice triggers. Treasure tokens are artifact tokens that can be sacrificed for one mana of any color, providing both ramp and fixing while triggering artifact-matters effects.\nTop commanders: Prosper, Tome-Bound, Korvold, Fae-Cursed King, Magda, Brazen Outlaw\nLegends Matter\nBuild a deck where most or all of your nonland cards are legendary, then use payoffs that scale with legendary density. Sisay, Weatherlight Captain tutors legendary permanents, Kethis, the Hidden Hand discounts legendary spells, Jodah, the Unifier chains legendary spells cast from your hand into free lower-mana-value legendary nonland spells from your library, and Dihada, Binder of Wills digs for legendary cards while making Treasures for revealed cards put into your graveyard. Many legendary-matters tools come from sets and releases such as Dominaria United and the Universes Beyond release The Lord of the Rings: Tales of Middle-earth.\nTop commanders: Sisay, Weatherlight Captain, Jodah, the Unifier, Kethis, the Hidden Hand\nGroup Hug\nAccelerate all players' resources with mass draw, extra lands, and other gifts. Group hug decks create political alliances by helping everyone, then win through hidden combo finishers or by directing opponents to fight each other.\nTop commanders: Kynaios and Tiro of Meletis, Phelddagrif, Kenrith, the Returned King\nNiche & emerging\nThe long tail\nLess common, sometimes powerful. Quick notes only.\nOutlaws\nBuild around outlaws.\nStompy\nPlay big creatures ahead of curve and attack.\nInfect\nWin by dealing 10 poison counters to an opponent.\nClones\nCopy your own or opponents' best creatures.\nExtra Combats\nTake additional combat phases to multiply your damage output.\nMutate\nCast mutate creatures for their mutate cost targeting a non-Human creature with the same owner as the spell, usually your non-Human commander or another non-Human creature you own, to build one merged permanent with a pile of abilities.\nPolymorph\nTurn cheap tokens into the biggest creatures in your library.\nSelf-Mill\nDeliberately fill your own graveyard to fuel delve, flashback, and recursion-based strategies.\nSuperfriends\nWin with planeswalkers.\nEnergy\nGenerate and spend energy counters for powerful activated abilities.\nVehicles\nTurn your artifacts into attackers by crewing Vehicles with your creatures.\nSagas\nBuild around Saga enchantments that trigger powerful chapter abilities over multiple turns.\nDiscard\nForce opponents to discard cards, stripping away their resources and options.\nGroup Slug\nDeal damage to all opponents simultaneously through permanents that punish common game actions.\nForced Combat\nForce opponents' creatures to attack each other using the goad mechanic.\nPillow Fort\nBuild up defensive enchantments and effects that discourage or prevent opponents from attacking you.\nUpkeep Triggers\nLeverage repeating abilities that trigger at the beginning of upkeep steps to generate value, force sacrifices, or deal damage as turns cycle.\nHybrids\nMost decks are hybrid archetypes\nLayering two engines is the norm in Commander. Here are the most common pairings on Spellweave.\nMost decks are not purely one archetype. An aristocrats deck might include a combo finish. A tribal deck might have reanimator elements to recur key creatures. A tokens strategy might blend with +1/+1 counters for explosive board growth.\nYour primary archetype determines your core card choices, and secondary themes fill supporting roles. When building, identify your main strategy first, then layer in complementary elements that strengthen your game plan without diluting it. Compare the most-played archetypes.\nFAQ\nFrequently asked questions\nWhat is an archetype in Commander?+An archetype is a deck's core game plan: how it generates value, defends itself, and ultimately wins. Most Commander decks anchor on one primary archetype and layer in elements from a second. Tokens, aristocrats, voltron, spellslinger, reanimator, and stax are all archetypes.\nWhat is the difference between an archetype and a theme?+Archetype is the engine: how the deck wins. Theme is the flavor: a tribe, a card type, or a mechanic. Slivers is a theme that lives inside the tribal archetype. Artifacts is often its own engine (artifact combo, treasure ramp, sacrifice, eggs, constructs) and only sometimes a theme inside another archetype like storm or aristocrats.\nWhich archetype is best for new Commander players?+Tokens and tribal are the gentlest entry points: clear plans, deep budget-friendly card pools, and easy mulligan decisions. Voltron has the simplest game plan (suit up, swing) but is strategically fragile because the whole deck depends on the commander surviving. That is fine for learning and less forgiving in actual play.\nCan a Commander deck be more than one archetype?+Yes, and most are. The Spellweave engine routinely detects a primary archetype with a secondary engine layered on top. Aristocrats plus tokens, lands matter plus big mana, and reanimator plus graveyard value are some of the most common hybrids.\nWhat is the difference between control and stax?+Control reacts to threats with answers like counterspells, removal, and board wipes. Stax denies opponents resources before threats land, using lock pieces, tax effects, and asymmetric prison cards. Stax is more proactive, control is more reactive, and many decks blend both.\nIs tribal an archetype?+Tribal is usually treated as both an archetype and a theme. The archetype framing is about the engine: lords, tribal payoffs, and creature density driving combat damage. The theme framing is about the creature type itself.\nWhat archetype wins fastest in casual Commander?+Voltron can eliminate one player early because commander damage scales linearly with equipment, but it often struggles to close out the full table. Combo and ramp-into-Craterhoof lines actually end multi-player games faster on average. Reanimator can also win on turn 4-5 with the right opener.\nWhere do these popularity numbers come from?+Live data from decks built on Spellweave. The engine classifies each deck's primary archetype based on its synergy profile, and we aggregate the result on a rolling 90-day window. Re-built decks count once each.\nBuild a deck around your archetype\nPick an archetype, choose a commander, and let the Spellweave engine assemble a synergy-driven shell you can tune card by card.\nOpen Deck BuilderBrowse Commanders\nRelated Guides\nChoosing a Commander\nHow to pick the right commander for your playstyle. Color identity, build-around vs value engines, and partner mechanics.\nCard Synergy Explained\nEnablers, payoffs, and engines. How to identify cards that work together and build a deck with a coherent game plan.\nEDH Deck Roles\nThe essential functional roles every Commander deck needs. Ramp, draw, removal, board wipes, counters, tutors, protection, recursion, and graveyard synergy.\nView all guides\nOn this page\nFeatured\nAristocrats\nTokens\nTribal\nVoltron\nReanimator\nSpellslinger\nCombo\n+1/+1 Counters\nLifegain\nBlink\nLands Matter\nLandfall\nStax\nAlso covered\nGraveyard\nMill\nStorm\nBurn\nWheels\nImpulse Draw\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nEquipment\nTreasure\nLegends Matter\nGroup Hug\nNiche & emerging\nOutlaws\nStompy\nInfect\nClones\nExtra Combats\nMutate\nPolymorph\nSelf-Mill\nSuperfriends\nEnergy\nVehicles\nSagas\nDiscard\nGroup Slug\nForced Combat\nPillow Fort\nUpkeep Triggers\nBack to topOn this page\nOn this page\nFeatured\nAristocrats\nTokens\nTribal\nVoltron\nReanimator\nSpellslinger\nCombo\n+1/+1 Counters\nLifegain\nBlink\nLands Matter\nLandfall\nStax\nAlso covered\nGraveyard\nMill\nStorm\nBurn\nWheels\nImpulse Draw\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nEquipment\nTreasure\nLegends Matter\nGroup Hug\nNiche & emerging\nOutlaws\nStompy\nInfect\nClones\nExtra Combats\nMutate\nPolymorph\nSelf-Mill\nSuperfriends\nEnergy\nVehicles\nSagas\nDiscard\nGroup Slug\nForced Combat\nPillow Fort\nUpkeep Triggers\nBack to top\n© 2026 Spellweave by 3Dec Studios · Unofficial Fan Content\nAboutPricingGuidesReleasesNewsletterSupportPrivacyTermsCookie settingsEnglishPortuguês (Brasil)Español日本語\nAboutPricingGuidesReleasesNewsletterSupportPrivacyTermsCookie settings\nEnglishPortuguês (Brasil)Español日本語\n© 2026 Spellweave by 3Dec Studios · Unofficial Fan Content"
  tz,_index,page_text,8222,"The 20 Commander Deck Archetypes, Explained | TurnZero HQ\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nAll 20 Archetypes\nDeck Archetypes\nThe 20 Commander Deck Archetypes\nEvery Commander deck is trying to do something. These are the twenty things: what each archetype does, which colors suit it, and the cards and commanders that define it.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nAn archetype is a deck's primary game plan: not what is in it, but what it is trying to do\non the turn it wins. Two decks can share sixty cards and be entirely different archetypes,\nand knowing which one you have built tells you what to cut, what to add, and which pods you\nare a good fit for.\nThese are the twenty archetypes TurnZero HQ classifies decks into. Most real decks are a\nblend of two or three: aristocrats decks go wide, lands decks ramp, and a combo lurks in\nthe back of plenty of otherwise fair decks. Pick the one that describes the win, and treat\nthe rest as support.\nAll 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger   Wins on a pile of instants and sorceries rather than a board of creatures.   Graveyard recursion   Cards come back from the graveyard, treating it as a second hand.   Mill   Empties libraries instead of attacking, winning when opponents cannot draw.   Voltron   One creature, usually the commander, carries the whole game plan.   Go wide   Floods the board with tokens and wins with numbers.   Aristocrats   Sacrifices its own creatures for value and drains the table.   Enchantress   Built around a critical mass of enchantments that draw cards as they resolve.   Artifacts   Built around a critical mass of artifacts and the payoffs that count them.   Tribal   Rewards playing many creatures that share a creature type.   Stax   Taxes or locks down what every player at the table is allowed to do.   Combo   Aims to assemble a specific card combination that ends the game.   Control   Answers everything and wins late, once the table has run out of threats.   Ramp   Accelerates into a mana advantage and casts bigger things sooner.   Aggro   Cheap, aggressive creatures apply pressure early and often.   Group hug   Gives the whole table resources, and wins from the goodwill it creates.   Superfriends   Built around a critical mass of planeswalkers.   Lands matter   Landfall and land-recursion turn the mana base itself into the engine.   Counters   Built around +1/+1 counters and the effects that multiply them.   Blink   Repeatedly exiles and returns its own creatures for their enter triggers.   Lifegain   Built around gaining life and the payoffs that turn it into a win condition.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat are the main Commander deck archetypes?\nThe twenty archetypes TurnZero HQ tracks are Spellslinger, Graveyard recursion, Mill, Voltron, Go wide, Aristocrats, Enchantress, Artifacts, Tribal, Stax, Combo, Control, Ramp, Aggro, Group hug, Superfriends, Lands matter, Counters, Blink, and Lifegain. Most real decks combine two or three of them.\nCan a Commander deck be more than one archetype?\nAlmost every deck is. Aristocrats decks are usually also go-wide, lands-matter decks are usually also ramp, and combo turns up as a backup plan inside decks built around something else entirely. The archetype is a description of the primary game plan, not an exclusive label.\nHow do I know which archetype my deck is?\nAsk what the deck is doing on the turn it wins. A deck that draws twenty cards and then attacks with one enormous creature is Voltron, not card draw. The archetype is whatever the rest of the deck is in service of.\nAdvertisement   Related Guides\nPlay Better Commander: In-Game Decision Frameworks →Build an Archetype in the Deck Builder →Commander Guides Hub →Best MTG Commander Decks & Precons →The Commander Deck Template →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →All 32 MTG Color Combinations and Their Names →What Is EDH Power Level? →\nSee which of your archetypes actually wins.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with its archetype, so your record breaks down by strategy, not just by commander. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,aggro,page_text,12257,"Aggro Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Combo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nAggro\nDeck Archetype\nAggro: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nAggro is the hardest archetype in Commander, because the format was designed to blunt it. One hundred and twenty life across three opponents is a lot of attacking.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nAggro: Cheap, aggressive creatures apply pressure early and often.\nWhat Aggro Decks Actually Do\nAn aggro deck plays cheap, efficient creatures and attacks early, aiming to end games before slower decks assemble their engines. It trades late-game power for tempo: a two-mana 3/2 is terrible on turn ten and excellent on turn two, and the deck is a bet that turn ten will not arrive.\nCommander pushes back on all of it. Forty life each and three opponents means one hundred and twenty total damage, everyone plays board wipes, and singleton means you cannot run four copies of your best two-drop. Aggro decks that work solve this in specific ways: commander damage, which needs only twenty-one; evasion and trample that make blockers irrelevant; extra combat steps; or effects that hit all opponents at once so every attack counts three times.\nThe Aggro engine\nThree steps that build to a finish. This one ends the game rather than coming back round.\n1 Cheap threats, early The curve tops out where other Commander decks start.\n2 Attack every turn Anger grants the whole team haste from the graveyard.\n3 Finish it Embercleave turns a board of small creatures into lethal damage.\nThe last step wins the game, so there is nothing to come back round for.\nWhich Colors Fit Aggro\nRed and white are the classic aggressive colors: cheap creatures, haste, and combat tricks. Green plays a different kind of aggro, trading speed for size and trample. Black attacks with efficient creatures backed by removal to clear blockers, and blue is largely absent, contributing evasion rather than pressure.\nRed Haste, cheap bodies, double strike, and extra combat steps. The color that most directly shortens the game.\nWhite Efficient small creatures and anthems, plus the protection that keeps an aggressive board alive through a wrath.\nGreen Size and trample. Green aggro attacks with creatures too big to block profitably rather than too many to block.\nBlack Efficient threats plus the removal to clear the way. Aggro with an answer for the one blocker that matters.\nBlue Barely present. Blue contributes evasion and the occasional tempo play, but it does not build pressure on its own.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Aggro Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding aggro for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nEmbercleave ↗\nCost reduction on a wide board, then double strike and trample at instant speed. Converts a stalled attack into lethal after blockers.\nIroas, God of Victory ↗\nYour attackers cannot be blocked by more than one creature and do not take damage from blockers. Attacking becomes nearly free.\nAnger ↗\nGrants haste to your whole team from the graveyard, permanently, as long as you control a Mountain.\nHammer of Purphoros ↗\nAn anthem-plus-haste enchantment that also makes blockers, so the deck keeps pressuring without overextending.\nAggravated Assault ↗\nRepeatable extra combat steps. With any mana-positive attack trigger this becomes an outright kill.\nBerserk ↗\nOne mana to double a creature's power and give it trample. The classic surprise kill from a board that looked survivable.\nReturn of the Wildspeaker ↗\nDraws cards equal to your biggest creature's power, or pumps the team. Solves aggro's card-advantage problem in green.\nElesh Norn, Grand Cenobite ↗\nPlus-two-plus-two for you and minus-two-minus-two for everyone else. Frequently a one-sided board wipe and a lethal attack in one card.\nDolmen Gate ↗\nYour attacking creatures take no combat damage, which lets a smaller board attack into a bigger one every turn.\nOhran Frostfang ↗\nDeathtouch and a card for every attacker. Makes blocking suicidal and refills your hand while you swing.\nTop 10 Aggro Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nIsshin, Two Heavens as One ↗\nDoubles every attack trigger in the deck. The most build-around aggressive commander of recent years.\nXenagos, God of Revels ↗\nHaste and doubled power to one creature each combat. Turns any large body into an immediate kill threat.\nNeheb, the Eternal ↗\nMana in your second main phase for damage dealt, so a big attack pays for the next threat.\nAurelia, Exemplar of Justice ↗\nMentor plus escalating buffs, and she pushes damage through with trample and first strike as the counters pile up.\nZurgo Helmsmasher ↗\nA 7/2 haste for five that cannot be destroyed on your turn. Aggro reduced to its bluntest form.\nGahiji, Honored One ↗\nPumps every attacker that is not attacking you, which turns the whole table into your offence.\nWulfgar of Icewind Dale ↗\nDoubles attack and haste triggers for your team, in a color pair full of them.\nIlharg, the Raze-Boar ↗\nPuts a creature from hand onto the battlefield attacking, then returns it. Cheats out giants every combat.\nRuric Thar, the Unbowed ↗\nA 6/6 vigilance that punishes noncreature spells, so the control decks pay for the turns they spend answering you.\nToski, Bearer of Secrets ↗\nIndestructible, must attack, and draws a card for every creature that connects. Aggro that never runs out of cards.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nThe politics are the hard part. Attacking early makes you the visible threat, and three opponents can agree to deal with you long before your deck is finished. Spread damage rather than focusing one player down unless you can finish them, and be realistic about the deck's clock. An aggro deck that has not won by turn eight is usually the worst deck at the table.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nIs aggro viable in Commander?\nYes, but it needs a specific build. Forty life across three opponents is one hundred and twenty damage, so successful aggro decks lean on commander damage, evasion and trample, extra combat steps, or effects that damage every opponent at once, rather than simply attacking with more creatures.\nWhat colors are best for aggro in Commander?\nRed and white for cheap creatures, haste, and anthems; green for size and trample. Black supports an aggressive plan with removal to clear blockers. Blue is the weakest aggro color, contributing evasion rather than pressure.\nHow fast should an aggro Commander deck win?\nAround turn seven to nine in a typical casual pod. Much later than that and slower decks have stabilised with board wipes and bigger creatures. If a deck regularly reaches turn ten without a clear path to victory, it usually needs more evasion or a way to end the game outside combat.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Aggro? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nVoltron →Go wide →How Combat Works →What Is Commander Damage? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Combo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,aristocrats,page_text,12299,"Aristocrats Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Mill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nAristocrats\nDeck Archetype\nAristocrats: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nAristocrats decks do not care whether their creatures live. Dying is the point: each death is a trigger, and enough triggers drain the table without ever attacking.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nAristocrats: Sacrifices its own creatures for value and drains the table.\nWhat Aristocrats Decks Actually Do\nAn aristocrats deck sacrifices its own creatures on purpose, converting each death into damage, drain, cards, or mana. The name comes from a run of Magic cards depicting Innistrad's vampire nobility profiting from other people's deaths, and it describes the play pattern exactly: you are not attacking, you are cashing creatures in.\nThe engine is three interlocking parts. A sacrifice outlet, ideally free and repeatable, so you can sacrifice at instant speed as many times as you like. A supply of bodies, usually tokens or recursive creatures. And a death payoff, most often a drain effect that hits all opponents at once, which is what makes the archetype so strong in a four-player game.\nThe Aristocrats engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Sacrifice a creature Viscera Seer and Ashnod's Altar do it for free, any number of times.\n2 The table loses life Zulaport Cutthroat drains every opponent, not just the one you were fighting.\n3 A token replaces it The body you spent comes back, so the outlet never runs dry.\n↻ Removal is no answer to a creature that wanted to die.\nWhich Colors Fit Aristocrats\nBlack is the core: it owns death triggers, drain effects, and most free sacrifice outlets. White contributes the best token generation and a few of the strongest death-trigger doublers. Red supplies free sacrifice outlets and damage-based payoffs, and green fills the board and recurs what dies. Blue is essentially absent.\nBlack Drain payoffs, death triggers, and the free sacrifice outlets the engine is built on. The one color the archetype cannot do without.\nWhite Token production to feed the outlets, plus effects that double death triggers outright.\nRed Free sacrifice outlets that also deal damage, so the outlet is itself a payoff.\nGreen Bodies and recursion: the fuel supply, returning the same creatures to be sacrificed again.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Aristocrats Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding aristocrats for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nBlood Artist ↗\nWhenever any creature dies, including theirs, the player you target loses 1 life and you gain 1. The defining card of the archetype.\nZulaport Cutthroat ↗\nCounts only your own creatures, but drains each opponent instead of one target. Redundancy is what turns a cute engine into a consistent kill.\nBastion of Remembrance ↗\nA third copy on an enchantment, which is much harder for most decks to remove than a 0/1 body.\nViscera Seer ↗\nA free sacrifice outlet on a one-drop, with scry attached. Being free and repeatable matters far more than the scry.\nAshnod's Altar ↗\nSacrifice for two colorless mana. The outlet that most combo lines in the archetype are built around.\nPhyrexian Altar ↗\nThe same at one mana of any color, which fixes as well as ramps and enables far more loops.\nGoblin Bombardment ↗\nA free outlet that deals a damage each time, so it is an outlet and a win condition in one two-mana enchantment.\nPitiless Plunderer ↗\nA Treasure for every creature that dies, which pays for the next sacrifice and turns many loops infinite.\nGrave Pact ↗\nEvery creature you lose costs each opponent one too. Turns your engine into a one-sided board wipe on repeat.\nDictate of Erebos ↗\nThe same effect with flash, so it lands after a wrath or in response to removal on your commander.\nTop 10 Aristocrats Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nTeysa Karlov ↗\nDoubles every death trigger and gives your tokens vigilance and lifelink. The most direct payoff commander in the archetype.\nKorvold, Fae-Cursed King ↗\nDraws and grows on every sacrifice, of any permanent type. The best card-advantage engine of any aristocrats commander.\nYawgmoth, Thran Physician ↗\nA free outlet, a draw engine, and repeatable removal on one four-drop. Rewrites what the deck is allowed to do.\nJudith, the Scourge Diva ↗\nAnthem plus a damage per nontoken death, aiming the engine at creatures as well as at players.\nAyara, First of Locthwain ↗\nDrains on every black creature entering and converts creatures into cards. Mono-black and relentlessly consistent.\nMarchesa, the Black Rose ↗\nReturns your creatures when they die with a counter on them, which makes sacrificing genuinely free.\nShirei, Shizo's Caretaker ↗\nReturns every one-power creature that died this turn, every turn. A recursion engine built for sacrifice loops.\nChatterfang, Squirrel General ↗\nAdds a Squirrel to every token you make and turns the board into removal. Fuel supply and outlet in one.\nJuri, Master of the Revue ↗\nGrows on every sacrifice and dumps all that damage at a player when he dies. A sacrifice deck with a built-in finisher.\nBraids, Arisen Nightmare ↗\nTurns your own sacrifices into cards or life loss and makes opponents choose which. Value even when the engine stalls.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nAristocrats decks can drain the table from a board that looks harmless, which is why experienced pods kill a Blood Artist on sight. Run enough redundancy that losing one payoff is not losing the game, and be honest at Rule 0. A deck that wins from an empty-looking board without attacking surprises people, and the surprise is the part that causes friction.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is an aristocrats deck in MTG?\nAn aristocrats deck sacrifices its own creatures for value, using a free sacrifice outlet, a steady supply of expendable bodies, and death-trigger payoffs like Blood Artist and Zulaport Cutthroat that drain life. It wins by accumulating those drain triggers rather than by attacking.\nWhat colors are aristocrats decks?\nBlack is essential, for the death triggers, drain effects, and free sacrifice outlets. White adds tokens and trigger doubling, red adds damage-dealing outlets, and green adds bodies and recursion. Orzhov, Rakdos, and Jund are the common pairings.\nWhat is the difference between aristocrats and go-wide?\nBoth make lots of small creatures, but go-wide attacks with them and aristocrats sacrifices them. The two overlap heavily (many token commanders support either plan) and plenty of decks run both, attacking when the board is favourable and draining when it is not.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Aristocrats? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Aristocrats Commanders →Common Aristocrats Deck Mistakes →Best Budget Aristocrats Upgrades →Go wide →Graveyard recursion →Lifegain →What Is Rule 0? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Mill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,artifacts,page_text,12734,"Artifact Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Go wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nArtifacts\nDeck Archetype\nArtifacts: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nArtifacts are the only card type that every color can play, which makes this the one archetype where the commander picks the colors rather than the strategy doing it.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nArtifacts: Built around a critical mass of artifacts and the payoffs that count them.\nWhat Artifacts Decks Actually Do\nArtifact decks assemble a critical mass of artifacts and run payoffs that count them, recur them, or turn them into something else. Because artifacts are colorless, the deck's actual engine (the mana rocks, the equipment, the cost reducers, the sacrifice loops) is available regardless of what is in the command zone.\nIn practice there are two lanes that share a lot of cards. Artifact aggro plays cheap artifacts and creatures that grow with the count, closing games quickly. Artifact value plays cost reducers and recursion, chaining artifacts into more artifacts until something in the pile is lethal. Both benefit from artifacts being the most re-buyable permanent type in the format: a graveyard full of them is a resource, not a loss.\nThe Artifacts engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Play cheap artifacts Most cost one or two mana, and several cost none at all.\n2 The next costs less Foundry Inspector takes a mana off everything that follows.\n3 The count pays out Cranial Plating and Steel Overseer scale with how many you have.\n↻ Each artifact makes the rest of the deck cheaper and bigger.\nWhich Colors Fit Artifacts\nThe honest answer is that the artifacts themselves are colorless and the colors are chosen for what they add on top. Blue is the strongest partner. It has the best artifact-specific card advantage and the cheating-into-play effects. Red brings recursion and treasure, white brings equipment and mass recursion, black brings tutors and sacrifice payoffs, and green brings ramp and, unusually, the format's best artifact removal. Colorless is a real option: several strong commanders let a deck run no colors at all.\nBlue The best artifact payoffs, cost reduction, and the effects that put artifacts onto the battlefield from anywhere.\nRed Recursion from the graveyard, Treasure generation, and the sacrifice-and-rebuy loops the archetype is built on.\nWhite Equipment support and mass artifact recursion. The color that turns an artifact board into an attacking one.\nBlack Tutors to find the missing piece, and payoffs that convert artifacts leaving the battlefield into drain.\nGreen Ramp to deploy faster and, worth remembering, the best artifact removal in the format, which cuts both ways.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Artifacts Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding artifacts for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nUrza's Saga ↗\nA land that makes a Construct scaling with your artifact count and then tutors a cheap artifact. Enormous value from a land slot.\nFoundry Inspector ↗\nArtifacts cost one less. Cost reduction is what turns a hand of artifacts into a single explosive turn.\nCranial Plating ↗\nPlus one power per artifact you control, with a black-mana equip at instant speed. The archetype's classic one-hit kill.\nSteel Overseer ↗\nA counter on every artifact creature each turn. Slow to start and unbeatable if ignored for three turns.\nGoblin Welder ↗\nSwaps an artifact on the battlefield for one in a graveyard, free, every turn. Recursion and disruption at once.\nKrark-Clan Ironworks ↗\nSacrifice any artifact for two mana. The engine behind most artifact combo loops in the format.\nMystic Forge ↗\nPlay artifacts off the top of your library. In a deck that is mostly artifacts, this is drawing several extra cards a turn.\nWurmcoil Engine ↗\nSix-mana lifelink and deathtouch that leaves two tokens behind. The fair, unglamorous card that stabilises boards.\nScrap Trawler ↗\nReturns a cheaper artifact whenever an artifact you control dies, chaining into itself across a sacrifice loop.\nUnwinding Clock ↗\nUntaps all your artifacts on every player's turn, turning tap abilities into four activations per round instead of one.\nTop 10 Artifacts Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nUrza, Lord High Artificer ↗\nTaps artifacts for mana, makes a Construct on entry, and digs into your library. Arguably the strongest artifact commander printed.\nBreya, Etherium Shaper ↗\nFour colors, a free Thopter pair, and three modal abilities. The most flexible artifact command zone in the format.\nOsgir, the Reconstructor ↗\nExiles artifacts from the graveyard to make two copies. Turns every dead artifact into two live ones.\nEmry, Lurker of the Loch ↗\nSelf-mills and casts artifacts out of the graveyard each turn, at a cost that drops as your artifact count rises.\nSharuum the Hegemon ↗\nReanimates an artifact on entry, which with a clone effect is one of the oldest combos in Commander.\nJhoira, Weatherlight Captain ↗\nDraws a card for every historic spell you cast. Card advantage that scales directly with artifact count.\nShorikai, Genesis Engine ↗\nA vehicle commander that makes a Pilot and loots every turn without ever attacking.\nDaretti, Scrap Savant ↗\nA planeswalker commander whose whole kit is discarding artifacts and returning them, with an emblem finisher.\nArcum Dagsson ↗\nTutors any artifact from the library each turn by sacrificing an artifact creature. Ruthlessly consistent, and famously divisive.\nKarn, Silver Golem ↗\nAnimates your noncreature artifacts, and being genuinely colorless lets the deck run every colorless land and artifact in the format.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nArtifact removal is the most commonly played removal in Commander after creature removal, and mass artifact destruction hits this deck harder than a board wipe hits most. Green and red both have one-sided sweepers for artifacts specifically. Spread your value across enough permanents that no single Vandalblast is a concession, and keep some recursion so the sweeper is a setback rather than an ending.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is an artifact deck in Commander?\nAn artifact deck runs a high concentration of artifacts and cards that reward that count: cost reducers, creatures that grow with the number of artifacts, and engines that sacrifice and return them. Because artifacts are colorless, the strategy can be built in any color combination.\nWhat is the best color for artifacts in MTG?\nBlue, for the strongest artifact-specific card advantage and cheat-into-play effects. Red is close behind for graveyard recursion and Treasure. That said, artifact decks exist in every color, and colorless commanders like Karn, Silver Golem support a deck with no colors at all.\nAre mana rocks part of the artifact archetype?\nThey overlap but are not the same thing. Almost every Commander deck plays mana rocks; an artifact deck plays them and also counts them, so a Sol Ring is both acceleration and a payoff trigger. A deck is only really the artifact archetype when cards care how many artifacts you control.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Artifacts? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Artifact Commanders →Combo →Voltron →Mana Rocks: The Complete Guide →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Go wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools 