  tz,blink,page_text,12147,"Blink Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Superfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nAristocrats Mistakes\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nBlink\nDeck Archetype\nBlink: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nA creature with a good enter trigger is a one-shot spell attached to a body. Blink decks make it a spell you get to cast every single turn.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nBlink: Repeatedly exiles and returns its own creatures for their enter triggers.\nWhat Blink Decks Actually Do\nBlink decks, also called flicker decks, exile their own creatures and immediately return them, re-triggering every enter-the-battlefield ability. A Solemn Simulacrum blinked four times has fetched four lands and drawn four cards from one card.\nThe archetype is built like an engine rather than a threat. Most of the deck is creatures whose value is entirely in their entry trigger, plus a suite of blink effects to reuse them. The by-products are what make it strong in practice: blinking is instant-speed removal protection, it resets combat damage and auras opponents put on your creatures, and effects that exile an opponent's creature permanently are just a blink effect pointed the wrong way.\nThe Blink engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 The creature enters Its enter trigger does the work: a card, a kill or a land.\n2 Exile it Ephemerate costs one mana, at instant speed, and can be bought back.\n3 It returns Same creature, same trigger, same turn.\n↻ One good enter trigger is the whole deck, used repeatedly.\nWhich Colors Fit Blink\nBlink is the most color-concentrated archetype after mill, and it is white-blue. White has the blink spells and most of the value creatures; blue has the flash, the card draw, and the untap effects. Green contributes a handful of excellent blink outlets and the best value creatures to blink, and black adds a death-and-return angle that overlaps with recursion. Red barely participates.\nWhite The deepest pool of blink spells and the value creatures worth blinking. The archetype's home color.\nBlue Flash, card draw, and the repeatable untap and blink outlets that make it an engine rather than a trick.\nGreen A few of the best repeatable blink outlets, plus the largest enter-trigger creatures in the format.\nBlack The death-and-return variant, where creatures come back from the graveyard rather than from exile.\nRed Almost absent. Red has sacrifice-and-return effects but little true blink.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Blink Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding blink for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nEphemerate ↗\nOne mana to blink a creature, with rebound so it does it again next turn. Two triggers and a removal dodge for a single card.\nEldrazi Displacer ↗\nA repeatable blink for three mana, usable at instant speed on any creature, including an opponent's, as removal.\nConjurer's Closet ↗\nBlinks a creature at the end of every one of your turns, for free, forever. The most reliable engine piece in the archetype.\nDeadeye Navigator ↗\nSoulbond gives unlimited blinks for two mana each. Powerful enough to be a combo piece with half the format.\nGhostly Flicker ↗\nBlinks two permanents including lands and artifacts, which is what turns blink into an infinite-mana engine.\nDisplacer Kitten ↗\nBlinks a nonland permanent whenever you cast a noncreature spell. Ties the archetype to spellslinger and to combo.\nRestoration Angel ↗\nA flash 3/4 flier that blinks a creature on entry. Value, a combat trick, and a removal dodge on one body.\nPanharmonicon ↗\nDoubles enter triggers for creatures and artifacts, so every blink pays twice for no extra mana.\nSolemn Simulacrum ↗\nFetches a land on entry and draws on death. The archetype's benchmark for what a blink target should do.\nCloudshift ↗\nThe one-mana baseline. Cheap, instant-speed, and often the difference between losing a creature and getting a free trigger.\nTop 10 Blink Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nBrago, King Eternal ↗\nBlinks all your nonland permanents on combat damage. The archetype's flagship and a notorious combo engine.\nRoon of the Hidden Realm ↗\nA repeatable blink from the command zone every turn, in the three colors with the best targets.\nYarok, the Desecrated ↗\nDoubles every enter trigger you control, which turns each blink into two payoffs.\nEmiel the Blessed ↗\nA three-mana repeatable blink outlet with no color requirements beyond green, and a combo engine besides.\nChulane, Teller of Tales ↗\nA card and a land on every creature cast, plus a bounce to reuse triggers. Value on every axis at once.\nAminatou, the Fateshifter ↗\nA planeswalker commander whose minus-one blinks a permanent every single turn, for free.\nRanar the Ever-Watchful ↗\nMakes a Spirit whenever you exile a card from hand or blink a permanent, so the engine builds a board.\nEphara, God of the Polis ↗\nDraws on every creature entering on any turn, which rewards flash and end-step blinking.\nYorion, Sky Nomad ↗\nBlinks any number of your permanents on entry, and can be recast repeatedly from the command zone.\nThassa, Deep-Dwelling ↗\nBlinks a creature at every end step and is an indestructible non-creature most of the time. Nearly impossible to answer.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nBlink decks are slow to threaten anything, and a pile of value creatures with no finisher can grind for an hour without winning. Make sure something in the deck converts the accumulated advantage into a victory. The archetype also relies on triggers, which means Torpor Orb and its family shut it off entirely, so keep an answer or two for artifacts and enchantments.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a blink deck in Commander?\nA blink deck exiles its own creatures and returns them immediately to re-trigger their enter-the-battlefield abilities. Repeating a Solemn Simulacrum or Mulldrifter every turn turns a one-shot value creature into an ongoing engine.\nWhat is the difference between blink and flicker?\nNothing. They are two names for the same effect. Flicker comes from the card Flicker; blink from Momentary Blink. Both describe exiling a permanent and returning it, usually immediately and usually under your control.\nWhat colors are blink decks?\nWhite and blue above all. White has the blink spells and most of the value creatures, blue has the card draw and repeatable outlets. Green adds strong blink outlets and big enter-trigger creatures, and black supports a death-and-return variant instead.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Blink? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Blink Commanders →Graveyard recursion →Control →ETB vs. Triggered vs. Activated Abilities →How the Stack Works →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Superfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nAristocrats Mistakes\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,combo,page_text,12204,"Combo Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Artifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nCombo\nDeck Archetype\nCombo: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nEvery other archetype accumulates an advantage until it is big enough to win. A combo deck skips that: it finds two or three specific cards and the game simply ends.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nCombo: Aims to assemble a specific card combination that ends the game.\nWhat Combo Decks Actually Do\nA combo deck is built to assemble a particular interaction that wins on the spot: an infinite loop, a single enormous turn, or an alternate win condition that ignores damage entirely. The rest of the deck exists to find those pieces, protect them, and survive long enough to deploy them.\nThat gives combo an unusual deck structure. It is mostly tutors, card selection, interaction, and mana, with a handful of actual win conditions. The archetype spans a huge power range: a casual deck with one two-card combo as a backup plan and a cEDH deck aiming to win on turn three are both combo decks, and the difference is entirely in how ruthlessly the supporting ninety cards are tuned.\nThe Combo engine\nThree steps that build to a finish. This one ends the game rather than coming back round.\n1 Tutor for the piece Demonic Tutor finds whichever half of it you are missing.\n2 Assemble it Two cards, each unremarkable on its own.\n3 Win on the spot Thassa's Oracle with Demonic Consultation ends the game outright.\nThe last step wins the game, so there is nothing to come back round for.\nWhich Colors Fit Combo\nBlue and black are the combo colors because they are the tutoring and protection colors. Black finds any card; blue protects the turn you use it and supplies the alternate win conditions. Green tutors creatures and produces the huge mana that powers loops, red supplies rituals and its own creature-based loops, and white contributes tutors for artifacts and enchantments plus a handful of famous two-card kills.\nBlue Protection, card selection, and the alternate win conditions. Blue is why a combo turn resolves instead of getting answered.\nBlack Unrestricted tutoring. Black finds the missing piece more cheaply and reliably than any other color.\nGreen Creature tutors and explosive mana. Green assembles combos out of the battlefield rather than out of the hand.\nRed Rituals for a single big turn, plus creature-copy loops that are combos in their own right.\nWhite Artifact and enchantment tutors, and several of the format's best-known two-card kills.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Combo Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding combo for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nDemonic Tutor ↗\nTwo mana, any card, straight to hand. The cleanest tutor ever printed and the backbone of consistency.\nVampiric Tutor ↗\nOne mana at instant speed for a card on top. Slower to use but cheap enough to hold up alongside protection.\nEnlightened Tutor ↗\nFinds artifacts and enchantments at instant speed, which covers a large share of the format's combo pieces.\nThassa's Oracle ↗\nWin the game on its enter trigger with an empty library. The most-played alternate win condition in Commander.\nDemonic Consultation ↗\nExiles your whole library for one card, which is a drawback everywhere except next to Thassa's Oracle, where it is the combo.\nIsochron Scepter ↗\nImprints a cheap instant and recasts it every turn. Half of the format's most famous mana-generating loop.\nDramatic Reversal ↗\nUntaps all your nonland permanents. The other half: with enough mana rocks the loop generates infinite mana.\nKiki-Jiki, Mirror Breaker ↗\nCopies a creature with haste each turn, which is a two-card infinite with any creature that untaps or blinks him.\nProtean Hulk ↗\nFetches six mana value of creatures when it dies, so a single sacrifice assembles a whole combo from the library.\nAd Nauseam ↗\nDraws most of your deck at the cost of life. In a low-curve list it finds every piece at once.\nTop 10 Combo Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nKinnan, Bonder Prodigy ↗\nDoubles mana from nonland sources and cheats big creatures out. One of the strongest mana engines in the format.\nGodo, Bandit Warlord ↗\nTutors Helm of the Host on entry, which grants infinite combat steps. A mono-red one-card combo.\nThrasios, Triton Hero ↗\nConverts infinite mana into drawing your library. The default outlet in cEDH, and legal as a solo commander.\nCaptain Sisay ↗\nTutors any legendary permanent every turn, chaining into a pile of legends that lock or win.\nWinota, Joiner of Forces ↗\nPuts Humans onto the battlefield from the library on attack. Snowballs into a game-ending turn immediately.\nYisan, the Wanderer Bard ↗\nTutors creatures by escalating mana value, assembling a creature combo one verse counter at a time.\nSelvala, Heart of the Wilds ↗\nEnormous mana from a single tap, which turns any big-mana payoff into a kill.\nTivit, Seller of Secrets ↗\nMakes Clue and Treasure tokens through voting, and combos cleanly with untap effects.\nOswald Fiddlebender ↗\nSacrifices an artifact to tutor another onto the battlefield, chaining a combo out of the library for one mana a time.\nKrark, the Thumbless ↗\nCoin-flip spell recursion that goes infinite with the right pairing. The most chaotic combo commander in common play.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nCombo is the archetype where power level is least visible from the outside, and where a mismatch is felt most sharply. A deck that wins on turn four in a pod expecting turn ten is not a better deck, it is a different game. Be specific in the Rule 0 conversation, saying that you have infinite combos and roughly when they land, and know which bracket your build belongs in.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a combo deck in Commander?\nA combo deck is built to assemble a specific set of cards that wins immediately: an infinite loop, a single game-ending turn, or an alternate win condition. Most of the deck is tutors, card selection, protection, and mana, with only a few cards that actually win.\nAre infinite combos allowed in Commander?\nYes, they are legal. A loop with a mandatory repeating action and no way to stop results in a draw, so you must be able to end it, but combos themselves are permitted. Whether a given pod wants them is a social question, which is what Rule 0 and the bracket system exist to settle.\nWhat is the difference between combo and cEDH?\nCombo is a strategy; cEDH is a format-wide power level in which nearly every deck is a tuned combo deck. Plenty of casual decks include a combo as a backup win condition without being anywhere near cEDH. The difference is how efficiently the other ninety cards support it.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Combo? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nSpellslinger →Artifacts →The Commander Bracket System, Explained →What Is EDH Power Level? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Artifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,control,page_text,12250,"Control Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Tribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nControl\nDeck Archetype\nControl: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nControl decks answer things. The hard part in Commander is that there are three times as many things to answer, and only one of you.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nControl: Answers everything and wins late, once the table has run out of threats.\nWhat Control Decks Actually Do\nA control deck aims to neutralise every threat that matters until it can deploy a win condition safely. Counterspells, removal, board wipes, and card draw make up most of the list, and the actual way it wins is often two or three cards deep in a ninety-nine-card deck.\nMultiplayer changes the archetype more than any other. In a duel, one-for-one answers are fine because there is one opponent; in Commander a Counterspell trades with one card out of three opponents' hands, and you fall behind by answering everything. Successful control decks therefore lean on effects that answer many things at once (board wipes, mass bounce, taxation) and on card draw that keeps up with three players drawing at the same time.\nThe Control engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Hold mana open Untapped mana is the threat, whether or not you have the card.\n2 Answer the threat Counterspell on the stack, or Swords to Plowshares after it lands.\n3 Untap They spent a card and a turn; you spent a card.\n↻ Win late, once three opponents have run out of things to try.\nWhich Colors Fit Control\nBlue is the archetype, effectively by definition: counterspells and the deepest card draw both live there. White supplies the board wipes and the cheapest unconditional removal, and black supplies targeted removal and its own sweepers. Red and green participate least: red's answers are damage-based and narrow, and green's are one-for-one artifact and enchantment removal rather than broad control tools.\nBlue Counterspells, card draw, and mass bounce. The color the archetype cannot exist without.\nWhite Board wipes and the most efficient unconditional removal in the format. Answers many threats at once, which is what multiplayer demands.\nBlack Targeted removal that hits anything, plus sweepers that get around indestructible.\nRed Narrow support: artifact removal and damage-based sweepers. Rarely a core control color.\nGreen The least suited. Green answers artifacts and enchantments well and almost nothing else on the control axis.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Control Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding control for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nCounterspell ↗\nTwo mana, no conditions. Still the baseline every other counterspell is measured against.\nCyclonic Rift ↗\nOverloaded, it returns every nonland permanent your opponents control at instant speed. The single most game-swinging card in the archetype.\nSwords to Plowshares ↗\nOne mana to exile any creature. The life gained is close to irrelevant at forty.\nWrath of God ↗\nFour mana, all creatures, no regeneration. Answering three players' boards with one card is how control keeps pace.\nToxic Deluge ↗\nA scaling sweeper paid for in life, which gets around indestructible and large creatures alike.\nRhystic Study ↗\nTaxes every spell the table casts, and either draws you cards or slows everyone down. Both outcomes are what control wants.\nMystic Remora ↗\nDraws enormously in the early turns for one mana, then retires. The best cheap draw engine in multiplayer.\nFierce Guardianship ↗\nA free counterspell with your commander out, which lets you tap out on your own turn and still hold up interaction.\nFarewell ↗\nModal exile of any combination of artifacts, creatures, enchantments, and graveyards. One card that answers whole strategies.\nNevinyrral's Disk ↗\nA repeatable-threat sweeper that hits artifacts and enchantments too, and is available in any color.\nTop 10 Control Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nBaral, Chief of Compliance ↗\nCheaper counterspells and loot on every counter. Mono-blue control with the draw problem partly solved.\nTeferi, Temporal Archmage ↗\nA planeswalker commander that untaps four permanents each turn, so you counter on their turn and still develop on yours.\nAzor, the Lawbringer ↗\nA sphinx that draws a large chunk of a deck and stops opponents casting instants and sorceries for a turn.\nDragonlord Ojutai ↗\nHexproof while untapped and dig on damage. A control finisher that survives everything a control deck fears.\nDragonlord Silumgar ↗\nSteals a creature or planeswalker and keeps stealing as he is recast. Removal that becomes your threat.\nThe Scarab God ↗\nScry and drain each upkeep, exiles graveyards for Zombies, and returns to hand instead of dying. Nearly unanswerable.\nErtai Resurrected ↗\nFlash plus a modal counter or removal on entry, and he returns to the command zone rather than dying.\nRashmi, Eternities Crafter ↗\nFree spells off the top on your first spell each turn, which fixes control's card-advantage math.\nNymris, Oona's Trickster ↗\nDraws whenever you cast on an opponent's turn, exactly the play pattern control already wants.\nDamia, Sage of Stone ↗\nRefills you to seven cards every upkeep, which makes trading one-for-one with three opponents sustainable.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nThe archetype's real risk is being the table's police officer. If you counter everything, three players learn that you are the problem, and in a format where they can all attack you at once that is a losing position regardless of how good your answers are. Let some things resolve, save your counterspells for what actually beats you, and make sure the deck can close a game rather than only prolong it.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nIs control good in multiplayer Commander?\nIt works, but it needs building differently than a duel control deck. One-for-one answers fall behind against three opponents, so multiplayer control leans on board wipes, mass bounce, and taxation effects that answer several players at once, plus card draw that keeps pace with a whole table.\nWhat colors are control decks in Commander?\nBlue above all, for counterspells and card draw. White adds board wipes and efficient removal, black adds unconditional targeted removal. Azorius, Dimir, and Esper are the classic control color combinations.\nHow does a control deck actually win?\nUsually with a small number of resilient threats deployed once the board is stable: a hexproof commander, a large flier, or an alternate win condition. Some control decks win by decking opponents or with a single combo instead, since the deck already plays the interaction needed to protect it.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Control? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nStax →Spellslinger →How the Stack Works →Priority and Responding to Spells →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Tribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,counters,page_text,12173,"+1/+1 Counters Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Group hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nCounters\nDeck Archetype\nCounters: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nCounters are permanent in a way that pump spells are not. A deck built on them grows every turn and keeps everything it has gained, even through a wrath, if the right pieces survive.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nCounters: Built around +1/+1 counters and the effects that multiply them.\nWhat Counters Decks Actually Do\nCounter decks accumulate plus-one-plus-one counters on their creatures and run payoffs that scale with the total. The distinguishing feature is compounding: counter doublers multiply everything placed after them, so a modest board becomes an overwhelming one in a single turn without drawing a single extra card.\nThe archetype overlaps with almost everything else here. Proliferate ties it to superfriends, token production ties it to go-wide, and the largest creatures in the format are usually counter payoffs. Its own identity comes from the doublers and the counter-manipulation cards: the pieces that do nothing on their own and change the scale of everything else.\nThe Counters engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Put a counter on it One at a time is fine, because the deck cares about what happens next.\n2 Multiply it Hardened Scales and Corpsejack Menace change the arithmetic.\n3 Keep them The Ozolith holds everything you built when the creature dies.\n↻ Each doubler makes every counter after it worth more.\nWhich Colors Fit Counters\nGreen is the primary color by a distance, holding the doublers, the hydras, and most of the payoffs. White adds counter-based anthems and token synergies, blue adds proliferate and evolve, and black adds the minus-one-minus-one counter variant, which uses the same infrastructure aimed at opponents' creatures instead. Red participates least.\nGreen Doublers, hydras, and the deepest pool of creatures that arrive with counters already on them.\nWhite Counter-based anthems and the token generation that gives the counters somewhere to go.\nBlue Proliferate and evolve, which add counters without spending cards on them.\nBlack The inverse: minus-one-minus-one counters used as removal, running on the same doublers and proliferate.\nRed Rarely a core color. Red contributes a handful of counter payoffs and little of the infrastructure.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Counters Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding counters for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nHardened Scales ↗\nOne extra counter every time counters are placed. A one-drop that quietly doubles the deck's output all game.\nBranching Evolution ↗\nDoubles counters placed on your creatures. The cleanest and most reliable multiplier in the archetype.\nCorpsejack Menace ↗\nThe same effect on a 4/4 body, so it doubles counters and pressures the board at the same time.\nThe Ozolith ↗\nCollects counters from creatures that die and moves them to a new creature. Makes removal cost you a body, not your investment.\nKalonian Hydra ↗\nDoubles the counters on every creature you control on attack. Two attacks usually ends the game.\nEvolution Sage ↗\nProliferates on every land drop, adding counters to every creature and planeswalker for free, every turn.\nMaster Biomancer ↗\nEvery creature you play enters with counters equal to its power and becomes a Mutant. Scales the whole deck at once.\nConclave Mentor ↗\nAn extra counter on placement plus lifegain when it dies. Cheap redundancy for the Hardened Scales effect.\nInspiring Call ↗\nDraws for every creature with a counter and makes them indestructible. Card advantage and a wrath answer in one instant.\nSimic Ascendancy ↗\nTracks growth counters and wins outright at twenty. An alternate win condition that costs the deck nothing to include.\nTop 10 Counters Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nEzuri, Claw of Progress ↗\nExperience counters that pump a creature every turn, growing permanently across the game.\nAnimar, Soul of Elements ↗\nGrows on every creature cast and reduces their cost, so the deck accelerates as it gets bigger.\nVorel of the Hull Clade ↗\nDoubles counters on a permanent every turn, including artifacts and lands with counters on them.\nHamza, Guardian of Arashin ↗\nCreatures cost one less per creature with a counter, which makes a developed board fuel enormous turns.\nKathril, Aspect Warper ↗\nDistributes counters based on keywords in your graveyard, tying counters to a self-mill plan.\nAnafenza, the Foremost ↗\nCounters on attack plus graveyard hate stapled on, which shuts down the recursion decks that beat you.\nShalai and Hallar ↗\nTurns counter placement into direct damage to every opponent, so growth is also a clock.\nRenata, Called to the Hunt ↗\nEvery creature enters with an extra counter. Mono-green, cheap, and it works with everything.\nRoalesk, Apex Hybrid ↗\nArrives with counters and proliferates twice when it dies, so removal still advances your board.\nHapatra, Vizier of Poisons ↗\nThe minus-one-minus-one side: she turns counters into Snake tokens and doubles as repeatable removal.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nThe doublers are the deck and they are all noncreature permanents, so a Vandalblast or a Back to Nature hurts more than a board wipe does. Spread the effect across creatures and enchantments rather than relying on one, and remember that counters do not survive a creature dying unless you have The Ozolith or similar. The archetype looks resilient but is only as durable as the bodies carrying it.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a +1/+1 counters deck in Commander?\nIt is a deck that accumulates plus-one-plus-one counters on its creatures and plays payoffs that scale with the total. Counter doublers like Branching Evolution multiply everything placed afterwards, so the board grows faster the longer the deck runs.\nWhat colors are best for counters?\nGreen first, for the doublers, hydras, and most payoffs. White adds anthems and tokens, blue adds proliferate and evolve. Simic and Selesnya are the most common pairings, with Abzan popular for combining tokens, counters, and recursion.\nDo +1/+1 counters stay if a creature leaves the battlefield?\nNo. A creature that leaves the battlefield returns as a new object with no counters, so all the accumulated growth is lost. Cards like The Ozolith and Skullbriar, the Walking Grave exist specifically to preserve counters through that, which is why they are archetype staples.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Counters? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest +1/+1 Counter Commanders →Go wide →Superfriends →Voltron →How Combat Works →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Group hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,enchantress,page_text,12308,"Enchantress Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Voltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nEnchantress\nDeck Archetype\nEnchantress: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nEnchantress solves the oldest problem with enchantments (they cost a card and do not affect the board immediately) by making every one of them replace itself.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nEnchantress: Built around a critical mass of enchantments that draw cards as they resolve.\nWhat Enchantress Decks Actually Do\nEnchantress decks run a critical mass of enchantments alongside creatures and enchantments that draw a card whenever you cast one. The archetype is named after Verduran Enchantress and its successors, and it has been recognisably the same deck since the 1990s: play a cheap draw engine, then cast enchantments until you have drawn most of your library.\nWhat makes it work in Commander specifically is that enchantments are the hardest permanent type for most decks to remove. A resolved enchantment tends to stay resolved, so a deck that accumulates twelve of them builds a board that green and black opponents often simply cannot interact with. The win condition is usually secondary: a Sigil of the Empty Throne making Angels, a Sphere of Safety making the deck unattackable while it assembles something better.\nThe Enchantress engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Cast an enchantment Cheap ones are fine. The trigger is what you are paying for.\n2 Draw for it Argothian Enchantress replaces every enchantment as it resolves.\n3 Cast what you drew The deck refills faster than it spends, so the turn keeps going.\n↻ Two enchantresses out and the deck stops missing land drops.\nWhich Colors Fit Enchantress\nGreen and white own this archetype almost outright. Green has the enchantress draw effects themselves and the mana to deploy them; white has the pillowfort enchantments, the mass recursion, and the payoffs. Blue appears in Bant builds for copy effects, and black shows up in Orzhov constellation shells, but Selesnya is the beating heart of it.\nGreen The draw engines themselves (Argothian Enchantress and its family) plus enchantment-based ramp.\nWhite Pillowfort effects that buy time, mass recursion like Replenish, and the enchantment payoffs that actually win.\nBlue Copy effects and card selection in Bant builds. A supporting color rather than a core one.\nBlack Constellation payoffs and removal in Orzhov shells, where enchantments drain rather than defend.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Enchantress Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding enchantress for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nArgothian Enchantress ↗\nA one-mana body with shroud that draws on every enchantment. The best draw engine in the archetype and the hardest to remove.\nEnchantress's Presence ↗\nThe same effect on an enchantment, which means it also triggers your other enchantresses when it resolves.\nEidolon of Blossoms ↗\nConstellation draw, so it triggers on enchantments entering rather than being cast, so it catches tokens and recursion too.\nSetessan Champion ↗\nDraws and grows on each constellation trigger, turning the draw engine into a genuine threat.\nSigil of the Empty Throne ↗\nA 4/4 Angel for every enchantment cast. The most common way an enchantress deck actually ends a game.\nSphere of Safety ↗\nAttacking you costs mana per enchantment you control, which in this deck quickly means nobody can attack at all.\nStarfield of Nyx ↗\nReturns an enchantment from your graveyard each upkeep and animates the whole board once you have five.\nSanctum Weaver ↗\nTaps for mana equal to your enchantment count. Ramp that scales with exactly the thing the deck is already doing.\nSerra's Sanctum ↗\nA land that taps for white mana per enchantment. Expensive to acquire, but no other card matches its ceiling here.\nReplenish ↗\nReturns every enchantment from your graveyard to the battlefield at once. The archetype's answer to a board wipe, and often a win on the spot.\nTop 10 Enchantress Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nSythis, Harvest's Hand ↗\nDraws and gains life on every enchantment cast, from a two-mana command zone. The most efficient starting point in the archetype.\nTuvasa the Sunlit ↗\nDraws on your first enchantment each turn and grows with the count, doubling as a Voltron threat.\nEstrid, the Masked ↗\nA planeswalker commander that untaps your enchanted permanents and recurs auras from the graveyard.\nZur the Enchanter ↗\nTutors an enchantment directly onto the battlefield on every attack, which makes the deck brutally consistent.\nDaxos the Returned ↗\nBuilds experience counters on enchantment casts and spends them on scaling Spirit tokens.\nCalix, Destiny's Hand ↗\nA planeswalker that finds enchantments, taps down threats, and returns auras. Removal and card advantage in one.\nKestia, the Cultivator ↗\nBestow lets her dodge removal, and she draws for each enchanted creature attacking.\nSiona, Captain of the Pyleas ↗\nA token per aura and a lethal combo with Shielded by Faith. The aggressive aura variant of the archetype.\nSigarda, Host of Herons ↗\nHexproof and immunity to sacrifice effects, which protects a heavy aura investment from the things auras usually lose to.\nGylwain, Casting Director ↗\nMakes a free Role token on every creature you cast, so the enchantment count climbs without spending cards.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nEnchantress can draw twenty cards and still not win. The archetype's classic failure is assembling a beautiful, unattackable board with no way to close, while the table plays around it. Decide early how the deck wins (Angels, an aura-based commander kill, or a combo) and run enough copies of that plan that drawing half your library actually finds one.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is an enchantress deck in Commander?\nAn enchantress deck plays a critical mass of enchantments alongside effects that draw a card whenever you cast one, named after cards like Argothian Enchantress. The draw engines chain into each other, so a resolved enchantress plus a hand of cheap enchantments can refill your hand every turn.\nWhat colors are enchantress decks in MTG?\nGreen and white. Green supplies the enchantress draw effects and enchantment ramp; white supplies the pillowfort defence, mass recursion, and the payoffs that win. Blue appears in Bant builds and black in Orzhov constellation shells.\nHow do enchantress decks win?\nMost commonly with a payoff that converts the enchantment count into a threat: Sigil of the Empty Throne making Angels, Starfield of Nyx animating the board, or an aura-stacked commander dealing commander damage. The draw engine finds the win condition; it is not the win condition itself.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Enchantress? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nVoltron →Stax →Control →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Voltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,go-wide,page_text,12237,"Go Wide Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Graveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nGo wide\nDeck Archetype\nGo Wide: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nGo wide is the opposite bet from Voltron: instead of one creature nobody can kill, twenty creatures nobody can kill all of. The whole deck is a setup for one enormous attack.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nGo wide: Floods the board with tokens and wins with numbers.\nWhat Go wide Decks Actually Do\nGo-wide decks produce creature tokens in bulk and win by attacking with all of them at once. Individually the tokens are irrelevant: a 1/1 does nothing. The deck's job is to reach a number of bodies where one anthem, one trample effect, or one damage-per-creature trigger converts the board into lethal across the whole table.\nStructurally that means three layers: token producers that scale, multipliers that turn every token trigger into two or three, and a finisher that cashes the board in. It is the archetype with the widest gap between its worst board state and its best. Five tokens is nothing; fifteen tokens plus a Craterhoof Behemoth ends the game on the spot.\nThe Go wide engine\nThree steps that build to a finish. This one ends the game rather than coming back round.\n1 Make tokens A few every turn, from whatever the deck is doing anyway.\n2 Double them Doubling Season and Anointed Procession turn three into six.\n3 Attack with all of it Cathars' Crusade means the board grows as it swings.\nThe last step wins the game, so there is nothing to come back round for.\nWhich Colors Fit Go wide\nWhite is the best token color in the format by a wide margin, both for making them and for the anthems that make them matter. Green supplies the mass-pump finishers and the token doublers, red brings goblins and damage-per-creature payoffs, and black overlaps heavily with Aristocrats by turning the same tokens into drain triggers. Blue is the odd color out.\nWhite The best token generation and nearly every anthem effect. A go-wide deck without white is choosing a harder path on purpose.\nGreen Token doublers and the mass-pump-with-trample finishers that turn a wide board into a single lethal attack.\nRed Goblins, haste, and damage-per-creature effects that let the board win without attacking at all.\nBlack Drain payoffs and sacrifice outlets: the overlap with Aristocrats, where the tokens are fuel rather than attackers.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Go wide Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding go wide for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nDoubling Season ↗\nDoubles tokens and counters. Expensive, format-defining, and the single most impactful card the archetype can draw.\nAnointed Procession ↗\nThe white half of the effect at four mana, and the reason token decks run several doublers rather than one.\nParallel Lives ↗\nThe green half. Stacking two doublers quadruples output, which is how a modest token engine becomes a lethal one in a turn.\nCathars' Crusade ↗\nA plus-one-plus-one counter on everything for every creature that enters. Grows the whole board geometrically and ends games quickly.\nDivine Visitation ↗\nEvery token becomes a 4/4 Angel with flying and vigilance instead. Turns quantity directly into quality.\nCraterhoof Behemoth ↗\nMass pump and trample on a haste body. The archetype's most reliable one-card kill from a developed board.\nIntangible Virtue ↗\nTwo mana for a token-only anthem plus vigilance, which is exactly the rate a deck that makes twelve of them wants.\nSkullclamp ↗\nEquip a 1/1, it dies, draw two. Converts surplus tokens into a card-draw engine and fixes the archetype's biggest weakness.\nImpact Tremors ↗\nA damage to each opponent per creature entering. Wins without combat, which matters against decks full of blockers.\nSecure the Wastes ↗\nAn instant-speed X tokens, so it dodges sorcery-speed wraths and can be held until the turn it is lethal.\nTop 10 Go wide Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nKrenko, Mob Boss ↗\nDoubles your goblin count on every tap. The fastest token accumulation in the format if it survives a rotation.\nAdeline, Resplendent Cathar ↗\nA Human token per opponent on every attack, and she grows with the board. Aggro and go-wide in the same card.\nRhys the Redeemed ↗\nA one-drop that first makes tokens and then copies your entire board. The cleanest scaling in the archetype.\nTrostani, Selesnya's Voice ↗\nPopulate plus lifegain on every creature entering, which buys the turns a wide board needs to set up.\nGhave, Guru of Spores ↗\nConverts counters into tokens and back. The bridge between go-wide, counters, and aristocrats, and a combo engine besides.\nJetmir, Nexus of Revels ↗\nEscalating buffs at three, six, and nine attackers. A commander that states the archetype's thresholds as text.\nProssh, Skyraider of Kher ↗\nKobolds scaling with the commander tax you have already paid, and a built-in sacrifice outlet.\nThe Locust God ↗\nA hasty flier per card drawn. The blue exception, powered by draw spells rather than by anthems.\nPurphoros, God of the Forge ↗\nTwo damage per creature entering, from an indestructible non-creature body. Wins through blockers and through wraths.\nEmmara, Soul of the Accord ↗\nA token whenever she taps, including for convoke, so she produces every turn without ever attacking.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nOne board wipe undoes an hour of work, and go-wide is the archetype wraths are printed to punish. Hold something back, run protection like Heroic Intervention or Boros Charm, and resist the urge to dump your whole hand before you can actually win. The second failure mode is quieter: a wide board with no finisher just sits there, so make sure the deck can convert numbers into damage.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a go-wide deck in Commander?\nA go-wide deck creates many small creature tokens and wins by attacking with all of them at once, usually after playing an anthem, a mass-pump effect, or a payoff that deals damage for each creature. The individual tokens are weak; the board as a whole is the threat.\nWhat is the best color for tokens in Commander?\nWhite. It has the deepest token generation and nearly every anthem effect, and it pairs naturally with green for doublers and mass pump, or with black for sacrifice payoffs. Selesnya and Naya are the most common go-wide color combinations.\nHow do go-wide decks deal with board wipes?\nBy not overcommitting, and by running protection. Keeping token producers in hand, playing instant-speed makers like Secure the Wastes, and holding an effect that grants indestructible or hexproof all let a deck rebuild or simply survive the wrath it knows is coming.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Go wide? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Go-Wide / Token Commanders →Aristocrats →Counters →Tribal →The Commander Deck Template →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Graveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,graveyard-recursion,page_text,13038,"Graveyard Recursion Commander Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nGraveyard recursion\nDeck Archetype\nGraveyard Recursion: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nMost decks treat the graveyard as where cards go to stop mattering. A recursion deck treats it as a second hand, one that fills itself up, and that opponents mostly cannot interact with.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nGraveyard recursion: Cards come back from the graveyard, treating it as a second hand.\nWhat Graveyard recursion Decks Actually Do\nGraveyard recursion decks put powerful cards into the graveyard on purpose and then bring them back. That covers two closely related plans: reanimator, which discards or mills a huge creature and cheats it onto the battlefield years before its mana cost, and value recursion, which loops smaller creatures and spells over and over for incremental advantage.\nThe engine has three parts, and a deck needs all three: a way to fill the graveyard (self-mill, discard outlets, entomb effects, or simply trading in combat), a way to get things back (reanimation spells, recursive commanders, or Eternal Witness-style bodies), and payoffs worth the effort. Miss one and the deck stalls. A hand full of reanimation spells with an empty graveyard is a hand full of blanks.\nThe Graveyard recursion engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Something dies Or you put it there yourself. Entomb costs one mana.\n2 Bring it back Reanimate pays a fraction of what casting it would have cost.\n3 It dies again Nothing is spent for good, so the same threat answers removal twice.\n↻ The graveyard is a second hand you keep drawing from.\nWhich Colors Fit Graveyard recursion\nBlack is non-negotiable. It owns reanimation as a color-pie concept, and almost every deck in the archetype is at least partly black. Green is the strongest partner, contributing self-mill and the recursion of any card type rather than just creatures. White returns small creatures and permanents cheaply, and blue supplies the self-mill and looting that fills the yard in the first place.\nBlack The core. Reanimation spells, tutors that put cards directly into the graveyard, and the sacrifice outlets that get them there.\nGreen Regrowth effects that return any card type, plus creature-based recursion that is much harder to remove than a spell.\nWhite Cheap, repeatable return-to-battlefield effects, usually with a mana value cap. Excellent at grinding, poor at cheating out a giant.\nBlue Self-mill and looting: the fill half of the engine. Blue rarely reanimates on its own but is the best color at deciding what ends up in the yard.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Graveyard recursion Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding graveyard recursion for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nReanimate ↗\nOne black mana for any creature in any graveyard. The most efficient reanimation spell ever printed, and the life loss rarely matters at 40.\nAnimate Dead ↗\nTwo mana, sticks around as an enchantment, and hits any graveyard. The redundancy that makes the plan consistent.\nEntomb ↗\nOne mana to put exactly the card you want into your own graveyard. Turns a reanimation spell into a tutor for anything.\nBuried Alive ↗\nThree creatures into the yard at once, which sets up both a reanimation spell and a mass-return effect in a single card.\nLiving Death ↗\nEveryone's graveyard creatures swap with the battlefield. In a deck built for it this is a one-sided board wipe that also rebuilds your team.\nEternal Witness ↗\nReturns any card from your graveyard on a body that can itself be recurred. The archetype's most-copied effect for a reason.\nVictimize ↗\nSacrifice one creature, return two tapped. Converts a spare token into your two best creatures at instant-speed value.\nPersist ↗\nTwo mana reanimation with a real drawback (the creature comes back with a minus-one-minus-one counter) that costs you almost nothing on a large body.\nSun Titan ↗\nRecurs a permanent worth three or less on every attack. The white grind engine that keeps rebuilding after a wrath.\nNecromancy ↗\nAnimate Dead with an instant-speed flash mode, which lets you reanimate in response to removal or at the end of an opponent's turn.\nTop 10 Graveyard recursion Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nMeren of Clan Nel Toth ↗\nFree recursion every end step, scaling with experience counters. The definitive value-recursion commander.\nMuldrotha, the Gravetide ↗\nOne permanent of each type per turn from the graveyard. Turns lands, artifacts, and enchantments into recursion targets, not just creatures.\nKarador, Ghost Chieftain ↗\nCosts less the fuller your graveyard is, and casts a creature from it every turn. Built to be milled.\nAlesha, Who Smiles at Death ↗\nReanimates a small creature on attack, for free, at a mana value cap that hides an enormous number of powerful bodies.\nChainer, Nightmare Adept ↗\nDiscard a card to cast a creature from your graveyard with haste: the fill and the payoff in one activated ability.\nSheoldred, Whispering One ↗\nReturns a creature to you and forces a sacrifice from everyone else each turn. A commander that attacks the archetype from both sides.\nThe Mimeoplasm ↗\nExiles two creatures from graveyards and becomes a hybrid of them. Rewards milling the whole table, not just yourself.\nGisa and Geralf ↗\nSelf-mill on cast plus one graveyard Zombie per turn, the natural bridge between recursion and tribal.\nSedris, the Traitor King ↗\nGrants unearth to everything in your graveyard, so any creature you mill becomes a hasty one-shot attacker.\nVarina, Lich Queen ↗\nDraw-and-discard on attack, which fills the yard while it builds the board. Fill and payoff on the same trigger.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nGraveyard hate is the most commonly played sideboard-style effect in Commander, and a single Rest in Peace can turn the deck off entirely. Build in a way that survives it: keep some number of cards that are fine cast from hand, run answers to enchantments and artifacts, and avoid a line where every win requires the graveyard to be intact.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a graveyard recursion deck in Commander?\nIt is a deck that deliberately puts cards into its own graveyard and then returns them to the battlefield or hand. This includes reanimator decks, which discard or mill a large creature and cheat it into play early, and value decks that repeatedly loop smaller creatures and spells for incremental advantage.\nWhat is the difference between reanimator and graveyard value?\nReanimator is explosive and aims to put one enormous threat onto the battlefield far ahead of schedule. Graveyard value grinds. It returns modest creatures and spells over and over so it always has more resources than everyone else. They use the same tools and often appear in the same deck.\nHow do you beat graveyard decks in Commander?\nExile rather than destroy. Effects that exile a graveyard, replace the graveyard entirely, or exile creatures as they die shut the plan down far more effectively than creature removal, because removing a reanimated creature usually just gives it back to the deck as a fresh target.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Graveyard recursion? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Reanimator Commanders →Common Graveyard Recursion & Reanimator Mistakes →Best Budget Graveyard Recursion Upgrades →Aristocrats →Mill →Best Recursion in Commander →Best Graveyard Hate in Commander →The Commander Deck Template →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,group-hug,page_text,12464,"Group Hug Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Control\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nGroup hug\nDeck Archetype\nGroup Hug: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nGroup hug gives everyone extra cards, extra lands, and extra mana. The interesting question, the one the archetype lives or dies on, is what you do with the game you have just accelerated for four people.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nGroup hug: Gives the whole table resources, and wins from the goodwill it creates.\nWhat Group hug Decks Actually Do\nGroup hug decks hand out resources to the whole table: extra draws, extra land drops, extra mana, sometimes extra creatures. In exchange they buy something less tangible: nobody attacks the player giving them cards, and a deck that is never anyone's first target gets to play a very long game unmolested.\nThe archetype splits into two honest halves. Pure group hug is a social deck that genuinely wants a bigger, faster, more interactive game and is relaxed about winning. Everything else is group hug as a disguise: accelerate the table, take the political cover it provides, and then convert the enormous shared resources into a win that only works because everyone has a full hand. Both are legitimate; being clear about which one you brought is the polite part.\nThe Group hug engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Give everyone cards Howling Mine draws the whole table an extra one each turn.\n2 Nobody attacks you You are the reason their turns are worth taking.\n3 Take more than you gave You built to use the extra resources. They did not.\n↻ The table keeps you alive right up until it should not have.\nWhich Colors Fit Group hug\nBlue, white, and green cover most of the archetype. Blue draws the cards, white makes and shares the resources, and green supplies the extra land drops and mana. Red appears in chaos-flavoured builds, and black is the odd color out. It has the drain payoffs that punish the table for the cards you gave them, which is the group-slug variant rather than the hug.\nBlue Table-wide draw effects, and the card advantage that lets you use the shared resources better than anyone else.\nWhite Shared resource generation and the pillowfort effects that make attacking you unattractive on top of impolite.\nGreen Extra land drops and mana for everyone, which accelerates the game more than any draw effect does.\nRed Chaos and randomness in the more anarchic builds, plus damage payoffs for handing out cards.\nBlack The group-slug angle: drain and discard payoffs that turn everyone's full hand into their problem.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Group hug Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding group hug for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nHowling Mine ↗\nAn extra card for everyone each turn. The original group hug card, and still the cleanest statement of the plan.\nFont of Mythos ↗\nTwo extra cards for everyone. Doubles the effect and doubles the goodwill.\nRites of Flourishing ↗\nAn extra card and an extra land drop for every player. Accelerates the whole table meaningfully, not just cosmetically.\nDictate of Kruphix ↗\nFlash makes it land at the end of the turn before yours, so you are the first to benefit from it.\nHeartbeat of Spring ↗\nLands produce double mana for everyone. Enormous, and it favours whoever has the best mana sink.\nHorn of Greed ↗\nA card for every land drop by anyone. In a table with extra land drops going around this draws absurdly.\nKami of the Crescent Moon ↗\nA cheap, repeatable draw for everyone on a body, and a lightning rod that is not you.\nMinds Aglow ↗\nA joined-forces draw where every player contributes mana. Generous, and it refills your hand for a fraction of the cost.\nCollective Voyage ↗\nThe land version of the same idea. Ramps the whole table, and you chose the timing.\nBraids, Conjurer Adept ↗\nEveryone puts a permanent onto the battlefield from hand each turn. The most generous effect in the archetype and the most easily abused.\nTop 10 Group hug Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nKynaios and Tiro of Meletis ↗\nA card and a land for everyone each turn. The archetype's flagship, and four colors of tools to exploit it.\nZedruu the Greathearted ↗\nGives away permanents and draws and gains life for each. Turns generosity into a genuine engine.\nPhelddagrif ↗\nThe original hug: every ability helps you and helps an opponent. Old, strange, and beloved.\nKwain, Itinerant Meddler ↗\nEach player may draw and gain life. Cheap, symmetrical, and a famous engine piece for decks that draw better than the table.\nSelvala, Explorer Returned ↗\nParley gives everyone cards and you the mana. Generous in appearance, lopsided in practice.\nEdric, Spymaster of Trest ↗\nEveryone draws for connecting with a creature, which points the whole table away from you and at each other.\nBraids, Conjurer Adept ↗\nFree permanents for all, every turn. You are the one who built a deck around abusing it.\nKenrith, the Returned King ↗\nFive abilities anyone can activate, in five colors. The most flexible political commander in the format.\nXyris, the Writhing Storm ↗\nForces opponents to draw and rewards you with a Snake for each. The clearest hug-into-win pipeline.\nNekusar, the Mindrazer ↗\nThe group-slug inversion: everyone draws extra and takes damage for it. The same cards, aimed the other way.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nGroup hug earns a reputation quickly, and it is usually deserved. A table that has been handed forty cards over eight turns and then loses to a Psychosis Crawler will remember. Decide what your deck is, say so at the start, and if it is a hug-into-kill deck, tell people. The strategy is fine; the ambush is what causes friction. The other failure mode is the sincere one: a genuine hug deck can accelerate a stronger deck into winning far sooner than it would have.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a group hug deck in Commander?\nA group hug deck gives resources to every player (extra card draw, extra land drops, extra mana) in exchange for political goodwill and the freedom to play a long game unthreatened. Some are genuinely social decks; others use the generosity as setup for a win that needs everyone to have a full hand.\nHow do group hug decks win?\nMost commonly by exploiting the resources they handed out: damage per card drawn, a payoff for maximum hand size, or a combo assembled while nobody was attacking them. Some pure hug decks barely try to win at all, treating the archetype as a way to make everyone else's game more interesting.\nWhat is the difference between group hug and group slug?\nGroup hug gives resources away and profits from the goodwill. Group slug gives them away and profits from the damage: cards like Nekusar, the Mindrazer turn every extra draw into life loss. They share almost all the same enablers and differ entirely in the payoff.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Group hug? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nStax →Control →What Is Rule 0? →The Ultimate Guide to Hosting Commander Night →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Control\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,lands-matter,page_text,12352,"Lands Matter Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Aggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nLands matter\nDeck Archetype\nLands Matter: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nMost decks treat lands as the cost of playing Magic. A lands deck treats them as the payoff: the one permanent type almost nobody at the table can interact with.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nLands matter: Landfall and land-recursion turn the mana base itself into the engine.\nWhat Lands matter Decks Actually Do\nLands-matter decks build around the land drop itself. Landfall triggers pay out whenever a land enters, extra land drops mean several payouts a turn, and recursion effects return lands from the graveyard so the same card triggers landfall repeatedly.\nWhat makes it strong in Commander is the interaction profile. Almost nobody runs land destruction, and board wipes do not touch lands, so an engine built on them survives what kills every other archetype. The deck also blurs the line with ramp: it accelerates as a side effect of doing its actual job, which means it is rarely short on mana for the enormous payoffs it eventually casts.\nThe Lands matter engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Play a land One a turn, or several with the right permanent out.\n2 Landfall triggers Lotus Cobra turns each land drop into extra mana.\n3 Get it back Ramunap Excavator plays lands out of the graveyard again.\n↻ The mana base is the engine, so it never stops being live.\nWhich Colors Fit Lands matter\nGreen owns this outright: landfall, land tutoring, extra land drops, and land recursion are all overwhelmingly green. Black is the strongest partner, adding graveyard recursion that treats lands like any other card. Blue contributes the draw-on-landfall effects that make the deck refuel, red adds land-based damage and sacrifice-for-value lands, and white participates least.\nGreen Landfall payoffs, extra land drops, land tutors, and the recursion that makes the engine loop. The archetype's foundation.\nBlack Graveyard recursion applied to lands, plus the sacrifice effects that put them there deliberately.\nBlue Draw-on-landfall effects, which turn every land drop into a card and keep the deck from running dry.\nRed Damage tied to lands and effects that sacrifice lands for value, knowing you will get them back.\nWhite The least represented, contributing a handful of land recursion and mass land-fetch effects.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Lands matter Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding lands matter for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nLotus Cobra ↗\nA Treasure-like mana boost on every landfall trigger. Turns a fetchland into acceleration and snowballs from turn two.\nCrucible of Worlds ↗\nPlay lands from your graveyard. The card that turns every fetchland and sacrifice land into a repeatable engine.\nRamunap Excavator ↗\nThe same effect on a creature, which means green can tutor for it. Redundancy is what makes the engine reliable.\nLife from the Loam ↗\nDredge back three lands a turn. The most efficient land-recursion engine ever printed, and it never runs out.\nSplendid Reclamation ↗\nReturns every land from your graveyard to the battlefield at once, triggering landfall for each of them.\nAvenger of Zendikar ↗\nA Plant per land, then pumps them all on every landfall. The archetype's most reliable one-card win condition.\nField of the Dead ↗\nA 2/2 Zombie for every land drop once you have seven differently named lands. Free bodies from a land slot.\nExploration ↗\nAn extra land drop every turn for one mana. Doubles the trigger count for the whole game.\nTireless Tracker ↗\nA Clue on every landfall and counters on itself when you crack them. Card advantage that also becomes a threat.\nAftermath Analyst ↗\nSelf-mills, then returns every land from your graveyard when sacrificed. A modern Splendid Reclamation on a body.\nTop 10 Lands matter Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nLord Windgrace ↗\nA planeswalker commander that recurs lands, draws, and makes 2/2 Cats. Purpose-built for the archetype.\nThe Gitrog Monster ↗\nDraws on every land put into the graveyard and eats a land each upkeep. Value engine and infamous combo piece.\nTitania, Protector of Argoth ↗\nA 5/3 Elemental for every land that hits your graveyard. Converts a fetchland into an army.\nTatyova, Benthic Druid ↗\nA card and a life on every landfall. The friendliest entry point to the archetype and still one of the best.\nOmnath, Locus of Creation ↗\nEscalating payouts on the first, second, and third landfall each turn. Rewards extra land drops directly.\nAesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait ↗\nAn extra land drop and a card on your first land each turn, Tatyova plus Exploration in the command zone.\nMultani, Yavimaya's Avatar ↗\nGrows with lands on the battlefield and in the graveyard, and returns to hand from either. A recurring Voltron threat.\nSasaya, Orochi Ascendant ↗\nFlips once you hold seven lands and then makes each land tap for its whole type. Absurd mana from a mono-green shell.\nOb Nixilis, the Fallen ↗\nLandfall that drains three from an opponent and grows him by three. A mono-black clock built on land drops.\nKodama of the East Tree ↗\nEvery permanent entering lets you put another from hand onto the battlefield, chaining off every land drop.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nThis is a slow archetype whose best turns come late, and it can spend the first six turns doing nothing that looks like a threat. That is usually fine (nobody attacks the lands player) but it means the deck needs a real payoff and enough draw to find it. Watch out too for combo lines the archetype falls into by accident: several lands commanders assemble loops that end games abruptly, which may be more than your pod signed up for.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a lands matter deck in Commander?\nA lands-matter deck builds around land drops as its engine, using landfall triggers that pay out when a land enters, extra land drops to trigger them repeatedly, and recursion that returns lands from the graveyard so the same land can trigger again.\nWhat is landfall in Magic?\nLandfall is a triggered ability that fires whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control. It triggers on any land entering, not just your land drop for the turn, which is why fetchlands and land-recursion effects are so strong alongside it.\nIs lands matter the same as ramp?\nThey overlap heavily but are not identical. Ramp wants mana in order to cast expensive spells; lands matter wants land drops as triggers, and the extra mana is a side effect. Most lands decks are also ramp decks, but a ramp deck full of mana rocks is not a lands deck.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Lands matter? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Lands-Matter Commanders →Ramp →Graveyard recursion →The Land & Mana Base Guide →What Makes a Curve and Why It Matters →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Aggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,lifegain,page_text,12198,"Lifegain Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Lands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nAristocrats Mistakes\nAristocrats Budget Upgrades\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nLifegain\nDeck Archetype\nLifegain: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nGaining life does not win games. Lifegain decks are built on the cards that change that: the ones where every point gained is also a point drained, a card drawn, or a counter placed.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nLifegain: Built around gaining life and the payoffs that turn it into a win condition.\nWhat Lifegain Decks Actually Do\nA lifegain deck gains life in volume and runs payoffs that convert it into something else. On its own a high life total only postpones losing, so the archetype is really about the second half: drain effects that mirror your gain onto opponents, draw engines that pay out per life gained, and creatures that grow with the total.\nIn Commander the archetype benefits from starting at forty, which makes life a spendable resource rather than a cushion. Cards that cost life become close to free, and the deck can afford to pay for effects that a twenty-life format could not support. The strongest builds pair a lifegain engine with a drain payoff so that the same trigger stabilises you and kills three opponents at once.\nThe Lifegain engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Gain life Small amounts, often, from cards that were doing something else.\n2 The payoff triggers Archangel of Thune grows the whole board every time you do.\n3 It becomes damage Vito turns the life you gained into life they lose.\n↻ Life stops being a buffer and starts being the win condition.\nWhich Colors Fit Lifegain\nWhite and black are the archetype's colors, and their split is clean: white gains life, black converts it into damage. Orzhov covers the great majority of decks. Green offers large single lifegain effects and creatures that grow with the total, and red is essentially absent. It is the color least interested in gaining life at all.\nWhite The gain half. Repeatable small triggers, mass lifegain, and the creatures that reward a high total.\nBlack The payoff half. Drain effects, the life-as-a-resource cards, and the combos that convert lifegain into a win.\nGreen Large one-shot lifegain and creatures that grow with the amount gained, usually in a counters-adjacent shell.\nBlue A minor partner, contributing draw engines that trigger on lifegain rather than any gain of its own.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Lifegain Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding lifegain for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nExquisite Blood ↗\nGain life whenever an opponent loses it. Half of the format's best-known two-card infinite combo.\nSanguine Bond ↗\nThe other half: opponents lose life whenever you gain it. Together they loop until the table is at zero.\nVito, Thorn of the Dusk Rose ↗\nA cheaper Sanguine Bond on a creature, which makes the combo far easier to assemble and tutor for.\nAetherflux Reservoir ↗\nGains escalating life per spell and pays fifty life to deal fifty damage. A win condition that needs no combat.\nArchangel of Thune ↗\nPuts a counter on every creature you control whenever you gain life, converting incidental gain into a lethal board.\nWell of Lost Dreams ↗\nDraw cards for life gained, up to the amount you paid. Turns the archetype's dead resource into cards.\nSerra Ascendant ↗\nA one-drop 6/6 flying lifelink at thirty or more life, which in a forty-life format is almost immediately.\nSoul Warden ↗\nA life for every creature entering, from anyone. Cheap, relentless, and the trigger most payoffs are counting.\nRhox Faithmender ↗\nDoubles all life you gain, which doubles every drain, draw, and counter your payoffs produce.\nFelidar Sovereign ↗\nWins the game outright at forty or more life on your upkeep. The most direct payoff the archetype has.\nTop 10 Lifegain Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nOloro, Ageless Ascetic ↗\nGains two life every upkeep even from the command zone, which makes him the format's most passive engine.\nKarlov of the Ghost Council ↗\nTwo counters per lifegain trigger and exiles creatures by spending them. Grows absurdly fast.\nAyli, Eternal Pilgrim ↗\nSacrifices creatures for life, then exiles any permanent once you are ten life ahead. Lifegain as removal.\nLicia, Sanguine Tribune ↗\nCosts less the more life you have gained and converts a chunk of your total into permanent power.\nWillowdusk, Essence Seer ↗\nMoves life gained onto a creature as counters, or turns it into damage. The green-black bridge to counters.\nTrelasarra, Moon Dancer ↗\nScry and a counter on every lifegain trigger, on a two-drop. Cheap, fast, and consistent.\nLathiel, the Bounteous Dawn ↗\nDistributes counters equal to the life gained that turn across your creatures at end step.\nLiesa, Shroud of Dusk ↗\nDrains every opponent whenever a creature dies and taxes their spells with life. Two payoffs on one body.\nObzedat, Ghost Council ↗\nDrains two every turn and exiles himself to dodge removal, returning to drain again.\nHeliod, Sun-Crowned ↗\nCounters on lifegain and grants lifelink, which makes him both an engine and half of a famous combo.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nA high life total is not a win, and it can be an active liability: being at eighty makes you look untouchable, and tables respond by attacking the person they cannot ignore. Make sure the deck converts life into damage, cards, or counters rather than just accumulating it. Watch out too for how quickly the Exquisite Blood combo ends a game; if your pod is not expecting an infinite, say so before you sit down.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nHow do lifegain decks win in Commander?\nThrough payoffs, not through the life total itself. Drain effects mirror your lifegain onto opponents, cards like Well of Lost Dreams convert it into draws, Archangel of Thune converts it into counters, and Felidar Sovereign or Aetherflux Reservoir win outright at a high enough total.\nWhat colors are lifegain decks in MTG?\nWhite and black. White supplies the lifegain triggers and mass gain, black converts life into damage through drain effects and combos. Orzhov is the standard pairing, with green appearing in counters-focused builds.\nIs gaining life good in Commander?\nOnly when it does something else at the same time. Starting at forty with three opponents means raw life gain rarely changes who wins. It just delays things. Lifegain is strong when each point also drains an opponent, draws a card, or grows a creature.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Lifegain? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Lifegain Commanders →Aristocrats →Counters →Combo →Commander Rules: The Complete Guide →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Lands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nVoltron Mistakes\nVoltron Budget Upgrades\nAristocrats Mistakes\nAristocrats Budget Upgrades\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,mill,page_text,12082,"Mill Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nMill\nDeck Archetype\nMill: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nA mill deck ignores life totals entirely. It attacks the one resource nobody plays around, the library, and wins when an opponent has to draw from an empty one.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nMill: Empties libraries instead of attacking, winning when opponents cannot draw.\nWhat Mill Decks Actually Do\nMill wins by emptying libraries. A player who is required to draw a card from an empty library loses the game the next time state-based actions are checked, so the deck's whole job is to get three opponents' ninety-nine-card decks to zero before they get your life total there.\nCommander makes that mathematically hard on purpose. Three opponents means roughly three hundred cards to remove, not sixty, and every point of mill you aim at one player does nothing to the other two. Successful mill decks answer that in one of two ways: mass mill that hits all opponents at once, or a secondary payoff that turns everyone's full graveyards into a real board presence.\nThe Mill engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Mill a chunk Glimpse the Unthinkable takes ten cards for two mana.\n2 The engine triggers Hedron Crab mills again every time a land hits the battlefield.\n3 The library shrinks Nobody has lost yet. They just have fewer cards left.\n↻ The loss happens on an empty draw, not when the library empties.\nWhich Colors Fit Mill\nThis is the narrowest color footprint of any archetype here. Blue is where mill lives as a mechanic, black is where the payoffs and the removal live, and Dimir covers the overwhelming majority of decks. Green mills, but almost always itself. Red and white barely participate at all.\nBlue The color of mill itself. Nearly every mass-mill spell, mill-per-draw enchantment, and library-halving effect is blue.\nBlack Payoffs and protection. Black turns opponents' graveyards into your creatures, and supplies the removal that buys the turns mill needs.\nGreen Almost exclusively self-mill for a recursion plan. Green does not attack other people's libraries in any meaningful way.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Mill Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding mill for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nGlimpse the Unthinkable ↗\nTen cards for two mana at sorcery speed. Still the most efficient single-target mill spell in the format.\nMaddening Cacophony ↗\nEight to one player, or with kicker a quarter of every opponent's library at once. Built for multiplayer in a way most mill cards are not.\nFractured Sanity ↗\nFourteen cards for three mana, with cycling so it is never a dead draw. The efficiency ceiling for single-target mill.\nTraumatize ↗\nHalves a library in one card. Enormous on turn five, notably worse later. Mill's timing is the opposite of most decks'.\nHedron Crab ↗\nThree cards off every land drop, including on other players' turns with a fetchland. A one-drop that accumulates absurd totals.\nRuin Crab ↗\nThe functional reprint, and the reason a mill deck can run eight crabs. Redundancy matters more here than raw power.\nPsychic Corrosion ↗\nMills every opponent whenever you draw, which turns a normal blue draw engine into a table-wide clock.\nSphinx's Tutelage ↗\nThe same effect aimed at one opponent, with a repeat clause on matching colors that makes it wildly variable and occasionally lethal.\nAltar of Dementia ↗\nA free sacrifice outlet that mills for the sacrificed creature's power. The bridge between mill and any token or aristocrats shell.\nMesmeric Orb ↗\nMills everyone for every untap, including yours. Symmetrical, but you are the only player at the table who wants it.\nTop 10 Mill Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nBruvac the Grandiloquent ↗\nDoubles all mill you deal to opponents. Turns Traumatize into a full library and is the single biggest payoff in the archetype.\nPhenax, God of Deception ↗\nTap any creature to mill for its toughness. Rewards big, defensive bodies that are good at surviving anyway.\nSzadek, Lord of Secrets ↗\nDoubles his own mill total on each successful hit. A commander-damage clock and a mill clock in the same card.\nMirko Vosk, Mind Drinker ↗\nMills to four lands on connect, which in a typical Commander deck means far more than four cards.\nAnowon, the Ruin Thief ↗\nRogues mill on damage and grow as opponents' graveyards fill. The tribal-mill hybrid, and the most consistent build in the archetype.\nCircu, Dimir Lobotomist ↗\nExiles what it mills, which pre-empts every graveyard-recursion deck the mill plan would otherwise be feeding.\nUmbris, Fear Manifest ↗\nExiles cards from libraries and grows enormous doing it, converting the mill plan into commander damage as a backup.\nLord Xander, the Collector ↗\nDiscard, mill, and sacrifice triggers stacked on one body. Slow but attacks every resource at once.\nOona, Queen of the Fae ↗\nExiles from a library and makes a flier for each card of the chosen color, a mill effect that builds a board.\nTasha, the Witch Queen ↗\nLets you cast what you mill from opponents, turning three libraries into your own card pool.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nMill is the archetype most likely to make a table turn on you, because milling everyone reads as attacking everyone. It also actively helps graveyard decks: filling a Muldrotha player's yard is doing their work for them. Talk about it in your Rule 0 conversation, and know which pods it is a good fit for before you sit down.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nDoes milling a player make them lose in Commander?\nNot immediately. A player with an empty library only loses when they are required to draw a card and cannot, normally at the start of their next draw step. Emptying the library does nothing on its own; the loss happens on the attempted draw.\nIs mill viable in Commander?\nIt is viable but demanding. Three opponents means roughly three times the cards to remove, so decks that succeed either mill all opponents at once with mass effects like Maddening Cacophony, or pair a partial mill plan with a payoff that uses the filled graveyards to win a different way.\nWhat colors is mill in MTG?\nBlue and black. Blue owns mill as a mechanic and supplies nearly every mass-mill effect; black supplies the payoffs and removal. Dimir is the standard pairing, and mono-blue is viable thanks to Bruvac the Grandiloquent.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Mill? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nGraveyard recursion →Control →What Is Rule 0? →Commander Rules: The Complete Guide →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,ramp,page_text,12302,"Ramp Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Stax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nRamp\nDeck Archetype\nRamp: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nEvery Commander deck plays ramp. A ramp deck is the one that treats the mana advantage itself as the plan, rather than as a way to cast its real plan on time.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nRamp: Accelerates into a mana advantage and casts bigger things sooner.\nWhat Ramp Decks Actually Do\nRamp decks accelerate their mana well past the one-land-per-turn baseline and then convert that lead into something the rest of the table cannot match: an enormous creature ten turns early, an X spell for twenty, or simply casting two threats a turn while everyone else casts one.\nThe distinction worth drawing is between ramp as a component and ramp as an archetype. Almost every deck runs ten or so acceleration pieces; that is deckbuilding, not a strategy. A ramp deck runs closer to twenty, plus payoffs that specifically reward having far more mana than the format expects. The risk profile is distinctive too. The deck is at its most vulnerable in the middle turns, when it has spent several cards on mana and has nothing on board to show for it.\nThe Ramp engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Land, then a rock Sol Ring on turn one is two extra mana every turn afterwards.\n2 More mana than the turn Nature's Lore puts a land onto the battlefield untapped.\n3 Cast ahead of schedule A six-drop on turn four is a different game from a six-drop on six.\n↻ The spells you cast early are the ones that find more mana.\nWhich Colors Fit Ramp\nGreen is the ramp color and it is not close. It has land-fetching sorceries, mana creatures, extra land drops, and the doubling effects, at rates no other color approaches. Colorless mana rocks are available to everyone, which is why blue and black ramp decks exist at all. Red ramps in bursts through rituals and Treasure, and white, historically the worst ramp color, now has catch-up effects that only work when you are already behind.\nGreen Land fetching, mana creatures, extra land drops, and mana doubling. The archetype's home by a very wide margin.\nRed Rituals and Treasure: burst mana for one big turn rather than a permanent advantage.\nBlue Artifact-based acceleration and untap effects, plus the card draw to use all that mana.\nBlack Mana from life payments and the Cabal Coffers style of Swamp-count acceleration.\nWhite Catch-up ramp that only triggers when behind, plus taxation-adjacent effects like Smothering Tithe.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Ramp Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding ramp for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nSol Ring ↗\nOne mana for a rock that taps for two. The most-played card in the format, and the single best ramp card in it.\nArcane Signet ↗\nTwo mana, taps for any color in your identity. The most efficient fixing-plus-ramp rock in a multicolor deck.\nNature's Lore ↗\nTwo mana for an untapped dual land from the library. Faster than Rampant Growth and fixes better.\nThree Visits ↗\nThe functional reprint of the above, which is why green ramp decks can run both effects.\nCultivate ↗\nSlower, but it puts one land on the battlefield and one in hand, so it fixes and guarantees the next land drop.\nKodama's Reach ↗\nThe same card again under a different name. Redundancy is the point in a deck that wants this effect every game.\nBurgeoning ↗\nAn extra land drop for every opponent's first land. On turn one this is the most explosive ramp card in the format.\nSmothering Tithe ↗\nA Treasure whenever an opponent draws, unless they pay. In a four-player game this generates absurd quantities of mana.\nCabal Coffers ↗\nMana per Swamp, which in mono-black turns a normal land count into double-digit mana by turn six.\nNyxbloom Ancient ↗\nTriples mana from every source you control. The largest single multiplier available, and a win condition attached to a 5/5 trample.\nTop 10 Ramp Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nAzusa, Lost but Seeking ↗\nTwo extra land drops every turn. Mono-green, cheap, and the purest expression of the archetype.\nOmnath, Locus of Mana ↗\nGreen mana does not empty from your pool and he grows with it. Turns a big turn into a big creature.\nMarwyn, the Nurturer ↗\nAn Elf that grows with every Elf and taps for her power. Ramp and tribal in one command zone.\nKarametra, God of Harvests ↗\nA land from the library for every creature you cast, so the deck ramps just by playing normally.\nZaxara, the Exemplary ↗\nA Hydra token for every X spell, plus infinite-mana-friendly abilities. The classic big-X payoff commander.\nNikya of the Old Ways ↗\nDouble mana from lands at the cost of casting no noncreature spells. A genuine deckbuilding constraint with a huge payoff.\nGoreclaw, Terror of Qal Sisma ↗\nCost reduction and a pump-and-trample anthem for big creatures. Ramp that also finishes.\nGrand Warlord Radha ↗\nMana for every attacking creature that carries into your second main phase. Aggro and ramp on the same trigger.\nJodah, Archmage Eternal ↗\nCasts anything for five mana of any colors, which turns ramp into an excuse to play the biggest cards in Magic.\nKruphix, God of Horizons ↗\nUnused mana carries over as colorless and your hand size is unlimited. Stores a ramp advantage across turns.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nRamp's danger window is turns four through six: you have spent cards on mana, you have nothing on board, and everyone else has creatures. Decks that lose with ramp usually lose there, not to a lack of mana. Keep the curve honest, run some interaction, and make sure the payoffs arrive before the window closes. A deck that ramps to twelve mana and then casts a seven-drop that dies to removal has not accomplished anything.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is ramp in MTG Commander?\nRamp is any effect that increases your available mana faster than one land per turn: mana rocks, mana creatures, land-fetching spells, and extra land drops. As an archetype, it means a deck that builds a large mana advantage and plays cards specifically designed to exploit it.\nHow much ramp should a Commander deck run?\nA typical deck runs about ten pieces of acceleration alongside its thirty-six or so lands. A dedicated ramp deck runs closer to fifteen or twenty, plus payoffs that reward the surplus. Running lots of ramp without payoffs just means drawing mana instead of spells.\nWhat is the best color for ramp?\nGreen, comfortably. It has the deepest and most efficient acceleration of every kind: land fetching, mana creatures, extra land drops, and mana doubling. Colorless mana rocks let every other color ramp, and red does it in bursts with rituals and Treasure.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Ramp? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nLands matter →Combo →Mana Rocks: The Complete Guide →The Land & Mana Base Guide →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Stax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,spellslinger,page_text,12823,"Spellslinger Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nDeck Archetype\nSpellslinger: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nA Spellslinger deck treats instants and sorceries as its permanents. The creatures are payoffs that convert a spell into damage, a token, or a card. The spells are the deck.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nSpellslinger: Wins on a pile of instants and sorceries rather than a board of creatures.\nWhat Spellslinger Decks Actually Do\nSpellslinger builds win on volume of instants and sorceries rather than a board of creatures. Where most decks cast a spell to affect the battlefield, a Spellslinger deck casts a spell to trigger something: a magecraft creature that draws a card, a token maker that pays out per spell, or a damage engine that pings the table every time you hold priority.\nThe practical shape is a low curve, a lot of cheap cantrips and rituals, and a handful of creatures and enchantments whose text starts with 'whenever you cast an instant or sorcery spell.' A turn where you cast four one-mana spells is a better turn than one where you cast a single four-drop, which inverts a lot of normal deckbuilding instincts.\nThe Spellslinger engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Cast something cheap A one-mana cantrip is a full turn's work in this deck.\n2 The trigger pays Archmage Emeritus draws a card; Storm-Kiln Artist makes a Treasure.\n3 Spend what it paid That card or that Treasure funds the next spell in the chain.\n↻ Four one-mana spells is a better turn than one four-drop.\nWhich Colors Fit Spellslinger\nSpellslinger is the most color-concentrated archetype on this list: it is blue-red first and everything else second. Blue supplies the cheap card selection and counterspells that keep the chain going, red supplies the rituals and the damage that turns a chain into a kill. White and black play supporting roles when a deck wants recursion or a wider removal suite, but a Spellslinger deck without blue or red is unusual.\nBlue Cantrips, card selection, counterspells, and the copy effects that turn one spell into three. The reason nearly every Spellslinger deck starts here.\nRed Rituals that let a turn go longer than your lands should allow, plus the direct damage and treasure production that convert spell count into a win.\nWhite A minor partner. Jeskai builds use it for protection and for prowess-adjacent bodies, rarely as a core engine.\nBlack Tutors and graveyard recastability. Grixis Spellslinger leans on black to buy back the spells it has already used.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Spellslinger Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding spellslinger for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nArchmage Emeritus ↗\nMagecraft draw. Every instant and sorcery replaces itself, which is the single cleanest way to keep a chain from running out of gas.\nStorm-Kiln Artist ↗\nA Treasure per spell, so the deck's own spell count pays for the rest of the turn. The most common enabler of a genuinely explosive turn.\nYoung Pyromancer ↗\nA 1/1 per spell for two mana. Converts a deck full of cheap cantrips into an actual board without changing the curve.\nThousand-Year Storm ↗\nEach spell copies itself once per spell already cast this turn. Expensive, but it turns the third spell of a turn into a game-ending one.\nJeska's Will ↗\nMana and cards in one card, and with your commander out it delivers both. The premier red ritual in the archetype.\nUnderworld Breach ↗\nEscape lets you recast the instants and sorceries already in your graveyard, which is where a Spellslinger deck keeps most of its deck by turn six.\nPast in Flames ↗\nFlashback on your whole graveyard for one turn. The classic way to make a second pass through everything you already cast.\nMurmuring Mystic ↗\nA 1/3 that makes a flying blocker per instant or sorcery. Solves the archetype's real weakness: having no bodies while durdling.\nRal, Storm Conduit ↗\nPings on every spell and copies your instants and sorceries. Doubles as a combo piece with any spell that copies itself.\nMizzix's Mastery ↗\nRecast the biggest sorcery in your graveyard; overloaded, recast all of them. A one-card turn that ends games out of nowhere.\nTop 10 Spellslinger Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nVeyran, Voice of Duality ↗\nDoubles your own magecraft and cast triggers, so every payoff on the board pays twice.\nZaffai, Thunder Conductor ↗\nScales with mana value rather than spell count, rewarding a deck that casts a few big spells instead of many small ones.\nMizzix of the Izmagnus ↗\nExperience counters make your instants and sorceries progressively cheaper, and the original cost-reduction commander.\nNiv-Mizzet, Parun ↗\nDraw a card, deal a damage; deal damage, draw a card. A commander that is also the deck's win condition.\nKess, Dissident Mage ↗\nOne free flashback per turn from the graveyard, which is why Grixis Spellslinger exists as its own lane.\nTalrand, Sky Summoner ↗\nA 2/2 flier per instant or sorcery, mono-blue. The budget entry point to the archetype and still a real clock.\nKalamax, the Stormsire ↗\nCopies the first instant you cast each turn while tapped, pushing the deck toward instant speed and combat tricks.\nBalmor, Battlemage Captain ↗\nPumps and grants flying to the team per spell, turning a token board into lethal in one cantrip-heavy turn.\nLier, Disciple of the Drowned ↗\nGives everything in your graveyard flashback and turns off counterspells, which is both an engine and a shield.\nElsha of the Infinite ↗\nPlay off the top of your library at instant speed. Rewards a deck built almost entirely of cheap noncreature spells.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nSpellslinger's failure mode is casting a lot of spells that do not add up to anything. A deck of cantrips with no payoff on the battlefield is a deck that drew cards and passed the turn. Keep enough payoff permanents that any given turn has something to trigger, and enough interaction that you are not simply durdling while three other players develop.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a Spellslinger deck in MTG Commander?\nA Spellslinger deck is one built around casting a high volume of instants and sorceries, with creatures and enchantments that trigger whenever you do. Instead of winning through a board of creatures, it wins by converting spell count into damage, tokens, or cards, often several spells in a single turn.\nWhat colors are best for Spellslinger?\nBlue and red. Blue provides cheap card selection, counterspells, and copy effects; red provides rituals and direct damage. Izzet (blue-red) is the default, extended to Grixis with black for tutors and graveyard recursion, or Jeskai with white for protection.\nIs Spellslinger the same as Storm?\nNo, though they overlap. Storm is a specific plan of casting many spells in one turn to fuel the storm mechanic or a per-spell payoff into a kill. Spellslinger is the broader archetype: plenty of Spellslinger decks accumulate value across many turns rather than trying to combo off in one.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Spellslinger? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Spellslinger Commanders →Common Spellslinger Deck Mistakes →Best Budget Spellslinger Upgrades →Combo →Control →How the Stack Works →What Makes a Curve and Why It Matters →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     All 20 Archetypes\nSpellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,stax,page_text,12585,"Stax Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Enchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nStax\nDeck Archetype\nStax: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nStax does not try to go faster than the table. It slows the table down to its own speed, then wins from a position where nobody else can act.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nStax: Taxes or locks down what every player at the table is allowed to do.\nWhat Stax Decks Actually Do\nStax decks attack resources rather than life totals. They tax spells, deny untaps, force sacrifices, cap how many spells can be cast in a turn, and generally make the game's normal actions expensive or impossible. The name comes from a deck built around Smokestack, and the pattern has been the same ever since: shrink everyone's options, including your own, and make sure you are better positioned inside those constraints.\nThe crucial detail is asymmetry. A symmetrical lock helps nobody, so a stax deck builds a way to sidestep its own prison: untapping outside the normal step, generating mana from sources the lock does not touch, or simply running few enough permanents that a sacrifice effect costs it less than everyone else. Winning is often an afterthought: once the lock is complete, a single small creature can close out the game over several turns.\nThe Stax engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Tax the table Sphere of Resistance makes every spell in the game cost more.\n2 They stay tapped out Winter Orb leaves everybody untapping one land a turn.\n3 You built for it Your deck is assembled to work under the lock; theirs is not.\n↻ Every turn under the lock is a turn they fall further behind.\nWhich Colors Fit Stax\nWhite is the strongest stax color in Commander. It owns taxation effects, the best sweepers, and the creatures that make everyone else's turn cost more. Blue adds the counterspells and untap denial that make a lock airtight, and black adds edicts and discard. Red contributes land destruction and Blood Moon effects, and green participates least, mostly through mana denial rather than taxation.\nWhite Taxation, pillowfort defence, and sweepers. The color with the deepest and most efficient lock pieces.\nBlue Counterspells and untap denial, which turn a partial lock into a complete one.\nBlack Edicts, discard, and the effects that punish opponents for the resources they still have.\nRed Land and artifact destruction, plus Blood Moon effects that punish greedy multicolor manabases.\nGreen The least represented. Green denies mana rather than actions, and mostly appears for the ramp that powers a lock through.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Stax Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding stax for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nWinter Orb ↗\nOnly one land untaps per untap step. Two mana that halves everyone's development, and the archetype's most recognisable card.\nSmokestack ↗\nEscalating forced sacrifices every turn. The card the archetype is named after, and still its most complete lock piece.\nTangle Wire ↗\nForces everyone to tap down for several turns while it fades. Bought turns, in a card, and it expires before it hurts you.\nSphere of Resistance ↗\nEvery spell costs one more. Symmetrical, but far worse for a four-color value deck than for a lean stax list.\nThalia, Guardian of Thraben ↗\nThe same tax on noncreature spells only, on a first-strike body that can pressure a player while it taxes.\nBlood Moon ↗\nNonbasic lands become Mountains. In a format built on nonbasics, this ends games that have not started yet.\nDrannith Magistrate ↗\nOpponents cannot cast from anywhere except hand: no commanders, no flashback, no cascade. A two-drop that blanks entire decks.\nRule of Law ↗\nOne spell per player per turn. Shuts down combo and storm outright and makes value decks crawl.\nCursed Totem ↗\nTurns off every creature's activated abilities, which is a one-card answer to mana dorks, sacrifice outlets, and most creature combos.\nEnsnaring Bridge ↗\nCreatures with power greater than your hand size cannot attack. A stax deck empties its hand anyway, so this is close to one-sided.\nTop 10 Stax Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nGrand Arbiter Augustin IV ↗\nYour spells cost less and everyone else's cost more. Taxation from the command zone, on turn four, every game.\nDerevi, Empyrial Tactician ↗\nRepeatedly taps and untaps permanents and returns from the command zone cheaply, which makes her nearly impossible to remove permanently.\nLavinia, Azorius Renegade ↗\nCounters any spell cast with more mana value than the caster's lands. Devastating against ritual-fuelled and free-spell decks.\nTergrid, God of Fright ↗\nEverything opponents sacrifice or discard comes to you instead. Turns denial into your own board.\nBraids, Cabal Minion ↗\nForces a sacrifice from every opponent every turn. Old, brutally simple, and banned in some circles for good reason.\nHokori, Dust Drinker ↗\nA Winter Orb on a body, in the command zone, available every game.\nSen Triplets ↗\nLocks a player out of casting and lets you play their hand. Denial and theft on the same trigger.\nElesh Norn, Mother of Machines ↗\nDoubles your enter-the-battlefield triggers and turns off everyone else's. A lock piece that also builds your engine.\nQueen Marchesa ↗\nThe monarch as a political lock: she creates a resource the table has to attack you for, while you defend it.\nVorinclex, Voice of Hunger ↗\nYour lands make double mana and opponents' lands enter tapped. Green's version of resource denial, and one of the most punishing.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nThis is the archetype most likely to cause a genuinely bad evening. Stax makes other people's games less fun in a direct, visible way, and in a casual pod it often produces long turns where three players do nothing. That does not make it wrong to play. It makes it something to raise before the game starts. Check the bracket your pod is at, say what your deck does in the Rule 0 conversation, and be ready to sleeve up something else.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a stax deck in Commander?\nA stax deck attacks resources rather than life totals, using taxation effects, forced sacrifices, untap denial, and spell restrictions to limit what everyone can do. It builds a position where its own limited resources still function while opponents' do not, then wins slowly from inside that lock.\nIs stax bad for casual Commander?\nIt is a poor fit for most casual pods without a conversation first. Stax deliberately reduces what other players can do, which many groups find unfun even when it is played well. It belongs in higher brackets or in pods that have explicitly agreed to it, which is exactly what the Rule 0 conversation is for.\nWhat colors are stax decks?\nWhite and blue primarily. White has the deepest taxation and sweeper suite, blue supplies counterspells and untap denial. Black adds edicts and discard, red adds land destruction and Blood Moon effects, and green is the least represented.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Stax? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nWhat Is Stax in MTG? →Control →Group hug →The Commander Bracket System, Explained →What Is Rule 0? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Enchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nAggro\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,superfriends,page_text,12489,"Superfriends Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Ramp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nSuperfriends\nDeck Archetype\nSuperfriends: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nPlaneswalkers are card advantage that opponents have to attack to stop. Superfriends runs so many that the table cannot attack them all.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nSuperfriends: Built around a critical mass of planeswalkers.\nWhat Superfriends Decks Actually Do\nA superfriends deck plays a critical mass of planeswalkers and the support that keeps them alive: proliferate effects to add loyalty, effects that let them activate twice, and defensive pieces that stop opponents attacking them. Each planeswalker generates value every turn it survives, so the deck wins by accumulating far more resources than three opponents combined.\nThe archetype has a distinctive problem and a distinctive solution. The problem is that planeswalkers are terrible in multiplayer by default: three opponents can attack them, and loyalty does not scale with the number of people trying to remove it. The solution is defence: pillowfort enchantments, deterrents, and board wipes that leave your walkers untouched. A superfriends deck is really a defensive deck that happens to win with planeswalker ultimates.\nThe Superfriends engine\nThree steps that feed each other, which is what makes it a deck rather than a pile of cards sharing a theme.\n1 Land a planeswalker Deepglow Skate doubles the loyalty it arrives with.\n2 Activate, then protect A walker that survives a turn cycle has paid for itself.\n3 Activate again The Chain Veil buys a second activation in the same turn.\n↻ Loyalty is a resource nobody else at the table is attacking yet.\nWhich Colors Fit Superfriends\nWhite, blue, and black have the deepest planeswalker pools and the best protective effects, which is why Esper and its extensions dominate. Green contributes proliferate and the doubling effects that make ultimates arrive immediately, and red has strong individual walkers but little of the defensive infrastructure the deck needs. Four- and five-color builds are common precisely because the deck wants the best walkers regardless of color.\nWhite Pillowfort defence, board wipes that spare planeswalkers, and several of the strongest walkers in the format.\nBlue Counterspells, card selection, and untap effects that turn one activation per turn into two.\nBlack Removal to clear the attackers, plus tutors to find the right walker for the board.\nGreen Proliferate and counter-doubling, which shortens the road to an ultimate from four turns to one.\nRed Strong individual planeswalkers but little support. Usually a splash rather than a core color.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Superfriends Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding superfriends for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nDoubling Season ↗\nPlaneswalkers enter with double loyalty, which means most of them can ultimate the turn they land.\nThe Chain Veil ↗\nActivate each planeswalker one additional time per turn. The archetype's signature engine, and its most abusable card.\nOath of Teferi ↗\nThe same extra activation on an enchantment, plus a blink trigger on entry. Cheaper and harder to remove.\nDeepglow Skate ↗\nDoubles the counters on any permanents you choose, usually every planeswalker at once, straight into multiple ultimates.\nVorinclex, Monstrous Raider ↗\nDoubles counters you place and halves everyone else's. A one-sided Doubling Season on a hasty body.\nUgin, the Spirit Dragon ↗\nA colorless sweeper that leaves your board intact, since almost everything in a superfriends deck is colorless or a planeswalker.\nElspeth, Sun's Champion ↗\nMakes three blockers a turn and wipes big creatures. Defends the whole team, which is the job that matters.\nInterplanar Beacon ↗\nA land that fixes for planeswalkers, gains life when you cast one, and costs nothing to include.\nKarn Liberated ↗\nColorless, high loyalty, exiles any permanent, and fits into any deck in the archetype regardless of colors.\nNissa, Who Shakes the World ↗\nDoubles green mana from Forests and makes 3/3 lands that defend the rest of the team.\nTop 10 Superfriends Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nAtraxa, Praetors' Voice ↗\nProliferate every end step, in the four colors with the best walkers. The definitive superfriends commander.\nCarth the Lion ↗\nPlaneswalkers enter with an extra counter and cost less to find. Quiet, cheap, and relentlessly consistent.\nSisay, Weatherlight Captain ↗\nTutors any legendary permanent, including planeswalkers, and grows with each one on the battlefield.\nDihada, Binder of Wills ↗\nA planeswalker commander that draws, gains life, and protects legends. Mardu superfriends in one card.\nCommodore Guff ↗\nAdds loyalty to a walker each turn and returns them from the graveyard. Built for this exact deck.\nFreyalise, Llanowar's Fury ↗\nA planeswalker commander that makes Elves, so the team defends itself while the loyalty climbs.\nNahiri, the Lithomancer ↗\nMono-white equipment-and-walkers, with a soldier token that keeps attackers busy.\nJared Carthalion ↗\nGrows with the monarch and can be a planeswalker magnet in a Naya shell that lacks blue's defensive tools.\nRamos, Dragon Engine ↗\nStores counters from multicolor spells and dumps them into mana for a five-color walker suite.\nSaheeli, the Gifted ↗\nA planeswalker commander whose Thopters defend the board while her ultimate copies artifacts for a win.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nA planeswalker with four loyalty in a four-player game is a planeswalker with one loyalty. The archetype fails when it plays walkers without defence: every one becomes a free attack target that dies before it does anything. Build the fort first: deterrent enchantments, blockers, and sweepers, then the walkers. Superfriends is also mana-hungry and slow, so expect to be the archdeck target once two ultimates have resolved.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a superfriends deck in Commander?\nA superfriends deck is built around playing many planeswalkers, supported by proliferate effects, extra-activation cards, and defensive pieces that keep them alive. Each surviving planeswalker generates value every turn, so the deck accumulates advantage the table cannot match.\nCan a planeswalker be your commander?\nOnly if the card says it can be your commander, or if it is a legendary creature that becomes a planeswalker. Several planeswalkers printed for Commander products carry that line explicitly: Freyalise, Daretti, and Teferi, Temporal Archmage among them.\nAre planeswalkers good in multiplayer Commander?\nIndividually they are weaker than in a duel, because three opponents can attack them and loyalty does not scale with the number of attackers. They become strong in a deck that also plays defence (pillowfort enchantments, blockers, and sweepers) which is exactly what superfriends is built to do.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Superfriends? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nMTG Planeswalkers, by Story Arc →What Is a Planeswalker? →Counters →Control →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Ramp\nAggro\nGroup hug\nSuperfriends\nLands matter\nCounters\nBlink\nLifegain\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,tribal,page_text,12123,"Tribal Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Aristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nTribal\nDeck Archetype\nTribal: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nTribal is the archetype most people build first, because it starts from a question everyone can answer: which creature type do you actually like?\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nTribal: Rewards playing many creatures that share a creature type.\nWhat Tribal Decks Actually Do\nA tribal deck (increasingly called typal in official Wizards language, though almost nobody says that at a table) is built around creatures sharing a type, plus lords and payoffs that reward the concentration. Elves, Goblins, Zombies, Vampires, Dragons, Slivers, Merfolk, Angels, and dozens more all support real decks.\nThe strength of the archetype is that its payoffs stack multiplicatively. One lord giving plus-one-plus-one is modest; three lords is a board of creatures that are each three points bigger, and it happened without any single card doing anything spectacular. The weakness is the mirror image: the deck is a house of cards against a wrath, and against targeted removal aimed at the lords rather than the bodies.\nThe Tribal engine\nThree steps that build to a finish. This one ends the game rather than coming back round.\n1 Commit to the type Cavern of Souls means the important ones cannot be countered.\n2 The lords pump Vanquisher's Banner adds a card as well as the stats.\n3 Attack as one Every creature is bigger because the last one arrived.\nThe last step wins the game, so there is nothing to come back round for.\nWhich Colors Fit Tribal\nUniquely on this list, tribal has no home colors: the creature type picks them. Elves are green, Goblins red, Merfolk blue, Zombies blue-black, Vampires black, Angels white, Dinosaurs Naya, Faeries Dimir, and Slivers and Dragons sprawl across all five. Choose the type first and the colors follow automatically, which is why this is the friendliest archetype for a first deck.\nWhite Angels, Humans, Knights, and Soldiers. The color of small creatures made large by anthems.\nBlue Merfolk, Faeries, Wizards, and Pirates: evasive types that win through unblockability rather than size.\nBlack Zombies, Vampires, and Skeletons. The types that get better when they die and come back.\nRed Goblins, Dragons, and Dwarves. Fast, aggressive, and by far the best at making expendable copies of themselves.\nGreen Elves, Beasts, and Dinosaurs. The types that ramp into each other or simply outsize everything.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Tribal Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding tribal for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nCavern of Souls ↗\nA land that makes your chosen type uncounterable. Expensive, but no other card protects a tribal deck's key spells this cleanly.\nPath of Ancestry ↗\nThe budget-friendly version of the same idea, adding a scry whenever it pays for a creature of your type.\nVanquisher's Banner ↗\nAn anthem that also draws a card for every creature of your type you cast. Fixes the archetype's card-advantage problem.\nHerald's Horn ↗\nCost reduction plus a free card off the top each upkeep when it hits your type. Cheap and consistently excellent.\nKindred Discovery ↗\nDraws whenever a creature of your type enters or attacks. The most explosive draw engine available to any tribe.\nCoat of Arms ↗\nEvery creature gets plus-one-plus-one for each other creature sharing a type. Enormous, and symmetrical enough to be dangerous.\nShared Animosity ↗\nThe one-sided version for attackers only, which usually converts a developed board into lethal on the spot.\nMetallic Mimic ↗\nA two-drop that names your type and puts a counter on every one that follows. Works in any color.\nUrza's Incubator ↗\nTwo generic mana off every creature spell of your type. The single biggest cost reduction available to tribal decks.\nPatriarch's Bidding ↗\nReturns every creature of the named types from all graveyards. The archetype's post-wrath reset button.\nTop 10 Tribal Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nEdgar Markov ↗\nVampires. Makes a token from the command zone on every Vampire cast, which is unmatched value for a tribal commander.\nThe Ur-Dragon ↗\nDragons. Cost reduction on Dragons and a free permanent from hand on every Dragon that connects.\nSliver Overlord ↗\nSlivers. Tutors any Sliver repeatedly, which makes the deck the most consistent tribal build in the format.\nLathril, Blade of the Elves ↗\nElves. Makes tokens on damage and converts a wide Elf board into a drain that ignores blockers entirely.\nWilhelt, the Rotcleaver ↗\nZombies. Turns every Zombie death into a Decayed token and a card, which is the whole aristocrats overlap in one commander.\nKumena, Tyrant of Orazca ↗\nMerfolk. Three abilities that convert a Merfolk board into unblockable damage, cards, and counters.\nGishath, Sun's Avatar ↗\nDinosaurs. Puts Dinosaurs directly from library to battlefield on combat damage, and a single hit often ends the game.\nNajeela, the Blade-Blossom ↗\nWarriors. Tokens on attack, and with five colors available she takes infinite combat steps.\nMuxus, Goblin Grandee ↗\nGoblins. Digs six deep and puts every Goblin found onto the battlefield at once.\nKaalia of the Vast ↗\nAngels, Demons, and Dragons. Cheats one onto the battlefield attacking, every combat, from turn four.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nTribal decks lose to board wipes harder than anything except go-wide, and the lords make targeted removal unusually effective too. Build in recursion (Patriarch's Bidding is in the archetype's top ten for a reason) and resist filling the last ten slots with weak creatures purely because they share a type. A bad card is still a bad card when it is on-theme.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a tribal deck in Commander?\nA tribal deck is built around creatures that share a creature type, together with lords and payoffs that reward playing many of them. Wizards now officially calls this typal, but tribal remains the term almost everyone uses at the table.\nWhat is the best tribe in Commander?\nThere is no single answer, but Elves, Goblins, Slivers, Dragons, and Vampires have the deepest card pools and the strongest payoffs. Slivers and Dragons get access to all five colors, while Elves and Goblins win on sheer speed and card count.\nWhat is the difference between tribal and typal?\nNothing mechanically. Wizards changed the official terminology to typal because tribal was already a card type on cards like Bound in Silence. Players almost universally still say tribal, and the two mean the same thing in conversation.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Tribal? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nGo wide →Aggro →Best MTG Commander Decks & Precons →What Is Color Identity in MTG? →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Aristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nTribal\nStax\nCombo\nControl\nRamp\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nEsc\nBrowse by section\nBeginner SeriesPlay Better CommanderCommander GlossaryMTG FormatsGuides & TipsCard SpotlightsDeck ArchetypesCommander GuidesColor CombinationsDraft & LimitedLore & StorySet GuidesSecret LairRoundupsApp ComparisonsBuying & SellingCommunity & MediaFor Game StoresPokémon TCGStar Wars: UnlimitedYu-Gi-Oh!One Piece Card GameRiftboundDisney LorcanaHow to Use TurnZero HQTools & Data\n↑↓ to navigate ↵ to open esc to close\nAsk TurnZero\nAsk TurnZero\nA question in your own words. We’ll point you at the page that answers it.\n×\nAsk   Try “how do I start a Commander deck”, “what is a bracket”, or a card name.\nWant answers about your decks?\nTurnZero Premium answers questions about your own games, decks, gatherings and your\nfriends’ lists — grounded in what you’ve tracked.\nGet TurnZero →\nCard stats Close"
  tz,voltron,page_text,12593,"Voltron Commander Deck Guide: Best Cards & Commanders\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Spellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\nFor Game Stores\nCommunity & Media\nStore   Learn       Guides Rules, strategy and how-tos\nFAQ Quick answers about TurnZero\nAbout Us Why we built TurnZero\nFeatures What the app does\nPokémon Pokémon collection and scanning\nYu-Gi-Oh! Yu-Gi-Oh! collection and scanning\nOne Piece One Piece collection and scanning\nRiftbound Riftbound collection and scanning\nStar Wars: Unlimited SWU collection and scanning\nLorcana Lorcana collection and scanning\nTools       Commander Finder Find a commander to build\nRate My Deck Bracket and review a decklist\nStats       Cards Play rates across the catalog\nCommanders The commander leaderboard\nDecks Published decks and their results\nSign In\nSearch /  Get Started     Home\nGuides\nDeck Archetypes\nVoltron\nDeck Archetype\nVoltron: The Commander Archetype, Explained\nVoltron puts every resource into one creature and swings. It is the only archetype that gets to ignore life totals almost entirely, because twenty-one commander damage is a much shorter road than forty.\nTrack your collections, build decks and remember games in the free TurnZero HQ app.\nApp Store     Google Play\nVoltron: One creature, usually the commander, carries the whole game plan.\nWhat Voltron Decks Actually Do\nA Voltron deck stacks equipment, auras, and counters onto a single creature, usually the commander, until it is large enough to kill in one or two connections. The name comes from the cartoon: individually unremarkable pieces assembling into one enormous thing.\nCommander's twenty-one-damage rule is what makes the plan work. Against a forty-life opponent a 10/10 is a four-turn clock, but as commander damage it is three connections, and against three opponents you only ever need to solve one of them at a time. The deck is really a protection deck wearing an aggro costume: the hard part is not making the creature big, it is keeping it alive through the removal a giant threat invites.\nThe Voltron engine\nThree steps that build to a finish. This one ends the game rather than coming back round.\n1 Protect first Lightning Greaves goes down before anything worth removing does.\n2 Suit up Shadowspear and a sword turn one creature into a real threat.\n3 Twenty-one damage Commander damage removes a player without touching the other two.\nThe last step wins the game, so there is nothing to come back round for.\nWhich Colors Fit Voltron\nWhite is the home color. It owns equipment, auras, and the protection effects that keep the plan from evaporating to a single removal spell. Green adds raw size and trample, red adds haste and the aggressive equipment, and blue adds unblockable and counterspell protection. Black participates least, though it offers some of the best single-target pump in the format.\nWhite Equipment, auras, and protection: hexproof grants, indestructibility, and the effects that answer a board wipe before it resolves.\nGreen Size and trample. Green makes the creature enormous and makes chump blockers stop working.\nRed Haste, double strike, and the most aggressive equipment. Red turns a big creature into a fast one.\nBlue Evasion and counterspells. Unblockable is a legitimate substitute for trample, and holding up a counter protects the investment.\nHow color identity decides what you can play →\nTop 10 Voltron Cards\nThe cards that define the archetype rather than the ones winning this week. If you are\nbuilding voltron for the first time, these are the effects to\nunderstand before anything else.\nLightning Greaves ↗\nZero-cost equip granting haste and shroud. The most-played protection equipment in the format, and often the first thing a Voltron deck finds.\nSwiftfoot Boots ↗\nHexproof instead of shroud, so you can still target your own creature with auras. The two boots do different jobs and most decks run both.\nShadowspear ↗\nTrample and lifelink for one mana, plus the ability to strip hexproof and indestructible from blockers. Small, cheap, and always relevant.\nSword of Feast and Famine ↗\nProtection from two colors, an opponent discards, and your lands untap. The best of the sword cycle for a deck that wants to keep casting after combat.\nColossus Hammer ↗\nPlus ten, minus ten, with an eight-mana equip cost that decks solve with Sigarda's Aid or equipment-cost reduction. A one-card kill when it works.\nWhispersilk Cloak ↗\nUnblockable and shroud in one card. Evasion and protection stapled together is exactly what the archetype is short on.\nRancor ↗\nTwo power, trample, and it comes back to hand when it goes to the graveyard. Auras are card disadvantage; Rancor simply is not.\nEthereal Armor ↗\nScales with your enchantment count and grants first strike, which is why aura-based Voltron builds get better the longer they go.\nBear Umbra ↗\nUntaps your lands on attack and shields the creature from the next removal spell via totem armor. Protection and ramp on one aura.\nKaldra Compleat ↗\nA free 5/5 germ with first strike, trample, haste, and indestructible, and it exiles whatever it damages. A whole Voltron package in one card.\nTop 10 Voltron Commanders\nTen command zones that support the strategy, spread across the color combinations it lives\nin. The color identity shown is what each commander lets you play in the other 99.\nCommander Spotlight\nUril, the Miststalker ↗\nUntargetable by opponents and grows with every aura you attach. The most protected Voltron commander in the format.\nRafiq of the Many ↗\nExalted plus double strike means a single attacker converts every pump effect into twice the damage.\nBruna, Light of Alabaster ↗\nAttaches every aura from your hand and graveyard on attack, rebuilding the whole stack after a removal spell.\nSram, Senior Edificer ↗\nDraws on every equipment, aura, or vehicle. Mono-white, cheap, and solves the archetype's card-advantage problem outright.\nHalvar, God of Battle ↗\nMoves equipment and auras around at no cost, and flips into an equipment that returns the creature it kills.\nLight-Paws, Emperor's Voice ↗\nTutors an aura onto the battlefield with each aura you cast, chaining a whole suite out of a single card.\nSyr Gwyn, Hero of Ashvale ↗\nFree equip costs for Knights plus card draw on damage. A Voltron commander that can spread across a team.\nNazahn, Revered Bladesmith ↗\nTutors an equipment on entry and gives your equipped creatures trample. Consistency plus evasion in the command zone.\nSkullbriar, the Walking Grave ↗\nKeeps its counters when it changes zones, so removal costs you a turn rather than the whole investment.\nGreven, Predator Captain ↗\nDraws cards equal to the life you paid and grows accordingly. The black Voltron shell, powered by its own life total.\nSee which commanders are actually winning games →\nDeck Lab\nBuild the deck, then let it measure itself\nThe Deck Lab checks the list while you are still writing it: lands, ramp, removal, card draw and the curve, each against what the deck is trying to be. Goldfish an opening hand, read the bracket the list actually earns, then track how every version of it does at the table.\nBuild a Deck FreeHow the Deck Lab works →\nWhat to Watch Out For\nEverything is in one card, and Swords to Plowshares costs one mana. Assume your commander will eat removal three or four times a game and build for it: cheap protection, recursion for your equipment, and a realistic sense of how much tax you can pay. Also watch the table read: a growing Voltron threat is visible to everyone, so you will be attacked before you are ready more often than any other archetype.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nWhat is a Voltron deck in Commander?\nA Voltron deck stacks equipment, auras, and counters onto one creature, usually the commander, until it can kill opponents in one or two hits. It aims to win through the twenty-one commander damage rule rather than by reducing anyone from forty life.\nHow much commander damage do you need to win?\nTwenty-one from a single commander. It is tracked per commander and per player, so a 7/7 commander needs three connections against one opponent, and damage from a different commander does not add to the same total.\nWhat colors are best for Voltron?\nWhite first, for equipment, auras, and protection. Green adds size and trample, red adds haste and double strike, and blue adds evasion and counterspells. Boros, Selesnya, and Naya are the most common pairings, with mono-white a strong and cheap option.\nTurnZero HQ tags every deck you track with one of these archetypes, so your win rate is\nbroken down by what your decks are actually trying to do, not just which commander is on\ntop of them.\nAll 20 Commander deck archetypes → See decks people published, tagged and with their win rates →\nCommander Finder\nLike the sound of Voltron? Find a commander that plays it.\nA minute, no account, five commanders and why each one suits you.\nFind your commander   Advertisement   Related Guides\nBest Voltron Commanders →Common Voltron Deck Mistakes →Best Budget Voltron Upgrades →Aggro →Counters →What Is Commander Damage? →How Combat Works →\nSee all 29 in Deck Archetypes →\nTrack how your archetype actually performs.\nLog your games with TurnZero HQ and see which of your decks wins, against what, and how often. Free to start.\nCreate Free Account      Download on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nTurnZero\nHome\nFeatures\nFAQ\nPremium\nStore\nExplore\nDeck Builder\nGuides\nCards\nCommanders\nDecks\nSets\nSecret Lair\nFollow\nTikTok\nInstagram\nYouTube\nAll links\n© 2026 TurnZero HQ  Privacy Policy\nTerms\nAffiliate Disclosure\nDelete Account\nTurnZero HQ is an independent companion for trading card games. It is not affiliated with, endorsed, sponsored, or approved by the publishers or rights holders of the games it supports. Game names, card names, card images, artwork, and related trademarks belong to their respective owners.\nTurnZero HQ is unofficial Fan Content permitted under the Fan Content Policy. Not approved/endorsed by Wizards. Portions of the materials used are property of Wizards of the Coast LLC. ©Wizards of the Coast LLC.\nTurnZero was created under Riot Games' \"Legal Jibber Jabber\" policy using assets owned by Riot Games. Riot Games does not endorse or sponsor this project.\nWhy TurnZero?    TurnZero HQ App\nTrack your Commander games\nLife counter\nDeck builder\nPlaygroup stats\nCard scanner\nDownload on the App Store       Get it on Google Play\nOr start free in your browser →\nTurnZero HQ store\nThe Commander Cube\nA deck-box-sized case for the rest of the table: 30 spring-loaded +1/+1 dials, up to 72\nstatus tokens, and your dice, all behind a magnetic lid.\nBuy on Etsy\nSee how it works →\nAdvertisement\nAll articles\nSearch 1799 articles /    Game Magic      Magic\nPokémon\nStar Wars: Unlimited\nYu-Gi-Oh!\nOne Piece\nRiftbound\nLorcana\nLearn to Play     Beginner Series\nMTG Formats\nCommander Glossary\nGuides & Tips\nDraft & Limited\nCommander     Play Better Commander\nCommander Guides\nDeck Archetypes     Spellslinger\nGraveyard recursion\nMill\nVoltron\nGo wide\nAristocrats\nEnchantress\nArtifacts\nSee all →\nColor Combinations\nCards & Decks     Card Spotlights\nRoundups\nTools & Data\nSets & Collecting     Set Guides\nSecret Lair\nBuying & Selling\nLore & Story\nTurnZero HQ     How to Use TurnZero HQ\nApp Comparisons\n